      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>_MINISTERIAL. On Sunday the Rev. D. Macbeth Paterson, who for the last two-and-a-half years has served as pastor of the Earlestown Baptist Church, entered upon his ministry in connection with the Wellington-road Baptist Church, Tcdmorclen. While travelling the other day, I accidentally met one of the deacons associated with Mr. Paterson's new charge, and he assured me that, by his earnest sermons on the occasion now referred to, the rev, gentleman abundantly confirmed the very favourable impression he had previously created, and the congregation generally considered the choice justified. Some interested readers may possibly recollect that the Rev. H. Ogden of Golborne-street Baptist Church, is a native of Todmorden, and was brought up at the very church to which Mr. Paterson has now gone. Noncon- formists in general and Baptists in particular are exceedingly strong and influential in that district. FOOTBALL ENTHUSIASTS. Taking a trip to Halifax on Saturday, I found the place all agog with excitement on account of the Northern Union final. Huddersfield was very well represented, and a fair number of eager adherents of the Wigan team came along to see their pets defeated. Gutter merchants in the neighbourhood of the railway station did a roaring trade in club favours. Gangs of young fellows out for the day made sport for the multitude after the usual manner. I saw one set of a dozen or so marching along in single file wearing white hats identical in pattern, and a similar party carrying bells, which they rang sharply one after another in quick succession. One red-headed man, with a " tile" which any comic singer might well have coveted, rode on the front of a big conveyance bear- ' ing on his knees a huge tin replica of the challenge cup decorated with coloured ribbons. The enterpris- ing management of a local Zoo took advantage of the crowded state of the town to send out a sandwich elephant, the fine beast carrying a couple of adver- tisement boards in the approved fashion. From this and another novel method of attracting attention I inferred that the common or garden sindwichman is not so much in demand as he was. I noticed a clean-shaven young man of decidedly refined appearance wearing a scholar's gown and' mortar board" in the main thoroughfare. He was chatting with a friend, and as I passed I turned to look at him again. On the back of the gown was a placard on which were printed the words : "Some go in for education, others give their leisure to pleasures, but all drink --------'s tea." A CHANGE, Before returning from Halifax, I dropped in on an old friend for a chat, and a little girl raember of the family, seven years of age, proudly produced her school composition book for inspection. It is her teachers's practice to let the children write short essays of their own, and if the one in which I revelled for a while on Saturday be a fair specimen, one need hardly wonder if " Uncle Caleb's " stock of" bowlers" should prove practically inexhaustible. Of course, I did not copy from the youngster's bm k, but one titbit so tickled my fancy that I can easily reproduce it from memory. Here it is —"I: like this part c f the year that is coming best of any, because we go off for a holiday, sometimes to Blackpool and to many other places. We nearly always go somewhere. If we do not, we have something given us that jest does as well. Last year but one we went to the seaside, but last summer we had measles instead " GROSS IGNORANCE OR CAREt ESSNESS. I was glad cf the medical officer's timely warning respecting measles. The recklessness or the fatalism of some parents when scarlatina is rife is bad enough; but when it comes to measles the foolishness of certain mothers positively borders on criminality. I have it on unquestionable authority that one of these whom I happen to know personally had a child down with that really dangerous malady, and, having a numerous family, put some of the healthy little ones in bed along with the sufferer with the remark that as long as they were bound to have it sooner or later they might as well " get it all over at once ! " There is sadly too much of that sort of thing even in this twentieth century. Yet we hear odd individuals ask- ing what we want with a health week ! FRESH AIR. " Eleven o'clock, gentlemen," murmurs the publican when intimating to the occupants of the bar parlour or the best room that closing time has arrived; then, passing on, he shouts at the taproom door, "Fresh cur, chaps:' - So, in effect, said Dr. Coote Hibbert. His most useful contribution to last week's oratorical flood was an eloquent and telling plea for fresh air, and I trust he will make the most of every opportunity to "rob it in." The "slumming expedition" revealed the need of each persistent effort —or, rather, emphasised it, for the necessity has all along been too apparent. I am especially familiar with industrial conditions in East Lancashire and West Yorkshire, and I used to notice when in those parts the factory inspector insisted on additional ventilation being provided in a weaving shed, the operatives themselves systematically stuffed up the inlets because they feared the draught. Moreover, I once called to see a Union official, and found the atmosphere of his office unbearably stuffy. For his own sake I ventured to remark that the air was so vitiated as to be poisonous upon which he snapped "Ali, you're one of those fresh air fiends, are you? Let me tell you this : I'd rather ran the risk of being poisoned than sit in a draught." "As you please," said I, as I hastened into the open street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <p>"LAY LOW AND SAY NUFFIN." Last week's local slummers noted that, whatever might be the explanation, dense black smoke pouring from a works chimney was at orce appreciably reduced in volume when the councillors passod that way. This fact, and the incidental reference I have made to the factory instector, reminds me of a story In a district in which I formerly worked, that functionary was a pretty familiar figure. Time- cribbing and the employment of young persons daring illegal hours were common offences, and the official did his best with a view to suppression, but found detection exceedingly difficult. The fact was that local manufacturers and other persistent offenders had an arrangement whereby due notice of the dreaded one's approach was ensured. Some railwayman would inform the nearest interested person of the factory inspector's arrival, and it would forthwith be 'phoned over the locality. On one of these occasions, a mill manager topped a youngster into an empty tub, and, telling him to remain perfectly quiet as the inspector was close at hand, replaced the lid. The unwelcome visitor happened to pass that way, and hearing a shuffling of feet, therein, had the curiosity to glance into the barrel. " What are you doing there, my boy ?" he asked ; and the rude and unintentionally illuminative answer he received was: "Shut up, the gurt boil. Doesn't ta know iii' inspector's on th' job ?"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <p>to an article which I read some months ago—and in the " Jewish Vierld," tco, of all papers !—students have for some time been trying to reduce to some- thing like the allotted span the great ages to which the Jewish patriarchs are recorded in the Bible as having lived. It is surmised that in the earliest times the moon was used for reckoning time. Counting by months, however, must have grown cumbersome, and a longer division became absolutely necessary. There is a theory that the first years were composed of five months of thirty days each, the limit of five being derived from the fingers of one hand. It is also con- tended that before the five-months year there was a one-month year; in other words, that a month the period of a moon cycle, was termed a year. An endeavour has been made to prove this by working out the great ages of the first men in the Bible. Thus Adam's 930 years of life, calculated at '291 days (the period of a " lunation ") a year, wcrks out at 751- years, which must strike many people as a reasonable period of existence. By this reckon- ing, Methuselah loses his famous record, for his 909 reputed years are whittled down to 781. Excuse for this arrangement (as a writer in the "Daily Telegraph" commented at the time our Jewish Con- temporary sought t) strip poor Methuselah of his patriarchal honours) is found in the Psalmist's limit of life to three score and ten years, and it is maintained that between the times of Noah and David</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>TH.E TITANIC INQUIRY.</head>
        <p>TH.E TITANIC INQUIRY.</p>
        <p>On Monday the Titanic Wreck Commis- sion spent five hours on the 'White Star liner Olympic, sister ship to the lost Titanic. A thorough inspection NN as made, observa- tions taken from the bridge, and there was a demonstration of launching lifeboats. The inquiry, presided over by Lord Mersey, was</p>
        <p>resumed on Tuesday. Our illustration shows, reading from left to right, Mr. Saunderson, Managing Director of the White Star Line, the first officer of the Olympic, and Captain Haddock of the Olympic, going to the inquiry,</p>
