      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>EMIGRATION.</head>
        <p>EMIGRATION.</p>
        <p>On the very day before I read of the arrival in Canada of the latest batch of local emigrants, I bad seen seven friends of my own. set sail for the same continent, their intention being to settle in Moose Jaw. They went from Liverpool ; and on the previous Monday I had had on a farewell visit from a cousin who on the Thursday evening left London for Australia. I know others who are t ) follow shortly. These are a few of many instances of the kind that have lately come under my own observation ; four other acquaint- ances hav:ng left me not long ago to try their luck in New Zealand, and another baying gone to South Africa with the intention of settling in that country. I know of three or four Yorkshire and Lancashire towns from which there has latterly been quite an exodus of emigrants for the new world. One whom last year I saw off for South Dacota soon left for Moose Jaw, and, as noted in last Saturday's" Examiner," the rush of American settlers to Canada is now heavier than ever. Yet the one to whom I have just now alluded as having chosen Australia did so after consulting a relative who had , considerable experience of Canada, being warned that the prospect of success there was not better than at home. OLD AS METHUSELAH."</p>
        <p>This Expression, const-a-n—tly on our lips, was used by General Booth during his afternorn meeting at the Hippodrome here. How old was Methuselah? Here is a reminder of the record :—Mrs. Tubbs: "But you can't expect us to believe tbat Methuselah could have lived to the age of 069 years ? " The Curate (cornered, and taking refuge in mild humour) : "Oh, I don't know. There were no motor-cars in those days.' But alas, nothing is sacred notvadlys, for according</p>
        <p>no such extraordinary change could have taken tilace as to reduce the life of man by eleven-twelfths. The next stage in the marking of time is supposed to have been the discovery of the equinoxes in spring ard autumn, when day and night were exactly of the same length. This would give five months of thirty days each. On this basis of a year of 150 days, Abraham's 175 years work out at 72 and Isaac's 180 at 74. The twelve months' year began with the Egyptians, who saw that a complete period was made up of the two " years," in one of which the days were longer than the nights, and in the other longer than the days. Perhaps sgme a 113— certainly including the worthy head of the Salvation Army—are too orthodox or jealous of any tampering with the Scriptures to receive this or ti have patience with such methods. Anyhow, we shall go on saying" as old as Methuselah " all the same.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>NERVES AND NEURALGIA.</head>
        <p>NERVES AND NEURALGIA.</p>
        <p>Go to any chemist and obtain one ounce of Alves°, one ounce of Glycerine, and half an ounce of Tincture of Gentian. Pour these into a six-ounce bottle and fill up with water. if dose is a teaspoonful three ior four times daily, n water f preferred. In severe cases take a dose hourly until relieved. This very simple mixture will cure the worst possible attack of terves, neuralgia, sleeplessness, headache, and similar ailments arising from a run- down state of health, will entirely remove "that tired feeling," and promote digestion and good spirits</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May, 11 1 912</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>MINIMUM WAGE OF TWO GUINEAS. MR. TOM MANN AND THE INDUSTRIAL REVOLUTION.</head>
        <p>MINIMUM WAGE OF TWO GUINEAS. MR. TOM MANN AND THE INDUSTRIAL REVOLUTION.</p>
        <p>No Confidence in Parliamentary Action.</p>
        <p>Mr. Tom Mann addressed an open-air meeting in Bewsey-road on Saturday afternoon, under the auspices of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council. Recent evebts have caused the well-known labour organiser to loom so prominently in the public eye that a great deal of interest was taken in his visit and there were memhers of all political parties in the large crowd which assembled. Listening to Mr. Mann, one readily understood the influence which he exerts over large bodies of workers. His oratory, homely and blunt as it is, has an impetus of fervour and enthusiasm which quickly communicates itself to his hearers and at the close of his address Mr. Mann had no difficulty in inducing the audience to cheer lustily for the success of the Labour movement. He has also a strong sense of the dramatic, and there was something irresistibly amusing about the way in which he portrayed the recent' happenings on the Olympic. "I am looking forward with great pleasure to being inside Strangeways in a day or two" was Mr. MANS'S opening remark. " I had the finest bit of exercise for about ten days, whilst I aav there waiting for the Police Court proceedings, that I have had for a long time. (Laughter.) If I have no worse time during the fifteen years that I uneerstand I am to get—(laughter) —1 shall be all right. lem not worrying abcut that— not one ha'porth." The reason for holding that demonstrAton, coxtinued Mr. Mann, was that they were g eatly concerned about industrial organization. Why ? Not for the sake of being able to declare that 09 pr cent, of the workers were in definite trade organizations, but because it would give them the leverage to get the poor out of the horrible poverty in which they were living, so that their children could have all the essentials of life in abundance. (Applause.) The amount which unskilled labourers were receiving-18s. to LI ls' a week—was cnly sufficient to traduce a dog's standard of life, It was absolutely impossible for a man to maintain a family under tolerable conditions on any- thing less than 355. to £2 a week. " I have definite], declared," said Mr. Alarm," in favour of</p>
        <p>A BirsllkiTJM OF TWO GUINEAS A WEEK, and I mean it too. (Applause.) You will say, 01 there's no earthly chance.' Isn't there ? Well, I'n amongst those who really believe there is. What's more, so far as a man can say he knows anything, 1 tee*, there is. I know it is obtainable by effective ihdustrial solidarity. What does that mean ? It means lining up. and agreeing as to the machinery we will use to enable ourselves to change the conditions. lam not here to talk of anti-Parliamentarianism to-day, but I am here to talk in favour of industrial organisation as the one agency upon which you must centre with a view to getting out of the social hole you are in." The main reason why the workers had not concentrated attention upon industrial organisation was because they had been encouraged to believe chiefly in Parliamentary action. He told them in the plainest of language that he did not believe one ha'porth in Parliamentary action. Warrington there must be quite 20,000 workers eligible for organisation, and yet only 6,000 had organised themselves. That showed that men had no belief at pre3ent in the eflicaoy of industrial organi- zation to lift them cut of poverty. Parliament had never been an institttion that bad worked in the interests of the workers—not for one week during the whole GOO years of its existence had it been honestly devoted to the workers' cause. It had existed primarily to enable tin ruling class to subjugate the working class and the only sign of hope was that there were men sufficiently reckless to move in spite of Parliament. " You silly fatheads " continued Mr. Mann "have not realized what sacrifices yrur fathers made to give you the opportunity of organizing, and the capitalists are trying to wean you from it." The capitalists were now encouraging systematic emigration for the express purpose of enabling them to continue systematic robbery when the workers got out there— and they were doing it. He had spent eight sears and a half in Australia and knew the conditions that obtained there. The standard was shamefully low. They might talk of philosophy, religion and politics, but the bed rock requirement of the workers was more food and better food, more clothes and better clothes, more leisure and a better chance of healthy enjoyment. They could produce enough and to spare, without over- working anybody. (Applause.) So long as they allowed the capitalists to retain their power of deciding what conditions of remuneration and hours should prevail, industry would continue to be run in the interests of the proflt-receiving class and there would be no change for the better.</p>
        <p>PARLIAMENT BELONGED TO THE PLUTOCRACY and to the dominant class. The reason why the wealthy exercised such Dower was because they owned and con- trolled the industrial establishments where wealth was produced. Let the workers obtain that control, and everything else would follow. Whoever controlled wealth production, held the key of the situation. They would then be able to build an industrial Parliament to control industry on a co-operative basis, guarantee-, ing work to everybody and bringing in labour-saving appliances as rapidly as they could be invented, never throwing anybody out of employment but reducing the workers' hours, commensurate with the introduction of labour saving devices. That could be done this year if they bad the wisdcm to do it. The recent events on the Olympic were a little indication of what solidarity on a small scale could achieve. What the Olympic seamen had done on a small scale, it was for the work. rug classes to do collectively and then they would have the capitalists by the horns " Don't wait for Parlia- ment and politicans " advised Mr. Mann. " Hell is in preparation for them. (Laughter.) We must save our- selves, or there is no salvation for us. The year 1912 will witness an enormous change for the better. How dare I say that ? Because I know what is going on in men's minds, and it warrants my declaring that the now Gospel of industrial solidarity has been learnt. ith every industry throughout the county linked together, we shall be ready for common action with our fellow workers in other countries. " (Arptause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>Mr. Mann Sent to Gaol.</head>
        <p>Mr. Mann Sent to Gaol.</p>
        <p>Considerable publio interest was displayed in the trial before Mr. Justice Bankes at Manche.ter Assizes, on Thursday, of Tom Mann, the well-known Socialist and trade union organizer, who bad been indicted for unlawfully uttering, circulating and publishing an article in 'The Syndicalist," with intent to seduce to mutinous conduct persons serving in His Majesty's forces on land.</p>
        <p>It was alleged for the prosecution that the prisoner had identified himself with an open letter, published in "The Syndicalist," callinf, upon soldiers not to fire on strikers if ordered to do so by their officers. It was contended that the intention of the letter was to seduce soldiers from Attic duty and to incite them to mutiny. The prisoner conducted his own defence. He sub- mitted that the letter in the "Syndicalist" was entirely within the law. All that it did was to ask the soldiers not to murder.</p>
        <p>"4 aii-r-i;OT;sking your mercy in any respect," added the prisoner, "but I am claiming the right, as a man, as a citizen, as a native of this country, and as a workman, to organise for the removal of the worst evils which afflict this country." Prisoner was found guilty and sentenced to six months' imprisonment in the second division.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>"MY MOTHER WAS A TERRIBLE SUFFERER."</head>
        <p>"MY MOTHER WAS A TERRIBLE SUFFERER."</p>
        <p>"THE FREE PRESCRIPTION CURED HER." "My mother thinks I ought to write and acknow- ledge the value of the free prescription you publish, as it is the only thing that did her any good. She has been a terrible sufferer for years. One doctor calls it Sciatica, another Neuralgia and Neuritis. The headaches were almost unbearable. The relief she obtained from the prescription was immediate, and we think she is now really cured. "Perhaps I ought to state the prescription just as I cut it from the paper. I was pad to ask our chemist for GO grains of Kephaldol in tablet form. He supplied it at once. Two tiblets gave instant relief, and it was not necessary for mother to take them all. Father was bad with Lumbago at the time, and the remaining tablets cured him."—Miss Jenkins, Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>CREW DYING OF HUNGER.</head>
        <p>CREW DYING OF HUNGER.</p>
        <p>Local Apprentice on Board.</p>
        <p>PARIS, Tuesday. The captain cf the Felix-Touacbe, a French boat belonging to the Compagnic de Navigation Mixte, which arrived yesterday at Marseilles, states that several hours after leaving Philippeville he perceived the English brig Birkdale. of Liverpool, which signalled to him "No more provisions ; dying of hunger." The Captain hastened to the aid of the sufferers, and found that the brig, with a crew of six men and the captain, bad left the Straits of Gibraltar 52 days before and that, kept back by the bad weather, they bad run abort of provisions, and the crew were starving. Before continuing the Captain of the Felix-Touache left them provisions for several days. The news of the Birkdale's plight aroused some excitement in Penketb, as Mr. Leonard Houghton, youngest son of Mrs Honghton, Hall Nook, is on board as apprentice. He left home over a year ago to join the sailing ship on her long voyage. His relatives are naturally still anxious to learn how he is after such unusual privations.</p>
        <p>Hardham Priory, a thirteenth century house, near Pu'thorough, Sussex. was destroyed by fire on Tuesday morning. The inmst,s escaped in their night attire.</p>
        <p>PRACTICAL M POULTRY FARMING • Monio giZIKIMMIEuri</p>
        <p>[BY REDCAP].</p>
        <p>;m the " Feathered World."</p>
        <p>Anconaiare one of our best egg-producing varieties. They are small eaters, good foragers and quick growers ; they are a non-sitting variety. Anconas are a very handsome looking fowl and although they are not exactly black in colour, they are a most suit- able breed for town runs, as they do not easily show the dirt. They are good layers, laying a large white- shelled egg.</p>
        <p>/- ly</p>
        <p>~ • -77 * . , . - = / • - SINGLE-COMBED ANCONA9 Fro</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <p>Telephone-1214 Royal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>AS AN EXHIBITION 818%</head>
        <p>AS AN EXHIBITION 818%</p>
        <p>Anconaa at the present time are not very popular as an exhibition fowl, so that a good chance is offered here for the novice. Prices for good class stock do not rule high, therefore they are well within the reach )f the r, orkingman fancier. Besides the single- comb -variety we have the rosecomb. At one time this was a very popular fowl. But fowls, like many other things unless they are well backed up by a few gocd men, soon lose their popularity. This is the case with the Ancona especially the Rosecomb. As I write my mind is carried back a few years in remembrance of a few good fanciers, who, when they died, it seems that the Rose-combed Ancona died with them. How- ever, we must not be too pessimistic. I belive that the good qualities of the Ancona fowl will even yet bt ing them back into the forefront as a popular breed.</p>
        <p>POULTRY FOR EXHIBITION. (continued). As promised last week, I will give some notes further still on exhibition poultry. We had got as far as selection, value, and specialists' clubs. The next point for consideration is</p>
        <p>BREEDING.</p>
        <p>Unlike utility stock, methods of in-breeding have to be resorted to in the breeding of exhibition fowls. This method must not be confounded with redigree breeding, because even this is essential in the produc- tion of first-class utility stock. And while pedigree breeding is more or less a process of inbreeding it is not so harmful to the general vitality of the stock as is close inbreeding. The fancier's watchword should always be : "Like begets like" ; and even if the actual numbers of healthy progeny are not great, the method must be rigidly adhered to, because fixity of type and colour can come by no other meats. It is useless to select and mate together breeding stock of so many different strains. Sometimes a novice— having got a few good hens—will go to 'a show and purchase a winning male Mid of the same breed, and mate Lim to the hens, in the hope of getting something good. But alas ! when the chicks arrive he then realizes his rcistake. You can- not possibly produce show fowls by this careless method of mating. You must start with a good strain and stick to it; and when an introduction of new blood is essential this must be introduced with great care, and even then it is always better to introduce a bird of distant relationship to the original strain. But to return to the first words on inbreeding. It means the mating together of closely related birds. For instance ; supposing you purchase an exhibitiou pen of fowls, the season following you should mate the female progeny from the original pen back to male of the first pen, and likewise the females of the original pen should be mated with a ccckerel from the first pen. By this method you are able to go on for quite a few seasons without any real danger to the vitality 'of the stock. When you are satisfied that a change of blood is necessary, always go back to the fountain bead, I go to the fancier who holds the original strain. Some fowls are better known by the'r breeders' names than sometimes even by the name of the breed. For instance, Cook's and Orpingtons must alway s go together, just as Firth's and Game, or Proctor and Cochins are insepat able. The important point is,'never introduce " alien ' blood because you happen to be struck with certain good appearances.</p>
        <p>BLOOD WILL TELL. I remember once paying a visit to a well known breeder of Blue Andalusians. In that year he bad taken all before him in the show pen, and as the following season progressed I thought I would pay him a visit and see how be had mated his pens. I wanted to see how these flyers were produced. Very well. On my arrival I asked my friend to show me the Dairy and Palace winning pullets. He did so, and to my astonishment (then) I found he had mated these valuable show birds to a cockerel that I would not give half-a-crown for. I remarked to my host as to the capabilities of the head of the harem ' and he replied : "I would not take £2O for that bird to-day," and, continuing, he said. "You know, my boy, blood will tell." And so, you FOB it is not always the biggest winner that proves the best breeder. Providing the stock are related, and that they are line bred exhibition birds, appearances do not always count. In conclusion then : stick to one strain, and by judicious in-breeding you are bound to build up a good exhibition stock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>NUMBER IN A PEN</head>
        <p>NUMBER IN A PEN</p>
        <p>Here again you -1-1.-ITer-o--v;rs; your operations from that of breeding utility stock. In breeding for exhibi- tion specimens, what you must aim at is quality' and not' quantity.' Therefore it is not wise to have more than four in a pen. Three females to one male. As a matter of fact I know some fanciers who coa'e only one hen with one cockerel. Of course these are of the heavy variety. In the case of the light non- sitting varieties you cculd mate up more than these per pen. But even then it is not wise to exceed four in number.</p>
        <p>DOUBLE MATING. This means running two separate pens, one for the production of show pullets and the other for the pro- duction of show cockerels. These pens are known as cock-breeding pens and pullet-breeding pens. Now this system does not apply to every breed, because in some breeds you are able to get good specimens of both sexes from one mating. For instance, double mating is not necessary with Minorcas, Black and White Leghorns, Black Orpingtons, White Orpingtons and the like. Double mating generally applies to those varieties where feather colour and markings pre- dominate as an essential towards prize winning. In all laced and penciled varieties double mating is essential. In some r.f the self-coloured varieties this system is adopted, especially in tne large combed varieti, a such as Leghorn, and Minorca., but when a proper selection is made it is not absrilutely necessary to run two pens. For instance, supposing you are mating a pen of Black Minorcas with the idea of pro ducing good specimens of both sexes. This could be done by mating say two pullet-breeding hens and cockerel breeding hens with a good exhibition cockerel, because the chief aim is for head points and those hens with only a few comb servations and strong at tl e base would produce cockerels, and on the other hand those hens with large overhanging combs would produce pullets. This system therefore, obviates the need for running two separate pens. Of course those in a large way and with plenty of accommodation would do well to run separate pens, because when scientifically mated much better results accrue as a result.</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
