      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>CRICKET NOTES.</head>
        <p>CRICKET NOTES.</p>
        <p>Saturday was anything but a suitable day for cricket and rain fell so persistently during the morning that it was at one :time dcubtful whetl er any matches would be brought off. However, it takes a great deal to daunt the wielders of the willow and a slight improvement in the outlook was sufficient to induce several teams to carry out their fixtures. Warrington first were not of that Lumber, their match with Ashton- under-Lynn being cancelled. The second eleven made the journey to Ashton, but rain prevented the match being fought to a finish. The home contingent declared their innings at 124 for two wickets and Warrington responded with 52 fcr one wicket, W. V. Turton carrying out his bat with 15 t 3 his credit, while J. Bradburn was bowled for 17.</p>
        <p>The Warrington and District League tourney made a start on Saturday when two matct es were decided. Whitecross opened the season in promising style with a victory at the expense of Wilderspo(l. The Brewery men had the first turn at the wicket, and were all dismissed for 53, the only players to :reach double figures beingrlarke and Lloyd with 11 each. White- cross compiled CO for the loss of eight wickets, the outstanding features of their innings being smart displays by H Berry and Lavell for 20 and 22 respectively.</p>
        <p>There was a keen encounter between St. He lens Cable Company and Cairo-street Unitarians, the firat- named triumphing by 6 runs. The Unitarians would Inv° made a poor show had it not been for D. Plinston, who contributed a fine not out innings of 31. Baker (11) was the top scorer for the victors.</p>
