      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION LEAGUE FINAL.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION LEAGUE FINAL.</p>
        <p>The Championship of the N.C. :League was decided at Halifax on Saturday, when Huddersfield deservedly gained the honours by defeating Wigan. Any other result would have baen false to the season's form, for Huddersfield have shown marked superiority through- out the tournament. Wigan played a strong game, but the Yorkshiremen were more finished behind, though Wrigley's absence had some effect on the combination. Thera were 12,491 spectators, and the receipts totalled L's9l 9s. 3d. At the start Wigan played very robust football, and the Wigan forwards were the superior body up to the interval. Hodder-01431d early on completed a round of orthodox passing, and Rosenfeld made the defence appear very slow. It was a great try and Longstaffe capped it with a superb goal. The Fartown men were now feeling their feet, but with intense enthusiasm Bradley got off, and but for one or two collisions in the course of a sprint over half the length of the field he might have succeeded. Still, he had got the Wigan men within striking distance again, and yet there were several movements, and several chances again missed on the Wigan right wing, before Jenkins picked up a pass from Thomas, and sent Bradley over. Thomas brought the scores level with a neat goal. Thomas, and then Grey, missed goals from straight shots in the early stages of the second half. Rosenfeld made the pace once with a fine dash, and it was the speed of the Huddersfield back play that eventually told its tale, Moorhouse drawing the defence until he found a way through for Clark. The try was made behind the posts, and Grey added the goal points. Persistent endeavour marked Wigan's play, and Bradley had to be well watched. Miller, too, improved ; but, with Sharrock's drops at goal unsuccessful, it was all too belated an effort. In the last minute,P.foorhouse cleared the course for Davies, and that try, although uncon- verted by Grey, settled the issue. Result :—Hudders- field two goals three tries, (13 points), Wigan one goal one try (five points). MR. PLATT AND NEW LEAGUE SCHEMES. At the complimentary dinner given to the Hudders- field Northern Union team, Mr. J. Platt, the secretary of the Union, expressed agreement with combination leagues and developing competitions foethe second teams of clubs, lie would like, he said, to see a com- bination formed to include the clubs in Yorkshire, as well as Lancashire, with the championship played for as it was now. He would be pleased to support a proposal with this object in view that was to be made by the Huddersfield club at the next annual meeting of the League. If they had more local players intthe first teams it would benefit the junior clubs. At Oldham junior players had a hard task to get into a first team. Clubs wanted piayers ready-made and fit to win matches straight off the reel. He favoured a suegestion made by the President of the Huddersfield Club that clubs should be induced to play teams half composed of local men. As to the local qualification he would say that a man who had been playing seven years for a club, or who was born within a radius of fifteen miles of the club's home, or who was on the register of the club when the rule was made should be qualified as a local man. The League Committee. Mr. Platt added, bad been subjected to severe criticism in Lancashire with regard to the present league system. But all past experience was against a revival of the two-league system. When they tried it they lost clubs from the league.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>WARRINGTON WORKS COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKS COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>A FINE FINAL. It was generally agreed that the final tie on Saturday between lands No. 1 and Pearson and Knowles provided the best game in the history of the Works Competition. Both teams played excellent football and proved so well matched that the issue was open right to the end. The weather turned out rather wet, but there was a capital crowd of about 2,500 and plenty of enthusiasm on the Wilderspool ground. The cup and gold watches eventually went to the men from Hylands' with a score of 3 goals, 2 tries (12 points) to 3 tries (9 points). Seeing that the forgemen scored a try more than the winners, they must be counted somewhat unlucky to lose. A reliable goal-kicker would have made all the difference. :Wands' were, however, the smarter side, and deserved the honours. Their football was of rather better class than that of P. and K., and it was only the forgemen's One forward work which gave them so much of the game. Harrop and Smith were Ithe outstanding men in the Icsers' back division, the latter getting a good try on the wing, and the half back two smart tries on his own. Webb (centre) and a forward were the scorers for Itylands, while Bramhall kicked three goals. Gallop was very nippy behind the pack, and the whole team handled well. Mr. Hestford, of Broughton, was the referee, and at the close he made flattering allusions to the quality of the football shown by the junior Warringtonians. PRESENTATION OF THE CUP. After the match there was a rush to the grandstand on the reserve side, and the captain of the winning team, Bramhall, was loudly cheered on receiving the cup from Mr. Thomas Locker, president of the War- rington Club. The winners were also the recipients of handsome gold watches. The runners-up were presented with oxydised watches. The President was thanked for his services on the motion of Mr. Prank Turner, which was seconded by Mr. W. Peter Ryland.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>ASSOCIATION. ALTRINCHAM AND DISTRICT MEDAL COMPETITION. _</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION. ALTRINCHAM AND DISTRICT MEDAL COMPETITION. _</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD RF:S. V. LATCHFORD RECS. After two drawn games and playing of extra time in the re-played tie, Latchford Recs achieved a well deserved victory in this co npetition on Saturday by two goals to one. The mach was decided oa the ground of the Latchford Club in Thelwall-lane. The It3cs. defending the Thelwall-lane goal, straightaway from the kick-off invaded the Reserves territory where Humphries cleared his linco and set his forwards going, and Lockhart getting pc-session scorad a good goal three minut2s from the start. The Recs again attacked bit met with a stubborn defence in Pilling, Johnson and llumphrit -, the latter keeping goal in fine style. Although the Re-' played sith only tea men for twenty minutes and did the greater amount of attacking they could no. score. At this point Fleming made his appearance. Davies left his position at hr k 1 and went to help the forwards and it was owing to this move that Davies himself roored a goal just bsfore half-time. Humphries having no chance to save. Resuming, both sets of players continued to play in determined fashion. Graivally, the Re-erves began to have more of the game, and gave the Rae's defenc3 an anxious time. A fine paesing run b3twc3n Grcan and Townley ended in the latter sending in the tst shot of the game, and Horrocks made an equally good save. After this, the Rea increased their energy and though Fleming WA4 not able to play up to his usual standard, he made some clever passes to Woodhead, who made some good runs on the right. Starkey was given march- ing orders, end immediately after the whistle blew the stipulated time, finding the scores level. An extra twenty minutes was played. The Reserves' defence mulled a long shot from J Ditchfield, vvbi h eventually found the net. During the latter portion of the game, the Reserves came near to equalising again, but failed at the critical moment, and the game ended with the Rees deserving winners by two goals to one. The gate receipts were in aid of the Titanic fund.</p>
      </div>
