        <head>t FEEDING SWEET PEAS.</head>
        <p>t FEEDING SWEET PEAS.</p>
        <p>The tendency is undoubtedly to commence this too i early in the season. If the soil s thoroughly worked to commence with, and rotten natural manure is incor- porated at the same time, with some artificial fertiliser two or three weeks in advance of sowing cr planting, there should be an abundant supply of food in the ground to last the plants until the buds are well advanced, and until that stage is reached special feeding should not be attempted. The practice of pouring strong liquid manure on the soil before the roots have become much more than comfortably established is sure ' to lead to sappy growth if the plants have the power to imbibe the food, and if they have not, the probabilities are that it will sour the ground and thus ruin the roots. When the plants are in full vigour, by all means feed if it is necessary, but let the manure tub alone until it is quite certain that the supply of food in the soil is not sufficient to meet the proper requirements of the plants. —" The Garden."</p>
        <p>Home Rule for Ireland HOME RULE. has received the assent of the Commons. On Thursday, after a finely sustained six days debate on the second reading, the Tory amendment to reject the Home Rule Bill was defeated by the great majority of 101 in a division in which no less than 640 members of the House took part. There was an almost record muster for the occasion, and the great triumph of the Government and PREMIER was the occasion for loud and prolonged cheering such as rarely wakes the echoes of the historic build- ing. The solidarity of what is called "the coalition" was never more apparent, and there need be no further repetition of the absurd allegation that the Government had no mandate from the country to introduce Home Rule. The absence of that mandate must inevitably have been disclosed by the voting, whereas we find Liberals, Nationalists, and Labour men practically solid for the great measure which has been in the forefront of Progressive policy at each election since 1906. There has been no revolt, and no secessions beyond those of Sir CLIFFORD CORY and Sir GEORGE KEMP, the former of whom voted against the Govern- ment. Times have changed, and the feeling in regard to Home Rule has changed with the times—a fact quite obvious before Thursday's great victory. Even Tories no longer talk of the "dismemberment of the Empire" as if they believed the phrase stood for anything which they must resist to the end. There are many members sitting on the Conservative benches who would be eager enough to pass a Home Rule Bill under some other name if their own party were in power.</p>
        <p>The majority of 101 recorded for the Bill contrasts not only with the defeat of 1886, but with the precarious victory of 1893. And beyond it stands to-day no impassable House of Lords. The Bill goes into Com- mittee, where no doubt much obstructive rhetoric will be repeated, and where we hope also that valuable improvements may be made; but it goes now with none of the tragic uncertainty of 1893. The worst the House of Commons can do is to delay the !passage of the measure for two years, and there are plenty of indications that the chastened Upper Chamber may adopt a much more amenable attitude. They had grown somewhat tired of kicking against the pricks and we are inclined to think that when the Bill reaches them amended, revised, and blessed by the Commons, there are dis- tinct possibilities of its passing even ihe hereditary enemy without serious injury.</p>
        <p>Under the heading "A THE PORPOISE AND Country Diary " in THE MAN wiTHME yesterday's "Ma n- GuN. chester Guardian," a</p>
        <p>correspondent makes some interesting observations on the presence of a salmon and a porpoise in the Mersey at Warrington within a week or so of each other. Incidentally, the Writer raises the question of the right to shoot or otherwise kill such uncommon visitors to the river shores. The "barbarous impulse "is, unfor- tunately, always manifested, and more than one venturesome denizen of the deep seas has been received with bullets instead of open arms on swimming into the confines of our ancient borough. Did not a fine seal experi- ence a similar fate a year or two ago? The correspondent in question writes as follows, under date, May Bth: No one will blame the man who, a short time ago, caught a large salmon which was left by the receding tide on the shore of the Mersey at Warrington. But a week ago yesterday in unfortunate porpoise was shot in the river near the same place, and those of us who ale interested in wild life may well ask, Why was it sh•A ? It was first seen in the river on the morning of April 30th, and "during the day a large number of people went to see the interesting sight." The graceful play of the porpoi-e in the water is not only an interesting but a very beautiful sight,and it is not surprising that many inhabitants of Warrington should wish to see it; amongst them, I am sure, were very many who would rather see this small relative of the wale enjoying life than witness its massacre. There was nothing extraordinary about it ; it was a typical fomfoot porpoise. It was not valuable for food, nor indeed had it commercial value. The real interest of the occurrence centres in the fact that a cetacean can still venture to ascend a polluted stream like the Mersey ; that it was not required for scientific purposes is proved by the wise decision of the authorities at the Warrington Museum in refus- ing to purchase its corpse. Are we never going to rise above the barbarous impulse to slay every interesting stranger which is foolish enough to visit us? One would like to know if it is legal to shoot on the river, and how far the scene of the murder was from a public road. One of the recognised objections to bird shooting in the Mersey was the danger to people in passing boats; why, •then, may anyone fire at a mammal and not at a bird?</p>
        <p>When the greater THE FUTURE OF part of Lymm and LYMM. Oughtrington passed _</p>
        <p>into the hands of Messrs. Lever Bros., everybody recognized that the ensuing developments must be fraught with great possibilities to the future of the district. The Urban Council has taken a sensible view of its responsibilities by availing itself of Sir William Lever's offer to sell the waterworks, the far-famed lake, and fourteen acres of land for the sum of £20,250. All progressive authorities are convinced that the community should itself be the owner of such elementary necessities of life as the water supply, and the Lymm Council has for some years been waiting for an opportunity to acquire the undertaking on reasonable terms. To a charming residential place like Lymm it was almost equally important that the Council should secure control of the lake and its surroundings in order that the amenities of the place might be. preserved for the inhabitants for all time. The action of the Council in deciding upon the purchase seems to us to be fully justified but we must confess to some astonishment that the ratepayers who were called to the public meeting on Monday were not allowed to express, by vote, their opinion for or against the proposal. There is nothing more galling to the average ratepayer than to feel that he is a nonentity—that every- thing has been "cut and dried " in advanc3 —and it would have been more in accord with sound public policy for the Council to have risked even an adverse verdict by allowing those who took the trouble to attend the meeting to have a real voice in a question of such vital importance to all the inhabitants.</p>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS. Where was the fire-eating Sir Edward Carson during the important division on Home Rule on Thursday? "Ulster will fight "—but not without its champion. * * * The coal strike is still being used as an excuse for the non-delivery of Howley Bridge. If we had the bridge,'the founda- tions are not ready! It is time a better scapegoat was found.. .</p>
        <p>* - Out of a population of 836 at Orford, Suffolk, the ages of 24 of the inhabitants aggregate 2,ooo—an average of 84 years, and there are 76 inhabitants over 70. We wonder if Orford, Warrington, can approach such a record in public health and longevity!</p>
        <p>- - The other day a local Councillor boggled over the derivation of" The law is a hass." On Tuesday Councillor Jenkins gave "the Old Book " as the source of the equally familiar and often misquoted saying :" Clean- liness is indeed next to Godliness." He was promptly taken to task by Alderman Tinnion and aroused much mirth by a lawyer-like quibble that he said an old book," but the evidence of many hearers is all against him. Either way the author-lawyer-Councillor was wrong. It was John Wesley who was responsible for thb- undying phrase which he created in a sermon " On Dress."</p>
        <p>0 * Questioned about the Marconi operators, Mr. Sammis, the chief engineer of the American Marconi Company, said at the Titanic inquiry that the operators were paid £4 a month aboard English ships and £8 on American ships.</p>
        <p>" The Americans," we read, "gasped at £4, and Mr. Sarnmis hurriedly explained that a month buys as much in England as .A.:B here." Without discussing the adequacy of either amount, we may ask Tariff Reformers to note this complete destruction of their pre- tence that American workers are so much better off under Protection.</p>
        <p>* * Evelyn-street schools are to have an open- air swimming bath (as well as douche baths) which will be available for public use after school hours. This is a praiseworthy innovation, and if extended to other parts of the town as new schools spring up, should afford Warrington exceptional, valuable, and extremely necessary bathing facilities. -On these lines education and public health may well run together. Expense! Blow the expense this time Some things are above pounds, shillings, and pence, and this is one of them. The boon to future generations will be incalculable.</p>
        <p>* * * HEALTH POINTS.</p>
        <p>Cleanliness is part of Godliness. The frying pan is the friend of woman, but the enemy of man.</p>
        <p>The nose is the best air-filter. Why not use it? A carpet-beater is an abomination. The possession of health makes up for the absence of wealth.</p>
        <p>Do not blame the window-breaking suffragette. She only wants to make air common property. Invisible foes are dangerous; mind the microbes.</p>
        <p>The Mayor decided on Tuesday that amendment can be put to a meeting ey if the proposer and seconder wish to Nvl.t draw it after having raised a discussi! He thus established a precedent for win( as Councillor Roberts pointed out, there vi the authority of the House of Comuln procedure. Several members of the Coun objected, but we think the balance of reas is on the side of the Mayor. An amendMe or resolution once introduced becomes t property of the meeting, and if the major] wish to record a vote upon it the chairra is right in affording facilities, whatever M. be the attitude of the original proposer a seconder. Standing orders are mute 111) the point!</p>
        <p>* * * In many quarters Poor Law administrati is regarded—perhaps not without sol justice—as essehtially harsh and soulle but occasionally incidents happen which te to display the work of Guardians of the Pc in a much more favourable light. This ye' for instance, the Warrington Board Guardians has been gratified io receive ti unsought tributes to the methods emplog locally, from recipients of assistance. 0, letter read "Kindly permit me to tem to yourselves my humble thanks and aPPr ciation for the courteous and kind consider tion I have received from you at all tiff, during the period it has been necessarY 3 me to apply for fresh relief. I have alwa been dealt with in the most gentleman manner." Another testimony ran: " All' me to thank you all for the splendid trq ment my little girl (in one of the Vtil institutions) is receiving."</p>
        <p>* Alderman Bolton has reached his " score years and ten." He was 69- this sve,' and few men of his age can boast so hg' hearted and elastic a temperament. addition to being the father of the To: Council, he is one of its most criticif members as chairman of that frequea delinquent body, the Town Hall, Parks a Baths Committee. Alderman Bennett Tuesday said that Alderman Bolton "I 1 had any pretension to taste, except inane&amp; old masters,and orchids. The orchid seemed suit him to a nicety because it was a ve exceptional kind of flower." At which t Council laughed heartily, and so 4 Alderman Bolton. He is very pachyderral ous and usually good-tempered. Ma happy returns to him! *</p>
        <p>The late Bishop of Truro was a Ch. Socialist, and in this connexion he son times told an anecdote. "I had," he 0; said, "called on a rich old merchant ifl North to ask him for a subscription. At n he was somewhat grumpy. 'Come,' he sa 'they call you a Socialist; what do you nie by Socialism?' My dear sir,' I replied, depends what Socialism you mean, Politi' Socialism or Christian Socialism, for ther,re a great difference between the two. ". Political Socialist says "What is yours mine " ; but the Christian Socialist " What is mine is yours.'" The old na9 eye twinkled. Ah I 'he said, I've Ti good many of the first sort; I never met a„ of the second. However, here's £;: for 57‘ fund." * * * An important addition should be made the list of unusual visitants with 1,1 Warrington has lately been honoured. time it is a rara avis indeed. At au'e 7 o'clock on Thursday morning an e%el tionally large bird—which careful observ. declare was an eagle—was seen conteMPP ing Church-street from the dizzy height the Parish Church spire. The attention passers-by was soon attracted and ever that early hour many people pad; to gaze upwards with an inteittri and persistence worthy of an echilij Field glasses were brought into I ut—happy to relate—no one seems to A. 11 thought of shooting the eagle, or whatet it was, ancl presenting it as an offering t°,,i Museum authorities. IThe majestic " emained on the spire until about ,)'clock when, apparently scared by the of an adjacent works bell, it soared higul' the sky and vanished in the directio3 Chester.</p>
        <p>* * 1- Don Quixote " tilted " at windmills. T Rev. A. M. Mitchell frequently tilts at tbi! equally unresponsive and undismayed. times it is the Chairman of the "Guar other times a benighted fellow mernbe_re the service of the hated "Trade." This it is smooth chins that the rev. gentleman 'I fallen foul of in that famous Parish Magt,zi which must fill more than one home "c glee. Mr. Mitchell sighs for the good t days when men grew "the manly venerai beard 'S (he sports one himself) and regrl this degenerate age of smooth china the " too-old at forty " bugbear. lie -t " What are we now ?—a beardless and tcS:lt less race!" But in the days when b,e59 were bounteous, bushy and beautiful, t teeth were defiers of decay, men masticA meat most manfully—which is one ta' many deadly sins in our reverend frieco eyes. Should he not, therefore, share 6 a of the blame for the blushing cheek ra the gold-capped tooth of this degelle generation?</p>
