      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>OUR PRIZE SCHEME.</head>
        <p>OUR PRIZE SCHEME.</p>
        <p>Carton's Fire Debris Sold. A sale by auction as a sequel to the recent fire at Messrs. Garton's took place on Thursday morning. After prospective buyers had viewed the ruins a brief sale followed,and the scrap iron was knccked down to Mr George Howard ,iron broker, Victoria-road, Grappen hall for the sum of £5OO. We understand that Messrs Dolan's have come to some arrangement with the firm in regard to the brick-work.</p>
        <p>" PREMIER " CYCLlS.—Special lines at £5 10s. or with 3 speed gear, £6 15s.—Agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51-55, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. Warricgton Women's Circle. , A most enjoyable evening was spent by the members of the above in the Clubroom, Bridge-street, on Thursday, when, before a crowded audience, the junior Bible band of the Circle executed a most interesting programme. Most of the parents and friends of the young performers were present, together with many outsiders, and judging by the lock of pleasurable anticipation ontthe faces of those assembled, all came expecting a "good time "in which they were not disappointed. Choruses splos and duets were interspersed with drill, march and gymnastic displays. In the latter, Miss Jones, who has most kindly undertaken the gymnasium class during the present session, delighted the audience with a study in clubs which was as fascinating as it was clever. A sketch written for the occasion by Miss Ball (leader of the circle), entitled " A. little child shall lead them," was executed without a bitch and received clamorous applause. This was followed by an object lesson on " The life and mission of a collecting box, as told by itself," which tack the fain of a reading adapted for the occasion by Miss Ball and evoking great interest. It will have inspired many hearts with fresh zeal in the work There was a short interval for refreshments after which the rest of the prog amine was executed and, with murmurs of satisfaction on all sides, the happy gathering dispersed. Many were the congratulations cffered to Miss Ball, who, as leader of the circle was mainly responsible for the success of the evening. The junior band of the Women's Circle which has only lately been organized and is so rapidly growing will, it is hoped and believed, be a special feature of interest in ths work in the future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Awbery's Superannuation.</p>
        <p>MISS BROADBENT'S PROTEST.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held at the Workhouse on Wednesdiy, Mr E L Bent presiding. There were also present : Mrs Collingwood, Mrs Simpson, Miss Broadbent, Dr Murray, Messrs A 3 Jolley, J BiHinge, W Gill, G Mather, W Houghton, A Brooks, W C Brooks, R Lee, J A Hughes, G A Allen' J A Rushworth, M W Seton, J Backhouse, P Uongton A Bottomley (clerk), R T Partridge (assistant clerk), 0 Moss (acting mister) and A Wright (superintendent of Cottage Homes).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>NEW RELIEVING OFFICER.</head>
        <p>NEW RELIEVING OFFICER.</p>
        <p>On the propeit'ion-7fMr JOLLEY, secoaded by Miss BROADBENT, it. was decided that Mr R W Peacock, assistant clerk, be appointed relieving officer for the number 4 district at a salary of £l2O per annum, rising on September 16th, 1912, to a maximum of £l3O per annum.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>' OFFICE LEASE RESCINDED.</head>
        <p>' OFFICE LEASE RESCINDED.</p>
        <p>The Rev A NI MITCHELL bad givto notice of a proposition to rescind a previous resolution of the Board leasing the premises 19 and 21, King-street, for office purposes. As the rev, gentleman was not present at the meeting, Mrs Simpson moved the resolution, contending that the building was not suitably situated to be used as offices of the Board. The resolution was unanimously agreed to. MR. AND MRS. AWBF.RY'S SUPERANNUATION. A PROTEST.</p>
        <p>Mr A J JOLLEY moved a resolution asking for the sanction of the Local Government Board to a proposal already approved by the Board, on the recommendation of the Finance Committee : that in view of the fact that Mr Awbery has been compelled to resign his post owing to illness, and having in mind the large amount of extra work which Mr and Mrs Awbery have performed in connection with the building recently erected for the accommodation of the aged and infirm inmates, that for the computa- tion of the superannuation allowance three years be added to Mr Awbery's services and two years to Mrs Awbery's services. Miss BROADBET.,IT alluded to the offer of Mr Awbery to devote his superannuation allowance to a benevolent fund for the relief of deserving cases. For how long would Mr Awbery gives them the superannuition money? The CLI:RK : I take it that Mr Awhery has men- tioned no time whatever. Mr MATHER : During his pleasara. The CLERK Of course, Mr Awbery has not yet received any superannuation allowance, and 1 may Inform the Guardians that he sent me a cheque for 5 guineas to be going on with, and some of that money bas already been put to good use. It is entirely at Mr Awbery's pleasure. No time is mentioned at all. Miss BROADBEW : I feel it is public money WO are asking for. We all know that Mr Awbery is going in for gambling in a professional way—in a way, lam told, that would not be permitted in England. Are we justified in asking for money-- 1 he CHAIRMAN I don't think it is a matter for the Board. IVhatever business Mr Awbery thinks fit to take up after he leaves our service, we cinnot interfere. MiS3 BROADBENT asked if it would he honourable to ask the Local Government Board's sanction to the proposal without acquainting them with the circum- stances to which she bad called attention. . .</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN : I am afraid you are raising a point that I cannot allow you to proceed with under the circumstances. It is not a matter for the Board. It is a private matter for Mr. Awbery. Miss BitoapeENT : I have seen his circular. I think all the Guardians have seen it too. Mr. A. Bnooirs and several other members. "No." The CHAIRMAN : I think one or two members have seen it. Miss BROADBENT : I don't think we are just'fied in asking for money— Tne CHAIRMAN : I don't like to object to listen to Ton, but it teams to me that under the circumstances it is out of order. Miss BROADBENT : One has been very sorry for this gambling example to be set. lhe CHAIRMAN : The question before the Board at the :present time is the question of Mr. Awber'ys superannuation and whether we shall extend the time. Miss BROADBENT moved an amendment that the Board should not ask for an extension. The CHAIRMAN : It must be a vote either for or age inst the resolution. On a vote being taken the resolution in favour of an extension of the time was carried by 10 votes to ti, 7 members abstaining from voting. The two dissentientt were Miss Broadbent and Mrs Collingwood.</p>
        <p>TREATMENT OF THE FEEBLE-MINDED. IMPORTANT PROPOSAL._</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the North-Western Poor Law Con- sereoce the following resolution was passed :—" That this Conference is of opinion that it is desirable Oat all the Boards of Guardians in the area of the North- Western district should avail themselves of the oppor- tunity offered by the Tarvin and Lunesdale Unions to receive feeble-minded adults and children into their institutions at a cost not exceeding lea. per week." The approval of the Board to this proposal was asked. Mr. MATHER proposed that the Board should comply with the suggestion. It would relieve them to some extent. The Criaium.tiv said it was a very important proposal and he thought it would be to the advantage of the Board to accept it. They had cases now which were costing them 12s 01 per week and the sooner they could transfer them to Tarvin the betcer. Miss MiOADBENT seconded. The CLERK said there was do doubt that legislation would soon be brought about to deal with such cases. The question was: - Were the Guardians going to deal with them or was another body to be formed t#6 take them in hand? The idea of putting such patients together in special training homes was what the Government required, and be strongly recommended the Board to adopt the resolution and get feeble-minded but uncertified cases sent to the institutions. Mr. JOLLEY supported the resolution because it did not bind them absolutely to any expenditure. He ventured to think th:t before they sent any consider- able number of cases away, they would want to satisfy themselves completely that they were going to benefit by the treatment. He was doubtful whether the Tarvin Workhouse would answer the purpose satisfactorily. Mr MATHER : It is the first step towards taking the feeble-minded out of the workhouse. If we don't do it, somebody else will. Mr JOLLEY : You should send them to a proper institution not to a place worse than your own. Mr Mather: It is not worse than our own. The CLERK said the institution would be put iii order for the treatment of the class of patient in ques- tion. In future it would not be known as the Tarvin Union Workhouse. Mr BILLINGE : It would appear that the Local Government Board is going to discontinue the Work- house there. The CLERK : That is the idea. Mr BILLINGE : Are they going to equip it as a special institution for the feeble-minded. The CLERK said there would be special teachers to train the children. The Board approved the re3olutloo.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>Exammer, Saturday, May, 11,1912.</head>
        <p>The Exammer, Saturday, May, 11,1912.</p>
        <p>WELL-KNOWN BOWLERS' SUICIDE.</p>
        <p>Depressed Through 111-Health.</p>
        <p>The death has occurred under tragic circumstances of a prominent figure in local and county bowling circles in the person of Mr. George Holbrook, wire- works foreman, (17, Algernon-street, Manchester-road, Warrington. On Wednesday afternoon the deceased was found banging by a cord suspended from the door of his bedroom. It appears the deceased has not been well for some time, having suffered from nervous debility and was seen asleep in bed by his wife at noon on WedLesday but when she went up later she found the door locked and got no reply when she called. She brought in a neighbour named Bell wiao burst the door open and found Holbrook banging from a hook behind the door. He cut the cord and called in Dr Anderson. The deceased bad been attended during his illness by Dr. Langdale. During the last twelve months Holbrook has not figured so prominently on the blwling green as in former years, his absence being attributed to his failing health. He was conspicuously identified with the Cemetery and Works Bowling Association and had played on many occasions for his couniy and gained county honours. In addition to numerous medals and trophies Mr. Holbrook was successful in winning the "Steel " Challenge Cup twice in the Oddfellows' competition and had may other mementos of successes with the woods.</p>
        <p>THE LATE MR. G. HOLBROOK.</p>
        <p>THE INQUEST.</p>
        <p>Mr S Brighouse conducted an inqu3st at the Warrington Coroner's Court yesterday afternoon. Mrs Holbrook, the widow, slid that on Wednesday morning about 8 o'clock, her dc ceased pot up and complained of feeling unwell and appeared very depressed. Later on he expressed a desire to g to sleep and she save him a sleeping draught and he went to bed. Dr Langdale called about 11.30 and the deceased was fast atlee^. At one o'clock she (witness) found him asleep an.cl left him and on going upstairs again about 3 o'clock found the room door was locked and she sent for John Bell, who burst the door open and found him hanging as stated. The Coroner : Had he been depressed ? At times he used to get very depressed but never gave me reason to think he would do himself any harm. John Bell,greengrocer,Granville-street, gave evidence as to his being called to the house and cutting the deceased down from the hook and sending for Dr. Anderson. He had known deceased fairly well and he had seemed to be depressed. Ile had said he did not like the idea of drawing his wages without working for them. The coroner to the jury : That shows he was a very honest man. It is very unusual now-a-days. Continuing, the coroner said Detective Woolacott had seen Dr. La3gdale, w 13.3 bad said he was not surprised at the case as it was one of break-down. The deceaseds brother had c Imm tted suicide some time ago. The jury returned a verdict of "suicide whilst of unsound mind."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>BROTHERS.</head>
        <p>THE BROTHERS.</p>
        <p>"F. E." FLIPPANT &amp; HAROLD VULGAR</p>
        <p>LAST NIGHT'S MEETING</p>
        <p>Mr. F. E. Smith, K.C., M.P., was the principal speaker at a mass meting of Cons( rvative in 63 Parr Hall last evening. By his mastery of invective and sarcasm "F. E. ' is always able to command a large audience and on this occasion the hall was crowded. Above the platform appeared the touching appeal "Can the loyalists of Ireland depend on you again? ' Echo might have answered : " Where was Carson? " bat was providentially silent. Mr. W. P. RYLINDS, who presided, made reference to therecent debate in the House of Commons in , which the Member for Warrington " distinguished himself so peculiarly "—as he aptly to put it. Mr. F. E. &amp;urn began his speech with the familiar Tory denunciation of the Parliament Act —"the most fatal menace to democratic liberty in this country which had ever been put forward by any government,' Warrington listened to the warnings given by the Conservatives before the last electicn and what Wallington thought then, the whole of Lancashire was thinking to day. (Applause). Having referred to the " South Manchester fiasco," Mr. Smith said that at the present moment if the Government wished to promote a minister, they promoted him, not in any relation to the merits t f the Minister but in relation to the size of his majority (Laughter). The result of the Parliament Act was not to cripple the House of Lords but to cripple the electors and deprive them of their legitimate control of legislation. " At the time of the last election" asked Mr. Smith did any man or woman in Warrington know that the Government were going to introduce the Insurance Act? Did anyone in Warrington know what the terms of the Act were going to be. I have always belonged to the number of those who said that it was highly desirable, in the broad national interest, that some measure of national insurance should become law, and as long as we were given an opportunity within reason- able limits of time of improving the Bill without attacking its main principles, those opportunities were generously used by the Unionist party. Mr. Lloyd George went into ONE OF THOSE PFLPITS _ _ _</p>
        <p>in which his powers of speech undergo euch develop- ment. (Lsughter.) He made a litter party attack upon the Unionists, although only eight or nine weeks before he had thanked them for helping him to make the nrasure a better one."</p>
        <p>The r:agon why Mr Lloyd George guillotined the Insurab C 3 Act through, was because the present session or POW:nut was given over to Ireland and to Wales, and there wag no time for Q.e tar•-p2r discussion of a Bill which concerned the interests of the English werke,s. " Now the Government have appointed a horde of lecturers " went on Mr. Smith, paid for by you and me, who go over the country explaining the Insurance Act. Up to the present they have cost £2.5,000 ; that is a trifle—about the yield of the land taxes —(laughter)—and they go about the country explaining to the people what the Insurance Act really means to the people who, on their theory, are supposed to have known at the last election what every Bill that was going to to carried In the House of Commons really meant." . . . .</p>
        <p>Mr. SMITH next waxed pathetically indignant about the Government attitude towards the Housing Reform Bill promoted by the linioniet Social Reform Committee.</p>
        <p>MR. BURNS,</p>
        <p>who seemed to spend his whole p. olitical life in crying " Don't trouble stout soy reform—l'm hero ; (Lalghter).—nothing else is necessary" tried to persuade the House of Commons to refuse to give the Bill a second reading. The promoters of the Bill ssked for £500,000 to use for the clearing away of some of the worst slum areas and give encouragement to various local authorities to deal with the prablarn. Needless to say, the Government utterly refused to provide any matey. They could find £4,000,000 for Ireland but they refused to find a penny for the Housing Bill and they were now doing their best in committee to deal with every clause of the Bill which was of the slightest value.</p>
        <p>....,,. Mr. Smith next embarked upon an attack on</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>lIONIE RULE BILL.</head>
        <p>THE lIONIE RULE BILL.</p>
        <p>He had much to say about the annual subsidy which England would have to pay to Ireland " to persuide her to accept the measure." Everyone of you" he said whenever you smoke a pipe or drink a glass of beer,— if you ever do—you will be able t ) do so with added zest when you reflect that, without being consulted, you are contributing to the Home Rule Parliament in Dublin." (Laughter). It was urged that Home Rule would relieve the congestion of business in the House of Commons, Did they know how many days were given to Irish business in the last session of Parliament? He believed four. That was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>'THE GREAT CONG ESTION.</head>
        <p>'THE GREAT CONG ESTION.</p>
        <p>—(laughter)—to get rid of which they were to pAy O. millions a year. I would rather have the congestion and the money" commented Mr. Smith. He next indulged in gibes about the" remarkable psychological development" which they were expected to believe that the Nationalist member 3, who at every crisis in our history had taken the side of our enemies, had undergone. It was a kind of baptism by total immersion. (Laughter.) "If ever I meet in any capacity" observed Mr Smith "with a sudden and other- wise unexplained repentance, I am cynical enough to ask myself in the first place this question : What are you getting out of it. (Laughter.) For all the safe- guards that are contained in this Bill I would not give you—though I don't value it much—the preamble of the Parliament Act." (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>IF ULSTER DECLINED to be driven into the Irish Parliament, it was en impossibility that any British Government—least or all a Government of exhausted credit and popularity like the present Government—could at the point of the bayonet and aided by British arms go to the Protestant population of Ulster and sax "We will force you by bayonets and by rifles to go into the Home Rule Parlia- ment." If they tried it, a day would have dawned in English history which would recall the most tragic excesses and misfortunes of our civil disturbaaces in the past. Lastly Mr. Smith turned to</p>
        <p>WELSH DISESTABLISHMENT. The Welsh party, he said, did not care a brass farthing about Disestablishrnent unless they got Disendowment with it. M Mr. Elli Griffiths said not very long ago to a Welsh audiance, "There's money in it." (Hear, hear.) He knew the audience he was addressing and the kind of argument they liked. (Laughter.) No more mean and shoddy measure was ever introduced into the House of Commons than the present Bill. (Applause). " I believe that after the next election " concluded Mr Smith " mj brother will be able to call himself, as he can to-day, the member for Warrington' leaving the excellent gentleman whom he defeated the agreeable claim that he is the member for Cannes." (Laughter and applause). .</p>
        <p>THE MEMBER AND THE CLOCKS. Mr. HAROLD SMITH, M.P., who expressed delight at the •opportunity of " playing second fiddle to my brother in the Parr Hall ' devoted much of his speo3h to the clocks controversy. At the dumping exhibition Lady Greenall, with the generosity which every Warringtonian expected from her, gave a prize of a clock every diy. Those docks—at least the clocks which the Radical " Examiner" touched upon—ticked merrily for a few days bat Mr. Parker a Tined them all. (Laughter) Those clocks were foreign, but there was not in any one of them any sign which the microscope could discover which would enable any living person to understand that they were foreign clocks. What right had Mr. Parker or the editor of the " Examiner .' to criticise anybody who bought in the cheapest market and who made no inquiries—if it were true that they did not—where the clocks came from? Free Traders said : Buy in the cheapest market. Never mind who makes the goods." Having referred to Mr Parker's arguments, Mr Smith said that if they were the best which the ablest brain in the local Free Trade party could produce, tariff reformers could take heart (Applause.) They could place Mr Parker politically on the shelf alongside that clock which belonged to their grandfather, and which " stopped short-, never to go again." (Laughter and applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>WARRINGTON FOOTBALL CLUB. Nominations for Annual Meeting.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON FOOTBALL CLUB. Nominations for Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington Football Club will be held on Thursday, May 23rd. The following have been nominated for re-election:— nesident, Mr. Thos. Locker ; hon. treasurer, Mr T Pemberton ; hon auditors, Messrs W Webster and G</p>
        <p>MR. ASHTON RESIGNS. Mr. IL Ashton has resigned the hon, secretaryship, and the only nomination is that of John Winstanley.. The Committee have passed the following special resolution which appears on the notice calling the meeting" That the time has now arrived that the dual office of hon. treasurer and hon. secretary be merged, and the committee recommend Mr T Pemberton for the position."</p>
        <p>COMMITTEE. The following have been nominated for committee : Messrs F Barber, T Barnes, jun., T Bunch, T Davies, W Hewitt, W Nevins, E Osborne, and J 11 Whitham, retiring committee eligible for re-election • H Ashton, T P Bradshaw, J Bibby, J F Foster, W J Lodge, George §mith, S Wright. PROPOSED BENEFIT TO DICKENSON. It is proposed by J A Bleck and seconded by T Barnes, j "That Of Dickenson have a benefit match."</p>
        <p>RECREATION GROUND ASSESSMENT. Rylands' Club's Successful Appeal The annual meeting of the Warrington Assessment Committee was held at the Workhouse yesterday. At the outset the clerk (Mr A Bottomley) presided and there were also present.—Messrs A Brooks, G Mather, J W Knowles, IV Gill, J Billinge, A J Jolley, W C Brookes, E Gunson (valuer) and E Lawrence (represent- ing the assistant overseer of Warrington). Responding to a vote of thanks for his services is chairman during the past year, Mr A Books said the past year might have been one of stress and trouble, and in some Unions it had been, but with the Clerk's advice and a good sensible committee they had got through the work without making themselves the focus of interest in the rating world. It was some- thing to be proud of that they had conducted the ' business without a single appeal against their decisions and without any serious dissatisfaction. Mr. Mather proposed, and Mr. Gill seconded the re-election of Mr. A. Brooks as chairman for the ensuing year. Mr. Knowles, in supporting. said Mr Brooks was a fine busitess man and a good chairman. The proposition was heartily agreed to. Mr. W. Furness was re-elected deputy-chairman. RVLANDS' RECREATION GROUND. Mr H Maddock appealed on behalf of the members of Itylands' Recreation Club against the assessment of their recreation ground in Gorsey-lane at £270 gross, £230 rateable. He stated that the club was formed for recreative purposes pure and simple, and not for profit making. The employees only contributed one penny or twopence each weekly and if it acre not for the generosity of the firm they would practically have to close the place. Their income from all sources just enabled them to pay their way. They had a bar for refreshments but the gross takings since it was opened hadlaveraged only £8 per week. Of that sum, 20 to 25 per cent was for pies and tubacco. Mr Mather said be was surprised to hear that the takings were only £B. He thoueit they wJuld have been nearer £3O.</p>
        <p>Mr Maddock : I think there has been some mis- apprehension with regaid to this. When we opened the club a rumour was circulated that we had an enormous turnover. I hive got here figures which I think will prove to you that what I have said is correct. I have the actual balance sheets for the past three years and we are quite willing that you should peruse them. There is nothing we wish to keep back. All we want is simply to keep our heads above water. We are handioamed in this way, that through our income not exceeding our expen- diture we are prevented from laying out our grounds. They are practically doing nothing. Mr. Maddock added that the area of the grounds was .15 acres and there were four bowling greens, but the expenditure of maintaining them was about treblo the receipts The buildings cost £2,800 and tbey bell thQ lend on a 25 years lease. Ile claimed that if it hid not been for the opening of the recreation club there would not have been as much property in the neighbourhood. The land was very bad—simply marsh land—and they had to go to enormous expense to improve certain parts. The money required for the formation of the club was borrowed from the firm and was being repaid with interest. Membership was also entirely confined to the employees. Mr. Maddock tubmitted figures showing that the receipts from use bar had decreased from an average of RS Os. 3d. per week in 1905 to .£7 Ss. In 1911. The committee decided to reduce the assessment to R:2OC gross, £175 rateable.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>HORSE WITH A DOUBLE.</head>
        <p>A HORSE WITH A DOUBLE.</p>
        <p>REMARKABLE CLAIM BY LYMM FARMER. At the Manchtster County Court on Tuesday, before Judge Mellor, Mr Alfred Henry Derbyshire of Tanyard Farm, Lymm, claimed a horse which he ssid was stolen from his farm in August, 1910. The defendants, in whose possession it was found, were Messrs J Kay and Co., Ltd., carriers, of Green-lane, Gorton. They brought in, as third ratty, John Russell, veterinary surgeon, of Minshull-street, who had sold the horse to them ; and he, in his turn, brought In as a fourth party Joseph Taylor, horse dealer, of Willis-street, Warrington. Mr Merriman was counsel for the plaintiff, Mr Horrowitz for Messrs Kay, Mr Dehu for Mr Russell, and Mr Borgia for Mr Taylor. By agreement between the defendants Mr Burgis conducted the defence, which, the Judge said, was really that of an alibi. Mr. Merriman said the plaintiff claimed either the return of the horse or £lO as its value. and £lO damages for detention and conversion. The animal was purchased by the plaintiff's father in November, 1904, as a three-year-old, and was handed over to the plaintiff in April, 1908, as worth £45. On August 5, 1910, it was turned out to grass in a fenced field, and on the following morning it was missing. The police were notified, the country scoured, and advertisements issued without avail. In November, 1911, the plaintiff's brother came to Manchester, and in Whitworth-street saw a horse working one of Messrs. Kay's vehicles which he instantly recognised as the one that had been stolen. After ascertaining the name of the firm he informed his brother. The next day, as both were travelling on a tramper to Gorton,the plaintiff suddenly recognised the same horse in the street. The police were informed, and in the presence of an inspector the plaintiff eaid that if they would re.nove the saddle they would find certain marks on the animal's back. The saddle was removed, and the marks were discovered, The age, height, build, and other marks on the horse were identical with those of the missing horse. The plaintiff, his brother, and a teatusman who had daily worked the missing animal for two years enumerated the points on which they said it corres- ponded with the one now in dispute, and a veterinary surgeon described an injury that had occurred to a hoof for which he had attended the horse for about a year. The animal in dispute he added, still bore the marks of this injury, lie agreed with the other witnesses that there could be no mistake of identity. For the defence, eleven witnesses gave evidence tracing the continuous ownership of the animal in dis- pute from farmers to hone dealers, and finally to Messrs. Kay, from the time it was bought at a Lincoln auction by Thomas M. Wilmott, a Newton-on-Trent farmer, in 1907—nearly three years before the plaintiff's animal was stoleu. They all described peculisrities in the markings and behaviour of the horse which corres- ponded, they said, with those of the animal in dispute. The Judge, after examining the horse and hearing arguments, said he had never had to deal with a Case in which so many witnesses of the highest respectability had given evidence so fairly and with such apparent conviction and yet so contradictory. That made his difficulty all the greater. In face of the evidence for the defence, he could not find that the pliant' ff had satisfactorily pioved the identity of the horse, and as the onus of proof was upon the plaintiff in such a case, there must he judgment for the defendants with costs</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>INDEPENDENT ORDER OF RECHABITES.</head>
        <p>INDEPENDENT ORDER OF RECHABITES.</p>
        <p>Warrington District No. 4. Ile quarterly meeting of the above District was held in the Wycliffe Memorial Buildings, Warrington, on Saturday last. The meeting was declared open (about 3.30 p.m) by Bro J C Gee, P.D.C.R., who then called upon Bro C S Fellows to conduct the installation ceremony of the following officers elected at the annual meeting : Bro Jlt Barker, D.C.R. ; Bro D Hamblett, D.D.R. ; Bro S Fernley, Cavite ; Bro J Gaskill, Guardian ; Bro T Winstonley, D.S. ; Bro J as Hill, A.D.S. ; Bro T Massey and Bros James Shaw, E Walkden and Wm Wright, trustees.— Bro C S Fellows, having real the officers into their respective positions, declared them duly installed. Bro J It Barker then took charge of the meeting and ordered the roll of representative officers to be called. The whole of the officera and twenty-two representa- tives from the various branch's responded to their names, there being present also a large number of vial tors.</p>
        <p>. The returns from the branches showed the member- ship of the District to be 1,300 benefit members. The juvenile superintendent's report was presented by Bro J R Barker, who stated that the Juvenile District consisted of 21 Juvenile Branches, with a total member- ship of 1,423 and funds amounting to .£533 19s. *l.,and that work of an interesting and educational character on temperance principles, was being done in the tents. He also gave a brief outline of the arrangements that are being made for the celebration of the 21st anniversary of the Juvenile District next September. The executive reported that an unusual number of meetings had been held during the past quarter, to consider and discuss viral matters affecting the district and tents under the Insurance Act. They were also pleased to report that after several preliminary visits to the Independent Methodist schools at Lowton Common a new branch had been opened at these schools and had every appearance of being very successful. The digest from the Board of Directors of the partial amendment of the general rules relating to the National Health Insurance Act to be considered at the special session of the High Movable Conference to be held in the Temperance Hall, Leicester, during Whit week was diacus ed. The delegates, Bros 'l' Winstanley and J R Barker, were instructed to support the recommendation of the Board and to vote in favour of the National consolilation of the Order. A resolution was submitted and carried that each branch of the district send a letter to the Borough Member and the M.P. representing their local areas requesting them to support the legislation now before Parliament in connection with the Licensing and Inspection of Clubs. It was decided to send four officers from each tent to attend a course of Government lectures to be held in Wigan, June 11th to 11th the district to defray the expenses. Recommendation re doctors. The Board desire to express their very strongest objection to the minimum payment of Bs. Gd. demanded by the British Med;cal Association for medical services under the Act, and trust the Treasury and the Joint Committee of the Com- missioners will remain firm against any increase, and adhere to the sum assumed by the Actuaries as being sufficient to cover medical attendance and medicine. This resolution was heartily supported. The next district meeting will he held in the independent Methodist Schools, Lowton Common, under the auspices of the tent. A very interesting item in the afternoon proceedings was the presentation of an inlaid mahogany clock,</p>
        <p>chiming hours and half hours on orchestra gong by Bro. .1 E Gee on behalf of the District, to Bro C G Fellows, P.D.C.R., as a recognition of his having passed through the offices of the District. The clock was supplied by Bro. S Fernley,7l, Bridge street. A vote of thanks on the proposition of Bro C WALKDEN, seconded by Bro W BAXENDALE MIS accorded to the friends of Wycliffe tent (and Pioneer female tent) for the catering for tea and providing the place of meeting. Sister H PALIN on behalf of the female tent, suitably responded.</p>
        <p>PROGRESSIVE LABOUR LEAGUE. Forthcoming Visit of Mr. J. MacVeagh, M.P. The Progressive Labour League w,ll open its summer campaign on Monday, May 20th, when a mass meeting will be held near Arpley Cannons. The principal speakers will be Mr. Jereniab MacVeagh, a leading authority on the Home Rule question. and Mr. A. H. Crosfield. Mr. E. Wynne Tones will preside The debate on the Home Rule Bill in the House of Commons on Wednesday was enlivened by a pers.nal episode. Mr. Jeremiah MacVeagh gave the 110114) of Commons some particulars of the rather singular career of the lively journalist who aims, not without some success up to the present, at a dictatorship over the Conservative party. The manner in which the Journalist in question urged his friends to adopt Home Rule as a policy as a set-off to the destruction of the Parliament Bill, is one of the landmarks of recent political history.</p>
        <p>THE BATHS EXTENSION.' Council and Secret Ceremony. AMUSING DISCUSSION. The extraordinary secrecy which marked the receat foundation stone-laying ceremony at the Carporation baths ;extension in Legh-street, formed the subject of some very amusing passages at the meeting of the Warrington Town Council on Tuesday evening. As we pointed out at the time, although the extension will involve a large expenditure of public money,only those members of the Town Council who happen also to be . members of the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee were aware that the ceremony was taking place, and. considerable resentment was shown at what Alderman Bennett termed the "surreptitious and shame-faced manner "in which the affair was conducted. Councillor PEMERTON raised the question by calling attention to the following paragraph in the minutes of the Town Ball, Parts and Baths Committee :—" That authority be given for the purchase of a stone to commemorate the extension of the public: baths, and that the Chairman be requested' to lay the same on Thurrrlay next, the 11th inst." He said that although the stone had been laid, if the Council were of his opinion they would send the minute back, and he would let the pers,n who ordered the stone pay for it out of his own pocket. It was an insult to the Council to ask their permission to buy something that had already been boughtiThe Council passed "alljthese matters like little children." It was neither businesslike nor manly. The extension of the baths was a matter of great public: interest, and there would not be much change out of £lO,OOO when the work had bent paid for; the corn- - .. mittee bad acted in a cOuricolis tháihei l the rnemberk of the Council would have known about the ceremony. After all, the members of the Council only represented the pablic. They were phased to say they were "obedient servants "—(laughter)—before an election, but they said little about the matter after they had received the ratepayers' votes. All the ratepayers were interested in the baths extension and were entitled to know that the ceremony was taking place. Not even the Press representatives were notified, and the affair was done in a hole-and-corner manner—as if it was something to be ashamed of. It was done in Le.gh-street, behind boards. (Laughter). The MaYoR: But it was above board. Councillor PEMBERTON : Behind boards. And they sneaked in as if they were going to steal something. (Laughter.) He understood it was a deplorable exhibition. He did not think any other committee chairman would have attempted to put such a slight on the Council. Alderman BOLTON: I have got the trowel. Councillor PEMBERTON : No doubt you have. You would have the stone if I had my way, and you would have a rope round it, too. (Laughter). Councillor ROBERTS : On behalf of the most important and influential section of the Council which were not invited to the luncheon— Councillor PEMBERTON : I never mentioned any- thing about luncheon. The MAYOR : It was a commemoration stone-laying not a luncheon. Alderman BOLTON: It was a very good luncheon. Councillor RoBERTS : By whatever name it is called, commemoration or stone-laying or a luncheon, we were not invited. The MAYOR: It was a dinner. Councilor ROBERTS : That makes the offence infinitely worse. (Laughter.) It is another illustra- tion of the scripture saying, that when they expected bread they only got a stone. (Laughter). The MAYOR : I was present, but I had nothing to do with the arrangements. Councillor PEMBERTON.—Shame. Replying to a criticism of the minutes at a later period Alderman BOLTON said the discussion had been dictated by " pura cussedness, because certain people were not invited to that lunch." It was making mischief out of nothing. Alderman BENNETT, in speaking on another qiestion raised in the minutes, expressA delight that Alderman Bolton had been a strong advocate on the question of providing douche baths for children at Council elemen- tary schools, and that he had not brought the matter forward fmav ard in that " sarreptioua aad shame faced manner " in which he inaugurated the baths extension. . Alderman Bor.Tov, replying to a general discussion OD the minutes, Mita-lied that the foandation-stone was a handsome one, and one that would do credit to the town and the Council. He hoped that every time Councillor Pemberton or any of those who did not get to the supper parsed the stone, he would take a. good look at it. If they had an opening ceremony he would see that he and others were not forgotten, if they could raise a subscription that would provide them with a good luncheon. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>_ - PALACE AND HIPPODROME. The overture at the Hippodrome this week " All the Winners" fits in very well with the entire pro- gramme for indeed all t!...e turns are winners and it is quite evident that the management are bent on getting the most up-to-date and striking attractions for the benefit of the large variety-loving public of Warrington. What Russell and Held do not know le the way of entertaining the public with a singing and dancing turn is hardly worth knowing. They hold the audience from the beginning to the end, when deafening applause rewards their efforts. The lady sings, in addition to two other songs, one of those ever popular ditties would emanate from South Carolina, in a manner characteristically American The graceful actions which accompany her voca efforts are done in a perfect and finished style and OEO is rather thankful that this turn wisely omits the loud clapping business which is generally given as an accom- paniment to the twists and turns of the negress, in song. The gentleman shows us in a refined way what a pitch of perfection "eccentric" dancing has reached. Many new steps are introduced and although be dances at lightning speed for some time, his move- ments are so graceful that he dces not show the least signs of any extra exertion. His voice also blends harmoniously with that of his partner in the Western songs. Richard Finberg astonishes the audience with his noteworthy impersonations 'and his exceptionally quick changes. His amazing deftness gives at once the lie to the proverb " more haste less speed " for when he has been off the stage a few seconds be returns "made up" to the hair in the character he has chosen to impersonate. His best study is that of Svengali from " Trilby " and his others are Marcus Superbus in his appeal for the life of Mercia in "The Sign of the Cross. 14 " The Rat" from the "Breed of the Treshams," each being highly iealistic. A short time ago Miss Marvell showed us what could be done in the way of bird training and this week a similar " show" is given by Daisy Graham's educated birds and her equally well trained dog "Baby Spot." The birds are put through similar tricks t) those seen here before but the house fully appreciates the excellence of their training. Burnell's Crazy Gofers appear in a noisy but decidedly humorous scena and prove themselves to be tumblers and linghter-provokers of no mean order. Mr. Sinclair Neill and company pres.mt a sensational dramatic episode " The Bank Thief" with a moderate amount of success, the story of the trusting wife, the gambling husband and the lover, being too highly- coloured and over-acted to please. Felix and give 113 a splendid acrobatic turn. Some feats are performed by " Fisher " who, hair -raising however, calmly alights on the stage with a sort of "What's the matter? expres-•ion on his face, and " after indulging in a few laughable preliminaries mounts the trapeze and does a looping the loop trick on a metal swing. Fred Shephard scores a decided success with his comedy, but pleases even better with his sentimental item. Maggie Fraser is a capital chorus singer and gives two catchy songs in a pipiant manner. Excellent animated pictures conclude an enjoyable two hours' entertainment. NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME. The Glazeroff troupe in "The life and habits of the Circassians in the mountains of the Caucasus" top the bill next week and the next place of honour is alotted to the Four Maisanos, who will appear in a first class musical show. The Melbins, original comedy artistes, , C II Charlton the phenomenal conjurer and other tains will also appear.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>CORPORATION INSPECTOR'S FRAUDS.</head>
        <p>CORPORATION INSPECTOR'S FRAUDS.</p>
        <p>THREE MONTHS FOR INGENIOUS THEFTS.</p>
        <p>A case in which a Corporation employe received three mont bs for embezzlement was heard at the Warrington Police Court yesterday (Friday),before the Mayor (in the chair), Messrs S Wright, A J Jolley, T Locker, and 0 Pilling. A slot meter collector for the Corporation, named William Briggs, Frederick-street. Latchford, was charged with embezzling three turns of £6 Hs 9d, 178 lid and 7s 4d, the moneys of the Corpora- tion. Mr T S Steel defended and pleaded guilty to the charges, Mr A T Hallaway, deputy town clerk who prosecuted, said that the prim ner had been employed by the Corporation for fifteen years and for the last six years he had done slot meter collecting. Each collector was allotted a certain district in the town and had to clear the meters once in three months. The moneys received had to be entered in a receipt book with counterfoil attached, and at the office the SUMS were entered in the schedule ledger. There was no snspizion aroused against the prisoner until the 17th April, when it was found necessiry in order to get the whole of the meters cleared as expeditiously as possibie to put a relief man on the &amp;strict allotted to the prisoner. Briggs gave this inspector certain meters to collect from and gave him the amounts registered at the last collection. It was d;scovered that in one house there was a difference of 7s 61 between the receipt book and the ledger. Briggs was informed of this, and on the following morning be went on his round and did not come back at night. Two inspectors went to his house hut did not find nim there. On examining his book it was discovered he had collected £6 16s 11d, and there was only 2s 2d in copper in his bag. He bad cleared another meter in Chester-road. On looking through the books, according to the schedule ledger this meter had not been cleared at all. On exlmination it was found that in this c,se there was lis 9d shalt, according ta the index fingers on the meter. It was then found out that Briggs had taken 17 7d from the meter and the counter- foil was only made out ts the amount of 7s 41 The third charge rf specting the is 4d referred to a meter in Clegg-street. Mr. Holloway said be was instructed by the _Gas Committee that the case was a very serious one. . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel, for the defence, said Briggs was a man whom they had all known for a number of years. He personally had always been struck by the cheerful manner in which he went about his duties. Underneath that there was a sad heart and there vas a story which would not have come to light if that charge had not been brought that morning. Recently his client had been in financial difllulties and had got in the hands of money-lenders. Unfortunately, when the distress Came be was overwhelmed and took slightly to drink and had taken some of the money belonging to the Corporation. He pleaded for lenient tnatinent in view of the prisoner's previous good character and another fact which should weigh with the magistrates in their decision was that he was a married man with a family. He suggested that justice would be met by binding him over under the Probation At, and that the other charges should Le adjourned. In reply to the Mayor Mr 11411 away said that the defalcations had extended over a period of six months and amounted to over £5O. The magistrates retired and on their return the Mayor after sentencing 13riggs to three months imprisonment said the present system was open to improvement, lending itself to collectors to commit offences of that kind. Mr. Hallaway said he had not fully explained the system to the magistrates but if the bench desired Mr Howard would explain it in detail to them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <p>St. Peter's Church. The school anniversary was celebrated on Sunday R hen the preacher in the morning was the Rev H Eaves, of St Andrew's Oldham, and in the evening the Vicar, the Rev B Percival. In the afternoon the young women's Bible class gave a service of song, entitled "Nothing to nobody," the connective reading being supplied by Mrs Percival, and the orgsn accompaniment by Mrs Oreaves ; these ladies deserving much credit for their careful training of the classes for the perform- ance. Special hymns were rendered by the children at the morning and evening services. The collections realized ,ElO 3s.</p>
        <p>Holy Trinity Clubroom. We have no doubt, says Holy Trinity "Parish Magazine," that oar readers will have heard of the important alterations and improvements that are being carried out by the committee in the clubroom. It is not many months since the committee made a very implrtant addition to the club premises, and it speaks very well for their energy and interest in the institution that they should so soon have embarked in the very serious business of raising the ceiling of the room some three feet, of providing improved Tentilation and renovating the interior generally. The work is now in progress under the supervision of Mr. Thomas Davies, jun., and there is every indication that the work will, when completed, be a great improvement. We tender the committee our congratulations and thanks. Rector of Lymm and Undignified Tactics." I feel I ought to say a word on the Welsh Disestab- lishrnent Bill which has lately been introduced into the House of Commons, says the Rector of Lymm (Rev G B Thurston) in the Church Magazine. The pretexts for the Bill are so flimsy and the details so mean and sordid that one hardly has patience to consider it seriously. It is sufficient comment on the tactics into which the Government has been forced that the debate had to be postponed till the 76 Irish Roman Catholic members could return from the National Convention in Dublin, to make up the majority of 74 by which the first reading was passed. Such a cynical action I should imagine is unprecedented in British politics. Church Establishment may be right or may be wrong, but it certainly does not help any cause to be promoted by such methods as these. Sorry as one is, for the anxieties of the Welsh Church in this attack on their ancient endowments, one is still more sorry for a great party in the State that finds itself compelled to resort to such undignified tacties. There is some comfort in feeling that they have no chance of success.</p>
        <p>All Saints' Mission. A successful concert was given by the members of Bewsey Male Voice Choir in the above on Monday, the proceeds being in aid of the New Mission. The follow- ing artist's appeared. Tiny Doris Welsby, Miss E Shaw, Mr H Bretherton, Mr W Wright, Mr W Shelly and Mr Joe Nurse, humourist. The Rev JII Banks, 8.A., occupied the chair. lhe select ions given by the choir were well rendered. Tiny Doris Welsby was heard to advantage in several songs which were deservedly encored. Miss E Shaw,Messrs H Bretherton W Wright and W Shelly contributed songs in a very capable manner. Mr Joe Nurse created a promising impression w.th his bumourous songs, which caused much laughter. The concluding item cf tbe programme was a laughable sketch entitled "The Irish Doctor" -which greatly amused the audience.</p>
        <p>Otir • 4„.7v, •-,56 -12111 a e hurit I c .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>Plea for the Beard.</head>
        <p>A Plea for the Beard.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell, Vicar of Burtonwood, has the following in his Parish Magazine :—" Beards are not fashionable to-day. The razor is in demand ; the penny shave is popular. Our mankind are qualifying for Egyptians ! With a change of headgear they would Prove fair imitations of the males of that ancient race. The close shaven dominate the great kingdom of labour and the world of business. The manly, venerable beard has lost its good name and is no longer an object of youthful ambition. Alas ! the beard is a tell-tale--it is a revealer of secrets ! The beard reveals in grey hairs, the years which most men are so anxious to conceal. Shave and be young ! The workman, the business man must not run the risk of being " too old " at forty, hence the beard has gone and with it the venerable appearance of the fathers and older men. The razor was a deceiver ever—it gives the youthful appearance at the expense of our good looks—it may deceive the eye, it cannot set back the old grandfather clock. The desire to appear young when bread and butter depend upcn youthful appearance is, on the whole, natural. But what a shame the beard —nature's throat protector and adorner of the male physiognomy—should be sacri- ficed to the tyranny of expediency. I would have all men bearded ; the unbearded I would penalise ! What are we now ? A beardless and a toothless race. Grow your beards and keep your teeth ! The strike has not been without its lessons and its gains. Lo ! our miners and other a of the great army of the unemployed have been growing beards ! For why ? There is hardly a razor to be found amongst them, and they lack the pence to pay the barber. Poor barber ! He, too, has</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <p>Hi</p>
        <p>ORLICKS</p>
        <p>corrects</p>
        <p>indigestion.</p>
        <p>Besides benefiting from the ex- ceptional proportion of nutriment it in florlick's, dyspeptics receive last- ing good to their digestive organs. Horlick's is often invaluable as a complete diet. Always beneficial between meals or with other food. Palatable, economical, easily pre- pared—only add hot or cold water. 16,2,6 &amp; I 1!-. of all Chemists, Grocers &amp; Stores Write Dept 98 for Free Sample and Booklet HORLICK'S MALTED MILK CO., Slough, Buc:.s (SS ALTED MILK</p>
        <p>lost his weekly wage. Beards, it seems, are not good for trade, for neither barber nor doctor. Well ! The barber is not a bad sort—our barber is one of the better sort, a kind and worthy fellow. The doctor? Ab, in his case," distance lends enchantment to the view." " After all, I had rather be shaved than physicked ! "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>Is It the End?</head>
        <p>Is It the End?</p>
        <p>The great strike is over ; the coal war of 1912 has ceased. Compared with the thirteen weeks of battle in 1893 this latest war was brief—lasting just six weeks. [What tales of woe, what unwritten tragedies lie behind those six never-to-be-forgotten weeks! But now the days of want are passing,the days of plenty are on their way. Jubilate ! Long may the days of peace and plenty reign ! Our men (writes the Rev A M Mitchell in (ha Burtonwood Parish Magazine) can now resume white collars, our women don their pretty blouses. For how long shall all this be ? Is it peace? Is it the end ? the end of strikes ? of coal strikes? of all strikes ? of strike calamities? of strike privations? These are hard questions—hard to answer. Perhaps, indeed, perhaps the time of industrial peace and goodwill is not just yet. Unrest is still abroad, the Labour world is full of tinder, a spark, one little spark will set it all ablaze. It may soon become the battlefield again. No particular prophetic gift is required to forecast in the near future, a instead deadly struggle between the united forces of labour and the concentrated powers of capital. For the time being there is a cessation of hostilities—nothing more, there is no permanent, no abiding peace. It is hardly to be expected. The passing of one order and the introduc- tion of another is never without noise and tumult, pain and stress. "Let sot your heart be troubled, neither let it be afraid." The kingdom of heaven is on its way : the kingdom of heaven is at hand. Tharsei ! "Through much tribulation we must enter the Kingdom of God."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>Brick-street Mission. Sunday School Anniversary.</head>
        <p>Brick-street Mission. Sunday School Anniversary.</p>
        <p>The above anniversary was celebrated on Sunday last. The preacher for the day was Mr. Will Price of Wigan (connectional president), who preached two very encouraging and inspiring sermons. In the morning and afternoon the scholars paraded the district, headed by the Sunday school brass band which played hymns and sacred marches very nicely, under the leadership of Mr Henry Shaw. The afternoon was set apart for the cantata, "The Children's Saviour." Mr R Henshall, of Latchford,presided and gave a short address to teachers and scholars on the benefits and blessings of .'7'unday school, encouraging the teachers to sow the good seed. The choir and children sanevery sweetly and effectively. Solos and duets were very well rendered by the Misses H Howell and Shaw, A Harding, Elsie Barton, Ethel Lightfoot, Phylis Ingham, and Jane Riley. At the evening service the choir sang in good style the anthem "Hark, bark my soul," Miss H Howell singing the obligato solo very finely, Mr Barton, jun., rendering the baritone solo with taste and expression. The quartett " God is a spirit " by Sterndale Bennett was sung with good expression by Miss Eva Shaw, Mr and Mrs W Barton, jun., and Mr A Morris. There were large congregations at the afternoon and evening services and the collection, which is devoted to the Sunday school funds, amounted to a litttle over £l2.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Thrre was only a fair attendance on Sunday. The Secretary presided. Mr. G. Davey Smitir gave a very interesting address on Letter writing." He quoted the ancient method of letter writing by men who had helped to form history. The mind of person to person was expressed by letter. Our future largely depends up7n the letters we wrote. Young, middle-aged and even old people ought to be most careful as they wrote upon a clean page of their own history. Lat the writing be good, clean and thoughtful so that in future the reading would be satisfactory. The Chairman led the prayer. Mr. McEachern read the lesson. Mr. Payton presided at the organ and also played an organ solo which was greatly enjoyed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>Proposed New Congregational School Chapel</head>
        <p>Proposed New Congregational School Chapel</p>
        <p>The Congregational School Chapel, which it is pro- posed to erect in Elmwood-avenue, Padgate-lane, is intended to continue leligions work which was originally established nearly flfty years ago in premises in Brick- street, attached to the Cockhedge Mills of Messrs. Armitage and Rigby, Limited. A committee, comprising members of Wycliffe Church and of the Mission, has for some time been considering what may best be done, and the design and plans for the building have been prepared by Mr. S. P. Silcock, F.R 1.8. A., and approved. There will be seating accommodation found for 300 worshippers, also a Guild room, usable as an infant school, a minister's vestry, seven class rooms with all necessary offices. The total cost—including furnishing and an existing (hack of £BO upon the pur- chase of the land—is estimated at £2,500. —Towards this probable outlay (says the Wycliffe Congregational Church Magazine) about £1,350 has already been 'promised. This amount includes £2OO from tbe existing Mission, which makes with £4OO previously raised for the purchase of the site a total contribution from the Mission of £OOO. Subscriptions are now invited towards providing the requisite sum, so that the work may be commenced at as early a date as possible. It may be added that it has been decided not to proceed with any building operations whatever until 00 per cent of the required amount has been promised.</p>
        <p>Bewsey-road Sunday School Annliersary. Large congregations assembled at tthe anniversary services held in connection with the Silver-street Wesleyan Sunday school at thelßewsey-road IVesleyan Church on Sunday. The annual visit of the Rev. J. Grange Bennett on the occasion of this anniversary is eagerly anticipated and a large number of outside friends joined in the services. The rev. gentleman 'preached two eloquent sermons and in the afternoon the Bev W A Beckett gave a forceful and interesting address to the teachers and scholars. Mr C E Parker was the chairman at the musical service in the afternoon and he gave an educating address on the subject of public health. Two violoncello solos " Simple Aveu " and " Meditation and Prelude" were given by Mr Edgar Balmford in is pleasing manner and special hymns were also sung at each service by the children and choir under the conductorship of Mr W. llipkiss. At the morn;ns service the duet " Love Divine " as feelingly rendered by Miss Hipkiss-and Mr W Tynan.—At the evening service a grand chorus " Awake the Harp" (Haydn) was inter- preted in finished style by the choir. The children, who had been trained by Mr Hipkiss, al:o sang special hymns. Mr NV Pearce presided at the organ, and prior to the evening service he gave an organ recital which was thoroughly enjoyed. Preaching at the evening service on the words "Thou art not far from the Kingdom of God," the rev, gentleman aaid there were many who were not far from the Kingdom of God by reason of their having been surrounded by the influence of a godly home, and he asked whether it was easy for one to turn aside from the right to the wrong. It was hard for some of them. A fence had been set up round those who kad been brought up in godly homes, which</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Frcin Reynolds' Newspaper.</head>
        <p>Frcin Reynolds' Newspaper.</p>
        <p>If they tried to break through would cause severe wounds. Others were not far from the Kingdom of God on account of their Sunday school training and experience. The rev, gentleman eulogised the work of the Sunday school teacher, and said the harvest came from the seed they had sown, though tb.ny were, perhaps, not privileged to see the gooi result. —The collections, in aid of the Sunday school funds, amounted to £5O.</p>
        <p>Welsh Chapel. The congregation worshipping at the Webb Chapel, Crosfield-street, had their anniversary services on Saturday and Sunday. At the Saturday evening service the preacher was the Rev 0 Lloyd Jones, of Liverpool, and the Rev W Owen occupied the pulpit at the morning and evening services on Sunday. A special English service was held on Sunday afternoon, the preacher being the Rev E Morgan of Padgate. Mrs Sanders was the soloist. The collections were in aid of the building fund.</p>
        <p>Rev. Dr. Mellor on " The New Force in Democracy." The Rev. Dr. Stanley Mellor preached at Cairo-street chapel on Sunday evening on "The new force in democracy." Having referred to the industrial unrest of the past year, he said he sincerely believed we had entered upon a wholly new step in the movement of civilization, that we stood at the beginning of changes in our life more vast and widespread than any which history had hitherto shown, in changes which would affect every aspect of our life, remould cur social and economic organization, radically alter our morality, make our art, our poetry and our literature all diffe•ent, compel us to reconstruct fundamentally our whole scheme and system of religious belief. To live in an age in which it seemed impossible to set any limits to mechanical invention, in an age which beheld the conqurst of the air as well as of the land sod the water, seemed remarkable enough. The changes which such movement implied were surely evident. But those were not the changes of which he spoke now. Ile desired to make them think of some- thing much more subtle and perbaps much more elusive— of changes in spiritual atmosphere as well as in material environment, of changes in mental and moral attitu le, in our whole salmi° of valuation. First of all, let their minds run back to the events of three or four weeks ago. They saw then a state of affairs which was admittedly without parallel in economic history ; they beheld an organized and concentrated refusal to work on the part of a vast body of men controlling a vital industry ; they saw it preluded by a relatively abortive attempt at the same end ; they saw it result in an astounding change in our notions of the function of government • they saw it, above all, as itself the pre- lude to still further similar concentrated out- breaks and refusal. Evers body knew that inevit- ably there would be npre, aid yet more, of these economic disturbances which we so beauti- fully described as strikes. But did they see more ? Did they see in it the most astonishing, the most startling, the most inspiring and encouraging fact in our history for flay years and more past? Did they see that those observers were not wrong who said that this most recent affair of economic disorder was no mere repetition of many smaller outbreaks of apparently similar kind, differing only in scale and degree •, but that it was, as a matter of fact, an event of a wholly new order, really unlike any hitherto shown In economic history. To some ears it might have sounded wilful and foolish exaggeration to speak of this country being on the verge of civil war, faced by the spectre of revolution, but exaggeration it certainly was not. It was mere I bald statement of fact. Tne unity of the movement was not compelled from the outside, by the authority, for example, of the unions. It was doubtful whether the men truste I their leaders at all, it was certain that the leaders were wholly subservient to the men. The compulsion came from within, being the inevitable expression of the individual will to live, resident in each single individual and acting through him. The power that threatened society was the sense,the feeling, the instinct of individuality ; that was why it was tree enough to say that we stood on the verge of revolution, for revolutiun sprang only from the power of that sense of individuality. And it was that sense of individuality that he Lad ventured to call the new force in democracy. Because that thing we called the coal strike wane' from the dissatisfied hearts of thou- sands of dissatisfled men, it was the most signal triumph that democracy bad accomplished since it began ita career in our modern world. For the first time in history, democracy joiaed hands with indi- vidualism; the individual members of the proletariat had become vaguely conscious of themselves as individuals. When they had, as they had now, a society containing multitudes of sach bent on asserting his individuality, of getting from life for himself all that he could of joy and.happiness and good, what must ba the result -? Surely, a breaking down of ancient barriers, a destruc- tion of the old order. To those who really cared for the best in life, the new movement of democracy came with a message of renewed and magnificent hope.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>CURSE OF EVE.</head>
        <p>THE CURSE OF EVE.</p>
        <p>"If men had to bear what women bear, most of them would be in bid three days a month," wrote a woman who knew the suffering. This is the curse of Eve; a wcrnan's penalty for being a woman is that from time to time she must bear agonising backaches, sick headaches, cr a desire (which any mere man would never resist) to lie down and let everything go its own way. She knows that in a day or two she will be better; but oh ! the misery of those days, and oh! the misery of knowing that the same thing will happen again and again ! It was this knowledge that made the late Mrs. Lydia E. Pinkharn devote herself to the work of gi7iog relief to women of all ages - from the girl in her teens to the woman whose middle-age is at hand. She used to make, over the kitchen fire, a vegetable compound of almost magical value for all the manifold aches and disorders of women, and its fame grew and spread until the preparation of it had to be transferred to a scientific laboratory. In order that women who could not come to her for relief might still be benefited, she caused her discovery to be sent for sale to all chemists throughout the world. If any suffering woman who reads this will ask the nearest chemist to supply or obtain for her a bottle of Mrs. Lydia E. Pinkham's Vegetable Compound, she can learn by blessed experi- ence how much this woman has done for all the daughters of Eve: or the Vegetable Compound can be had post free for 2s. 9d. (a very large bottle, 4s. Gd.) from The Lady Manager, c/o Lydia E. Pinkham Medicine Co., 42a, Hampstead road, London, Mrs. Pinkham's famous book of advice free of all charge, to women only, from same address.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>THE PROPOSED BATHS AT EVELYN- STREET SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>LIVELY DISCUSSIONS.</p>
        <p>There were several breezy discussions at the monthly mekting of the Warrington Town Council which was held at the Town Hall on Tuesday evening. The Mayor (Councillor Dr Joseph) presided and there were also presett :—The Deputy Mayor (Alderman Forshaw) ; Aldermen A Bennett, W Bolton, T Burton, John Evans, James Evans, W Pierpoint and D Tinnion ; Councillors G Archer, T H Ashcroft, %V H Bellian, 1' Birtles, T Chantler, E Cunnington, J Dickenson, J 'Halliwell, J Hawthorn, R Elenshall, C Jackson Holmes, W H J Jenkins, F T Lashford,F Lindley, J T Locker, J Pemberton, H Roberts, J Sankey, J Skellaa, T S Steel, 0 Toole, 3 Wilkinson and W J Wilson ; Messrs J L Whittle (town clerk), A T Hallaway (deputy town o'er's!, T H Spinks (assistant town clerk), J Fairhurst (borough treasurer),F V L Matbils (electrical engineer) 3M Murray (director of education), Dr J C Hibbert (medical officer), J Gray (Aater engineer). THE LATE COUNCILLOR ROGERS. - -</p>
        <p>The MAYOR, made a sympathetic allusion to the death of Councillor W Rogers who, he -said, was a highly useful member, who p-issessed sound common-sense and whose cheery, genial manner made him a very popular colleague. lie movel that a vote of condolence be forwarded to the bereaved family. The DEPUTY MAYOR seconded.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS, in supporting, said to him Councillor Rogers was a little more than a member of the Council—he was a personal friend—and he was glad to have the opportunity of paying a token of respect to his memory. Alderman BENNETT said Councilor Rogers was an absolutely straightforward, honest, decent Englishman. Alderman JAMES EVANS referred to the initiative which Councillor Rogers displayed in the work of the Health Committee.</p>
        <p>•The resolution - "wag passed in si:ence, the members standing. _</p>
        <p>POLICEMEN'S PAY.</p>
        <p>The minutes of —tiie—Wrii-ci'd—orWitiee showed that the wages of six police constables were increased by a shilling per week, in accordance with scale. Councillor HAWTHORN asked whether the constables were those who did not come under the superannuation scheme. He thought there were eight or nine officers who did not go under the scheme •nd understood that none of those had received an advance in wages. It seemed that they were being treated rather hardly because the ground n which the pay of the others was raised was on account of the increased cost of living. The MAYOR : I think you ought to give notice of that question, because the Chief Constable is not here and nobody else is able to answer it. We will make inquiries. Councillor lla.wTtrortx expressed himself satified with the promise that inquiries would be made. The minutes were approved. WIDENING OF BUTTERMARKET-STREET. The Street Improvement Committee resolved that the property belonging to the Corporation between Croppers-brow and the property belonging to Mr. William Isaac in Buttermarket-street be demolished, and that the Borough Surveyor he instructed to adver- tise for tenders for such demolition.</p>
        <p>The minutes was approved. THE SMOKE NUISANCE. It was reported thPt a person had been summoned before the committee with reference to the emission of black smoke and severely cautioned. Alderman BENNETT said he should like to see similar minutes much more frequently. He bad not the slightest idea who the delinquent was, but he hoped that if the caution was not attended to, the Council would apply the further stimulus of a fine. Councillor Asllcuorr : I hope we will let those people alone. I think we are proud to see so much black smoke at present. I think Alderman Bennett is too hard altogethe-. Alderman BENNETT I should like to know how many fines have been imposed in the last ten years by the Warrington Corporation on offenders. (" Oh.")</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>HEALTH WEEK AND THE SLUMS.</head>
        <p>HEALTH WEEK AND THE SLUMS.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR called attention to the recent observance of Health Week in Warrington. He spoke of the official visit to some of thelow quarters of the town" last week and said that although the party who made the inspec- tion was not supported by many members, yet he thought there was some enure for encouragement. If such visits could be periodically and systematically made the movement would bear good fruit. The move- ment was not a fantastic thing. " Some of you may look of it with a certain amount—l was going to say— of contempt. (Cries of " No.") Well, as you were not present, I cannot tell. It may or may not be. I will put a favourable construction upon it. lam sure there is some real merit in it, and I commend it to your future consideratlon.' Alderman JAMES EVANS concluded that as so few members accompanied the Mayor " round these low quarters " that they wanted to hear nothing about it, but he expressed the hope that the visits would be made more freonently. He hoped every councillor would make himself conversant, through the rounds of inspec- tion, with the conditions obtaining in the town. Councillor IlAwmostlsr remarked that all who accom- panied the deputation were convinced of the necessity of the Health Committee's attitude on the slum question. They were also impressed with the fact that there was a great deal of work requiring to be done. There had been a considerable improvement even in repairing of the houses, and it showed some disposition on the part of property owners to meet. the Health Committee in the right spirit. He hoped the progress made was only a beginning and that soon there would be no slums to visit. The MAYOR slso referred to the useful and valuable lecture delivered by the medical officer of health. He believed that Warrington made a very good show—as good a show as any town in the country—in the Health Week movement.</p>
        <p>Councillor SKRLLAND said a great many of the tenants required educating to their duties. MORE MONEY TO BE 13ORROWED. ^ • _ _ .</p>
        <p>The Paving and Sewerage Committee resolved that application be made to the Local Government Bosrd for their sanction to the borrowing of the sum of £8,747 Is Gd for the purposes of private street works, and that the Board be requested to allow a period of not less then ten years for the repayment thereof.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>TIPS IN HOWLEY _WARD.</head>
        <p>TIPS IN HOWLEY _WARD.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Paving and Sewerage Committee a letter from the Howley Ward Liberal Committee with reference to the condition of certain streets in that ward, was read and referred to the Borough Surveyor. Councillor ARCHER called attention to the disgrace- ful state of the tips in Howley Ward, adjacent to dwelling-houses, and said he considered it detrimental to public health. One which contained all kinds of refuse was situated near Ellesmere-street School, where there were children from five aid six years of age to ten. He felt it to be his duty as one of the representa- tives of the ward to call attention to the matter. The MAYOR : Councillor Archer is now a member of the committee, and he will be able to inquire into the question. Alderman BOLTON : Not yet. He has not yet been confirmed. (Laughter). Alderman TENSION said that if the members looked through the Hat of streets for which the committee was applying to tFe Local Government Board for powers, they would find that a number in Howley Ward were included. It was not part of the duty of the committee t.) supervize the unsightly, unhealthy and shocking condition in tchich many of the tips were left. There was hardly a tip in good order in the town. The minutes were carried. ST. ELM-YIN'S PARK. " ONE OF IIIE PRETTIEST VIEWS IN _ WARRINGTON RUINED."</p>
        <p>The Town Ball, Parks and Baths Committee had passed the following minute:—" The Borough Surveyor was instructed to prepare plans and estimates for the laying out of t'De land agreed to be purchased from Mr. Peter Lawton, in Padgate-lane, and also the land adjoining the St. Elphin's Park bowling green." Alderman BENNETT raid that ever since the Council acquired the laud in St. Elphin's Par, be had felt that the true solution of the questioh was to employ a landscape gardener to see that the land was laid out in such a way as to harmonise with the surroundings. He had been sorry more than once that he did not press that suggestion to a decision in the Council, but in his innocence he thought the matter would work out all right. The way in which the work had been done bad shaken his confidence in the capacity of that committee to deal with open spaces on proper lines. Some of them might regard him as an ideglist or faddist but on that question be was voicing the practically unanimous opinicn of all who passed the park. A unique oppor- tunity had been spoiled by want of taste or of an ordinary sense of symmetry. Instead of enhancing the I beauty of the spot they had absolutely ruined one of the prettiest views in Warrington. Ile was told that three cyclists riding into the town, on getting opposite that abortion, the new pavilion--(laughter)--dis- mounted and gazed at the structure almost dumb- founded. Then one of them was overheard to say " Did you ever see such a piece of idiotic nonsense in your life—and in these enlightened days, too." (Laughter.) He never thought Alderman Bolton had any pretension to tav,te—(laughter)—except in medals, old masters and orchids. (Laughter). Alderman BOLTON: What about champagne? (Laughter). Alderman BENNETT, continuing, said they had heard various flowers associated with prominent men, and the orchid seemed to suit Alderman Bolton, because it was a very exceptional kind of flower. (Laughter.) It seemed to fit his character to a nicety. (Laughter). The alderman had not only perpetrated that abomina- tion in the erection of the pavilion, but he bad planted a large number of trees and shrubs which could not possibly prow there. Many trees had been planted in that neighbourhood but would not grow, because of the smoke-laden atmosphere. Alderman BOLTON : You have planted them the wrong way up. (Laughter).</p>
        <p>Councillor BIRTLES did not think it would be a bad job if they could appoint Alderman Bennett a member of the committee. (Hear, hear.) Only the pavilion had yet been constructed, and he did not think any com- plaint should be msd3 until the work was finished. If it were completed on the same lines as the bowling green at Latchford, he believed it would give sltisfac- don to Alderman Bennett and the residents of the district. (Hear, hear). Councillor ASHCROFT hoped the remaining portion of the park would be set apart for children to play in instead of planting it with fancy treas. (Hear, hear). Councillor PEMBERTON also emphasised the claims of the children with regard to open spaces. He contended that such spaces ought to be lungs for th) town in the , fullest tense of the word.</p>
        <p>The miuutes were appproved. THE BATHS AT EVELYN-STREET SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee resolved that the Borough Surveyor be requested to prepare plans and estimate for new baths at the Evelyn-street Council School, and to include in the estimate a sum for the provision of a boiler capable of supplying the douche baths at that school. Alderman Jextits EVANS asked how it was that baths were to be built at the school when a considerable extension of the Corporation baths to the tune of £lO,OOO, had been embarked upon? The cost of school baths, should, in his opinion, be borne by the Education Committee. He would like to know how the necessity for them had arisen? Alderman BOLTON : You should know. You were on the committee.</p>
        <p>Alderman EVANS I know nothing. Alderman BOLTON: Then you are very slow at learning. Alderman EVANS I can afford to be slow some- times, and shall be over a j )1) like this. Continuing he said that, so far as he could recollect, there was no discussion on the question in the Council which could be said to have led to the Town Hall,Parks and Baths Committee giving instructions for the preparation of plans. The Board of Education had set its face against the proposed expenditure and, because it could not be carried out under the educa- tional code, the Council was asked to sanction it in another way. He (the speaker) made up his mind at the beginning of the year that he would set his face against expenditure--- Alderman BOLTON: This is a matter of health and you ought to be a warm supporter of it. Alderman EVANS: We are making provision for the town's requirements as far as baths are concerned, in Legh street. Alderman BOLTON : They are for the able-bodied— not for the children. Alderman EVANS : No man in this Council has been pestered more with arguments about the extension of the baths than Alderman Bolton and no man has resisted it as much—l mean with regard to bathing facilities for children. I move that this minute be referred back Councillor WILKINSON seconded the amendment. He said the recommendation of the committee involved an important principle for if Evelyn street School required baths, other schools were equally entitled to have them. A scheme to deal ivith the whole town on those lines would c ist £20,000 or £30,000 The principle ought to be brought before the Council for discussion, before the committee took any definite action.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR : Don't you think it is a matter for the Education Committee ? It seems t 3 be more in their province. Councillor WILKINSON : The Education Committee have been refused permission to put swimming baths in the schools.</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON: The Board of Educ ,tion passed the plans but refused to sanction the expenditure. What's the use of talking soft.? .</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON : Where is the difference ? I am entirely in favour of baths. Alderman JAMES EVANS So are I.</p>
        <p>'Councillor WlLKEstsom : Give all the facilities you can, but the matter is of suoh importance that the Council should first give its sanction to the principle. Alderman BENNETT shared Alderman Bolton's impatience at the apparent want of knowledge which had characterised the praceding speeches. The matter bad been under consideration for years and had COMB before the Council, in one shape or another, again and again. Councillor WITJUNLON : It is very uoc died-for, for Alderman Bennett to speak of the apparent want of knowledge of members of the Council. We have as much knowledg3 of this matter as he has.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT : If I am not expressing an obvious fact I shall be glad to withdraw it. Councillor WILKINSON : You say it too often. Alderman BENNETT continuing, said that during the first year of the Education Committee's work, a deputa- tion visited schools in other towns. One school which was equipped with swimming baths,made a deep impres- sion on their minds and he (the speaker) had advocated the provision of baths at the Warrington schools ever since. He believed that Warrington would be the first town in England to provide douche baths in a school. (Hear, hear.) The Board of Education saw the wisdom of erecting swimming baths at the school, but with a I want of wisdom, declined to go further and sanction the expenditure. If there was to be a swimming bath at all, it would therefore have to be provided by the Town Council and not by the Education Committee. He was surprised that the Chairman of the Education Committee should have taken up such an attitude on the question. It would be a great advantage to the public health if every man, woman, and child could be taught to swim. Councillor ROBERTS said it was fully approved by the Council that, as far as Evelyn-street school was con- cerned, they would support the principle of providing swimming and douche baths. Alderman Evans was wrong in his facts and in his history and, as chairman of the Health Committee, be (the speaker) submitted, with all respect and deference and with a full apprecia- tion of the alderman's work, he was making a serious mistake from the point of view of the health of the children. Tie Board of Education gave a benevolent approval to the scheme for bathing accommodation but the expenditure not being one that cculd properly and legally come within the scope of expenditure for educational purposes, they had reluctantly to refuse their consent to it, at the same time hoping that the Council would deal with the matter by application to the Local Government Board. 1 The present resolution merely carried out in detail the principle which received the confirmation of the Council nearly two years ago. When the baths exten- sion in Legh-street was completed, they would have in the centre of Warrington a magnificent series of accommodation for all kinds of bathing purposes but the use of that accommodation would necessarily be restricted, owing to the distances to he traversed, very largely to the adult papulatien. What were they going to do for the children? The proposal to.provide baths at the Evelyn-street school was a splendid step in the right direction. They would be taking baths into an outlying part, of the town where there had been many deiths owing to the inability of children to learn to swim, through lack of opportunity. It was one of the finest chances the Council had ever had of giving the children facilities to learn not only how to protect their own lives and to save ths lives of others, hut to prepare themselves to be healthier and stronger citizens. Councillor P'EmnErtToN said the question had been discussed very fully in the General Purposes Committee and the attitude of some members that night only showed the lack of interest in the business of the Council. Why teaching children to swim was not regarded as education, he did not know. It would r.,,t matter to the ratepayers whether the Board of Education or the Local Govern- ment Board granted the loan ; the cost to them would be just the same. He was pleased to see Councillor Wilkinson had developed an economical turn of mind. If he could get Is. 6d. knocked off the rates, everybody would be thankful.</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON said he did not know whether the opposition had been raised from pure " cussedness "or with a desire to ma: e mischief out of nothing, fie was surprised:at Councillor Wilkinspn, for he was on the committee and supported the scheme which he regarded as one of the finest things that could be proposed. Councillor Wilkinson : T agree with it now. Alderman BoLToN,continuing, said the committee had been taunted for years with not providing baths for the children. Councillor Jenkins many years ago brought forward a proposal for open-air swimming baths,and the Council now had a fine chance of providing an open-air bath with a roof on. (Laughter.) After school hours,</p>
        <p>t was intended to throw the baths open to ihe Obi; and the question was whether they should allow th,ste to make use of them free, or charge sufficent to psi wages of the caretaker. From the health standpoito was the best thing that could be done in neighbourhood. 000 Aldermen JAMES EVANS disclaimed any autag to the provision of baths. The MAYOR said he thonsughly agreed with the vision of baths,but he could not help protesting isgsi The the inconsistencies of the Board of Education. .os evidently did not know the difference between educatiof and health. Their attitude was either an instaneetter ignorance or of meddlesome interference in °, it. people's provinces, and the Council ought to exPOO Councillor ASIICROFT Said the provision of " would be a blessing to the neighbourhood. Councillor JENKINS said the religious elemert in btift was exceedingly small—(laughter)—but he th0n,,,g13.05, was a feature in the constitution of Alderman D'l'tse and was therefore surprised ta find him opposing is proposal. He should not forget that " Cleanlinegs next to godliness." Alderman TINNION : Where do you find that ? Councillor JeNsiNs : It is in the Old (Laughter and" No. ") Never mind where it is. 'Jolt written somewhere, by an exceedingly sensible insettbet Cleanlines is nellt to godliness." He contended 00, the Council had been short-sighted in not carryingod. the policy, which he carried many years ago, of pro ing open-air baths. is do Alderman BURTON hoped the Council asoll`:„so nothing to defer the construction of the baths, s" district in the town needed them mare. ta4 Alderman TINNION took Councillor Jenkins to se on the scorel of religious knowledge, whereupon!A councillor replied " I am perfectly right. I refer' to an old book.' Alderman Tresntost : You said " the Old -° There is only one. Councillor HaavxnouN said the misunderstaincliog arisen through the dilatoriness of the Town I'aoro and Baths Committee. They had been two Lyot making up their minds to instruct the borough Bur' to prepare plans. Alderman EVANS offered to withdraw the erleijjef merit but the Council on the suggestion of Coneessoltr Roberts pressed for a vote to be taken, with the fr`ity, that the amendment was defeated by a large BAP "DISSENTERS." letterr At a meeting of the Cemetery Committee, s cs from the Rev W Cooper with reference to the Iders‘ the word " Dissenters " was read, and the nocsi tion of the matter deferred. 35' TRAMWAY DRIVERS' AND CONDUCT° WAGES INCREASED. „s At a meeting of the Electricity and Trams's)" soss mittee, a request was considered for concessions 01 it the employees of the Tramyvaye Department, 11,100' was resolved that the wages of the drivers se" ductors be increased Id. per hour. TRAMS AND TRAM-TRACKS. MORE COMPLAINTS. ber Councillor WILSON suggested that a larger nt111.1„, lb. men should be engaged on the work of repair'abn tram-track. Its present condition was dangerous eared. understood that already several accidents had cor Councillor JACKSON HOLMES asked whether tl'e„rs to ductors could not be provided with dusters or sqPd"been keep thesseats of the cars clean. The question ha As it mentioned to him by a number of passensers• silage was, the seats were sometimes covered with oer which bad dropped from baskets used for the c"s' ance of dinners. f f" Councillor PEMBERTON said the condition 9..iugP tram-track was deplorable, and the work of reps stas was not proceeding as quickly as it should do. tod to a wonder to him that the Coporation had not t meet several claims for damages. There issesox in; beers a special Providence watching over the trl thins a long time. Its condition must be a very g°°„d theY, for the wheelwrights and rooter tyre-makers, s"„erfeo; would no doubt be sorry when it was put iii r tbei order. It had been the greatest source of inconie 10 had known in their history. (Laughter). Councillor WILKINSON said they had a class er Irsias° in Warrington who no doubt would like to be Pr" oarS' tbe with coffee and cigarettes while travelling on f (Laughter.) They had to complain simply 13"°.iiroP;)4 workingman's wife was taking his dinner Bud a 00,, grease happened to reach the seat. He advise advise the questioner to carry a " wiper " with° aveto a regular thing. (Laughter.) The committe „ they, obe; proceeding with the tram track as quickly as As to the wheelwrights of Warrington being s°lloo the track was put in repair, his advice to the In t on drove a trap was " keep off our tram lines." m lice° Alderman JAMES EVANS : Take your tram of the road. (Laughter). The minutes were approved. THE NEW BRIDGE. proPertit The Bridges 'Committee resolved that the • ;el sr, belonging to the Warrington Co-operative Steal' Bridge Foot be purchasd and that the cost of e'l° ing be paid in accordance with scale. ssis 9 ssl WHAT ABOUT HOWLEY BRIDGE 'the csts Councillor CHANTLER said that now that ;be as t strike was over, perhaps the Town Clerk WI-11%w coo to say what progress was being made with the nsli bridge at Howley. The TOWN CLERK : I am told by the, ell, Surveyor that the difficulty is caused by tue of He cannot get delivery of the bridge. 4 00115 te Councillor JACKSON HoLsies said the feces% Ise the new bridge had been prepared and the Pl" 0 being driven, so that the work was proceeding. 00 Councillor Cussarmen: I understood there difficulty with Mrs Hewitt. tO The TOWN CLERK : That has been removea•oing Cauncillor CIIANTLER : Then there is ISO" , ICJ stoic) the bridge. The MAYOR : The bridge Is not deliver". , hose cannot fix it till we get it. Councillor ARCHER said.the matter had heetnhat tte ing fire since November. They were assure-„ old to bridge was ready for delivery and that it.awnetrof erected before Christmas. The coal strike dI" ? aserr place till after Christmas. Why all this delsionoth it were led to believe first one thing and thecd 00,130 i, First it was the difficulty with Mrs. Hewitt Ow/stripy was the question of conveying the bridge t°, govr ton. It was