      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>KING AND TITS FLEET.</head>
        <p>THE KING AND TITS FLEET.</p>
        <p>This week the King paid a visit to Wey- mouth to review the fleet assembled there of 120 vessels. On Wednesday his Majesty went aboard the flagship of the combined fleets, the Neptune, and proceeded to sea for battle practice. The outstanding feature</p>
        <p>of the Royal visit has been the display by naval airmen, one of the, battleships, the Hibernia, having been fitted with a launch- ing platform for the machines. Our photo- graph shows the Neptune, on which the King had his quarters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <p>refuse it. They really were not anxious. They thought it would to very nice if they could have the waterworks, but the ratepayere most understand that the offer came entirely from Sir William Lover. Ihe Council refused to pay .C 23,000 because they did not want to purchise,unless they could get the property at a price w'uich would he to the advantage of the raeepavers. Finally the eller had come to them of buying the lake, waterworks, ressrvoir and other lani for .C 20,250. For every reason that could be given to show that the Council would be rong in entertaining the offer, he could give two reasons to show that it would be wrong of them ti rejf et it. It , the property had gone to prhate persons, they mould aleays hive been in the condition of never knowing what wile in; to happen. The lake would have lost its privacy ; and anything might have happened to the the land surrounding it. Another point was that if at any time anybody heel applied for an Electric Light Bill, they would have had to obtain power from the Dingle (which was incle led in the purchase) and el 'citric light would have ruined the Coen:il's gas work. The Council would, by making the purchase, hold the key of the whole district. It would have been worth while for the ratepayers to have got hold of that very valuable property, even if it had cost them a twopenny, threepenny, or even a fourpenny rate. There ea., however, every probability of there being no cost whatever to the ratepayers. He quite joined with the general outcry of the ratepast rs when the Chairman spoke about making .C2OO a year out of the lake. That was beside the rnark—(bear, hear)—but at the same time thus co rld ben ) Objection to allowing boating and fishing by resid ' ents and some revenue would accrue from that. su•qms. At the lowest compu- tation, if they obtained a fifty sears' loan, the revenue from the water undertaking would more than cover the amount they would have to pay off each year. He could foresee the time when Lymm would grow and more residents would be attracted, if the place were properly developed. When they had a gentleman like Sir William Lever ready to throw his heart and his money into the business, they would be very short-sighted if they did not cordially close with his offer. Mr. O. G. Smith said the Council should, if it was at all possible, prevent the land falling into the hands of the exploiter, the adventurer or the jerry builder. When the waterworks passed into the bands of the CoThlcil, they would be safe and secure in that direction and by acquiring the whole of the property they would be able to kee? rewdyism and hooliganism in clock. Once part with the property, and they had no security. At present the Local Government Board andits Pfeil lent (Mr. John Buns) were emphatically in f tvour of long periods for the repayment of loans and there would be no difficulty in getting fifty or (lien eighty years for part of the prom rty. In addition to the purchase price there would be at least £l,OOO for legal and emergency expense.). Sir William Lever had agreed to make a road costing £5,000 or £6,000, and be was the last man in the eorld to sink that, money without getting a return. His only means of getting a return was by opening up the surrounding property for build- ing purposes. As he got his return, the Council would get theirs. Starting, as they did, with the revenue balancing the expenditure and looking forward to the inevitable developnie it, they wee 3 in a perfectly sound financial poi tion in accepting thd proposal. Lymm lent itself pecul arily and happily to sane, artistic, wh us- some development. Ile did not know where they would find another place that offered the same advantages. Ile weuld like to see the Council, the roil say company, Sir William Lever, the Lynam Development Comeany, the Tradesmen's Association and the big land- , oeners of the neighbourhood called together to discuss a well thought-out plan of development. The Town planning Act should first be put into operation ard the sooner they Came to grips with that .question the I etter. i If these schemes were properly car ed out, they would be at the beginning of a period of prosperity for the district of Lymm. (Applause.) The Chairman said it was originally Sir William Lever's inteution to make the road on one side of the lake only at present, but he said that within the next five years he would enter into an agreement to cou- struct the road all round the lake. That afternoon, however, the clerk to the Council had received the following telegram : " It may interest you to know that Sir William, after visiting Lymm a fortnight ago, has docided to make up both roads at once." (Applause). Discussion having been invited, gr. Radcliffe com- plained that nothing had been said as to the condition or capacity of the engines at the waterworks, the averege consumption or the quality of the water. Had the Council had the works valusd by an independent and competent valuer? (Applause.) It was all very well to tell them that the revenue would increase largely so would the expenses, particularly if the capacity of the engines would net meet the consumption. had the contract been signed? If so, what was the object of that meeting ? Were the Council going on with the purchase, despite what the feeling of that meeting might be? He came there with the intention of heartily Eupporting the purchase of the waterworks, became on general principles it was desirable that the community should control one of the first necessities of life' but until a more businesslike proposition was placed bete re them, he could not endorse the action of the Council If they had completed the purchase, they ought not 10 have done so, and if they were about to complete it he still said they ought net to do 80. The Chairman : The contract has not been signed. Mr. Radcliffe: Is it intended to sign it? The Chairman: That is for the District Council to decide. (Cries of "Shame" and "Oh.') Mr. 0. G. Smith pointed out that, mum ing the two engines worked 21 hours a day, they were capable of pumping over 360,000 gallons per day. Ile understood the greatest demands ever mado on the pumping plant were made last year, wh-n they reached 1738,7aciygallons per day—lees than half the total pumping 0 The Chairman said the reservoir capacity had been doubled within the last five years and no extension would be needed in that direction for the next twenty years. The Council were, however, acquiring land near the reservoir,to be pteparad for any future development, Mr Dixon Have you had expert advice from anyone on the condition of the plant? The Chairman said that if they had engaged an expert he would have got his information second band, from the water engineer. The water company bad always kept the plant in perfect order. Only a few years ago a new engine was installed at a cost of £1,200. Dr. Trantom said they had been told that Sir William Lever sought the best advice in the country before making the Council an offer. Did the Council take any expert advice before they decided to give £io,ooo ? Mr Lockyer (a member of the Council): No. Dr Trantom : There is a little bit too much lake and not quite enough waterworks about it. (Applause.) We have not had a word as to the condition of the mains—the most important item of all--or as to the quality of the water. (A voice : "The best in England.) Continuing he said they would look forward to increased revenue from the waterworks, but that development would necessitate the raising of increased quantities of water, Had the Council con- sidered what the quality of the water was going to be, when they put extra pressure on the sandstone from which it was pumped ? As they developed the district they reduced their gathering ground and that would present one of the chief difficulties in the future. Ile was not averse to the Council acquiring the works on a business proposition. Mr Molesworth ; May I ask whether the Town</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <p>Planning Act is to be put in force before the develop- ment takes place ? The Chairman : We have mrv'e application to the Local Government Board to put it in force. Mr Lockyer was of opinion that if they were not going to let the ratepayers have e voice in t e purchase, the meeting was en absolute waste of time. (Applause.) fie strongly objected to it unless the ratepayers had the opportunity of votieg. (Applause.) Boring operatious were at present proceeding in the Bentley district and what would the Council do if the eater supply was eventually interfered with in that way ? lie proposed td at a vote of the ratepayers present be taken in creer to aecertain their ferling and save time. Mr W P Ryland pointed out that the revenue wee the only rock bottom fact they had to deal with in estimating the value of the waterwurks and it was inevitable that, as more !looses were erected, the income must grow very substantially. He was dis- posed to believe that the Commit had g t the best terms they could but he was convinced that if that meeting by a large mf ray asked thorn to seep end the scheme they would reconsider the matter. Ile did not think however, that they would succeed in redueng the price, but they would run a grave risk of the nevi i •tions being broken off and something not so desirable might happen. £20,000 kVbS not an unreasonable price for Si, William to ask under the circumstances. Although he would have preferred to continue under the old con- ditions, whereby a water supply was steered without trouble or risk, he realised that the clump of ow warship had tbr ewe the whole estate into ties melting pot ani it world not be wise for them simply to .allow the future to take care of I itself, In acquiring the worts they would also Ix following out what was recognised throughout t'-e country—that the community should Control such necessaries of life as water and r.s. Mr. Covvper, as an owner of property and employer of labour, supported the action of the Council, proP•ided they did not commit themselvea to a lot of extra exr PDS°. Mr Jones (Heatley) said be would eecond Mr Lockyer's motion which had been ignored by the chair He did not see what good a meeting was, if the rate- payers had to have no voice in it. The Chairman : If you Lad any knowledge at all of the routine of this kind of thing, you Nvould know perfectly well that a Commissioner will be sent down to hold an inquiry and you, cc any other ratepayer, will have a perfect right to appear before him and state your objections. We do not intend to take a vote on this question. We never intended to do so. Mr. Jones That is all very well from your point of view, but it is no use locking the door when the horse is gone. We shall have to pay for that inquiry. Mr. Radcliffe again attempted to eddresa the meeting The Chairman You have epoken before and we cannot allow this to go on hour after hour. Yee have had your say. Mr. Radcliffe : This is not a public meeting then. I am willing to abide by the decision of the meeting as to whether I shell Is heard again. The Rector I move that Mr. Radcliffe he not heard again. VVe may go round and round and get no nearer the end.</p>
        <p>A counter-proplsition by a member of the audience that Mr. Radcliffe be allowed to speak, was loudl3 acclaimed.</p>
        <p>The Chairman : I, as chairman of this meeting, sili not allow you to s2eakiagkin. That is decided now. ,- Mr. Radcliffe ; Well, 1 think that concludes the bosinesi.</p>
        <p>Mr. Parker : No, I would like t ) move a vote of ensure on the Council for their way of conductive business,</p>
        <p>Mr. A. C. Smithson : It would be courteous to ask if Mr. Radcliffe wished to refer to points raised on his previous speeeh. The Chairman : Mr. Radcliffe has already Token and cannot address the meeting again. That Is my ruling. Mr. Radcliffe, you moat sit down. Mr. Radcliffe : Would you mind reading the notice convening the meeting ? (A Voice : "Let:somebody else have a chance "). Mr. Exert said be failed to see how the number of new consumers could have been sufficiently large to increase the revenue of the waterworks by one half during the last ten years. Voice : The asseesments must have gone up to bring that aborrte Mr T A rdern said the Council might have published some particulars of the scheme before asking the rate- payers, after half an hour's explanation, to support the expenditure. The Rector : But you are n.rt going to be asked. Mr. Brearley raid the ratepayers had returned the members of the Council to represent them. Vere not three gentlemen capable of going into the details of the scheme ? (A Voice: "They ought to be.") Could not the ratepayers trust them to deal with the matter ? He thought the ratepayers ought not to hesitate for one moment, but should give the Council their united approval. (Derisive laughter.) Even if they wanted to put 3d. or id. on the rates, he would not mind. Ile believed in the concerns which supplied the community with necessaries of life being under the control of the ratepayers. lie wished, honorer, to put in a plea on behalf of the workingmen. Ile did not want a boat house to be built on the lake-side to the detriment of workingmen who paid rates. Let them have boats as well, without baying to go to the expense of erecting a boathouse. Ile Council might provide locate and charge so much an hour. The Chairman ; If wo purchase the lake, we are not going to use it for purposes of that sort. We could not then deny anyone who came forward. Anyone who wishes to use the lake for boating must have a private boat and pay a licence for it Mr. Smithson: Is there any reasonable explanation of the fact that, in face of a declining population, the revenue of the waterworks has increased so much? The Chairman ; Is there a declining population? Mr. Smithson Yes. The Übalrman : Where do you take that from ? Mr. Smithson: The Rector will tell you all about it. The Chairman: You are only taking the ecclesias- tical district of Lymm, not the district of Lymm and Oughtrington. After a little further discussion, the Chairman decla-ed the meeting closed, no vote having been taken.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>PREMIER AND ARMAMENTS.</head>
        <p>PREMIER AND ARMAMENTS.</p>
        <p>Speaking at the annual dinner of the Central Association of Bankers and the Association of English County Bankers on Wednesday night, Mr. Asquith referred to his visit to the fleet at Weymouth, and said that the thought could not but occur whether the time might not come when the gigantic naval expenditure would be diverted into some fruitful and fertilising channels. At the same time, while the world remained in its prcsent temper, we could not neglect any means for making our countless interests secure. The maiz. tenance of our indisputable and invincible supl'epacy sea was the first and fundamental conditiuu not only of our prosperity and the integrity of the I.lmpire, but of the life of the Br4ish pe;spie,</p>
        <p>_ Daniel Henderson was tined ,E3O at Edinburgh on Tuesday for selling as "real Kerry tweed " cloth found to be " Irish " tweed made In Scotland.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>GREAT INSURANCE ACT.</head>
        <p>THE GREAT INSURANCE ACT.</p>
        <p>HOW IT AFFECTS EMPLOYERS.</p>
        <p>BY H. MAN FI ELD, M.P.</p>
        <p>By4he passing of the Insurance Act a new obligation has been placed on employers. They will have to bear part of the premiums in respect of the employees. How will this affect them? Is the new obligation a burden or is it not rather a benefit? As an employer I hold the latter opinion. It is not my province in this article to dis- cuss the advantages to the workman, but to deal only with those which I foresee for the employer. Putting the question at its highest, of conrse, the two things are related ; and in this respect, as in others, the interests of capital and labour identical. For the purpose of perfect plainness, however, I wish to put the question not at its highest, but at its lowest.</p>
        <p>At this level the case is susceptible of treatment in terms which must readily com- mend themselves to both employers and em- ployees. For this reason I confine my view of the results to be obtained from this bene- ficent measure only to pounds, shillings, and pence, and I restrict my illustra- tion of argement to mere plant or machinery. From the lowest standpoint, from the plain monetary and practical aspect, it eeems to me the centention that the employers will benefit under the National Insurance Act is some- thing more than demonstrabie. In this country the repair, maintenance, and, if possible, improvement of plant and machinery is n prime element of considera- tion with every up-to-date manufacturer, and he deems it best to spare no expense in its renewal or efficient maintenance. His con- tinuals aim is to keep it in the most perfect state possible. ,It is just here that the operation of this new Act comes into consideration. However efficient the machinery, there is still the human problem remaining. The skill ef human hands is necessary. This, indeed, is an integral part of the machinery, and, as such, the question of the human health and the human happiness upon ishich, after all, that skill depends, is of the utmost import- ance to employers—if I may so consider it for a moment from their own standpoint 0n1y.., The simple fact is that the health and happiness of his workpeople pay him. That, as I hope I have made it quite plain, is only putting it on the very lowest of grounds, and for the simplest of purposes, Ask any em- ployer if he will not get better results from a workman in perfect health than from one not in that condition—not physically fitted, that is to say, for going about his employ- ment—and what will the reply bet There can be no doubt on that point. Good health is indispensable to efficiency. There is something, moreover, which is in• dispensable, in any body of workpeople, be their good health. It is that not only ehonid thp in- dividual have health, but should feel he has security for it, He cannot long remain per- fectly .lisalthy if he is always worrying whether Ile is likely to remain so. Often— there is no use in denying it—he has had in the past good reason for such worry, and from- the point of view of the effect which the Insurance Act is going to have, there is no more important consideration than that of replacing this feeling by one of absolute security. That is just what the Act does, and, in doing so, its influence is twofold. In the individual it will prevent that apprehen- siveness, which is often worse than the ill- ness, and when he actually is ill it will pre- vent the necessity of his continuing at work, R 3 he at present so often has to do, knowing he has male no provision for being laid up for a while, From the employer's point of view, the presence in the workshop of a workman physically unfit for his work has a discouraging effect on his fellows, and he be- comes, to use the familiar illustration, the weak link in the chain of collective human efficiency. To remedy this .is.. therefore. in</p>
        <p>the intereet of the industry in 'lts 'entirety. It is thus that the Act confers its two-fold benefits.</p>
        <p>There are plain and practical as there are also sentimental reasons 'why the employer should pay his share of the premium under the Insurance Act. The scheme is as just and right as it is equitable. If insurance benefits the worker physically and the employer thereby derives an advantage, should he not bear a proportion of the cost of that insur- ance He has, in any event, to bear the ex- pense of keeping the plant and machinery of his factory in order. Should he not pay also some part of the expense of keeping fit and effective the human skill which is requi- site to that plant and machinery It is, after all, only a matter of sense, even more than of sentiment. The employer's share, be it re- membered, is only one-third of the cost of the insurance. It is this fact which makes it interesting to compare the experience in Ger- many. According to an extract relating to the iron and steel industry, which I take from the White Paper on German Insurance Legis- -Istion , I find it stated: " It cannot be denied that in many Cases the workman's feeling of confidence increases his productive power and his efficiency," and, " the employers willingly bear the costs which the Insurance Laws im- pose on them." Yet in Germany the total charges, given by Mr. Chiozz a Money's "A Nation Insured," vary from £6 Is. 70. a year per head for the Cologne Mining Com- pany to £,2 Bs. 2d. per head for the Vulkan Shipbuilding Company. The German em- ployer, however, is content to regard his con- tributions, as I have in effect regarded the matter here, as simply an establishment charge, and he has found, as I have prophe- sied will be the experience here, that in practice it pays this largely a consequence of increased efficiency. • If a good thing for the German manufacturer it will, a fortiori, be good for the British manufacturer. . . . . . _ _</p>
        <p>still keeping to the low ground I have selected, it is possible to carry the ease fur- ther. The fact is not to be lost eight of that The Act recognises the efforts of the good em- ployer. It penalises the employer who con- ducts his business under bad conditions. It renders him liable for execGsive sickness due to had conditions of his factory, and if he enjoys the luxury of cheap labour and low wages he pays the workman's contribution. In its entirety the argument is an intercon- nected series of economic causes and effects. 'lle ease may be summed up thus:</p>
        <p>Th 2 standard of comfort rises with edu- cation. The standard of wages rises with the standard of comfort. A high standard of comfort demands pro- vision for sickness. This demand can only be met by: _</p>
        <p>(1) Insurance. (2) A rise in wages sufficient to provide for the thriftless as well as the thrifty. To put it on no higher ground, the employer will in due eourse find insurance the cheaper alternative. In the last resort it would be itupossible, even if it were desired, to leave it an such ground. The ideal is one to which neither employer nor employee can be indif- ferent. It is national health. The interests of capital and labour are nowhere more identical than they are in this ideal. Here is a mighty national asset in which both must share. But recognising his simple duty in this respect the employer will have the addi- tional ftstisfaction of finding that in increas- ing the efficiency of his workmen he will be Increasing the efficiency of his business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>INDIGESTION</head>
        <p>INDIGESTION</p>
        <p>THE GREAT DIGESTIVE TONIC &amp; REGULATOR.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>Li-digestion is the cause of nearly all the minor ailments to which the human system is subject. When you have Indiges- tion—that is, when you cannot digest what you eat—your food cannot possibly make good blood, nor nourish your body. Consequently, your sys- tein is starved. You</p>
        <p>become weak, nervous, anmtnic ; impurities, due to decomposition of the undigested mass in the stomach and bowels, are drawn into your blood to poison your whole system and prepare the way for dangerous diseases.</p>
        <p>"For months," says Mr. T. Hamilton, xB, Upper Fox St., Leicester, "1 was bilious, con- stipated, unable to digest food. Eating used to produce pain across the chest and between the shoulders, flatulence, acidity, &amp;c. "By the time I had taken two bottles of Mother Seigel's Syrup all the distressing symp- toms bad disappeared and I was well again,"</p>
        <p>"When I was approaching middle age, I fell a victim to Indigestion and suffered all the inconvenience and pain of that terrible ailment. After trying many medicines without benefit, Mother Seigel's Syrup effected my complete cure."—Mrs. Gib- son, 6, Rycroft Place, Sale, Manchester.</p>
        <p>AND MAKES FOOD NOURISH YOU.</p>
        <p>The 216 bottle contains three timce es much ea the 1111 tize. - Sold also it) tablet form. Price 212.</p>
        <p>PREPARED FROM ROOTS, LEAVES AND DARKS.</p>
        <p>If you have any of the symptoms of Indigestion, such as pains after eating, biliousness, constipation, wind or headaches, take Mother Seigel's Syrup daily. It is a herbal remedy, made of roots, barks and leaves which act directly on the stom- ach, liver and bowels. As a digestive tonic and stomachic remedy it has no equal. From fifteen to thirty drops in water, taken after meals, banishes indigestion, sharpens the appetite, restores health and vitality. You can test it for one shilling or thereabouts.</p>
