        <head>OUR SERIAL STORY.</head>
        <p>OUR SERIAL STORY.</p>
        <p>(ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.]</p>
        <p>"1 :cm here on your business, Mr. Ten- nant !" "What! Scale! You!" Jaspar exclaimed. "What a wonderful disguise, man! I didn't know you in the least The detective laughed drily. "It would not be much use disguising my- self if you could see through it !' he replied. Then he went on, in a lower tone, "There was no need for you to follow the Chinaman. As it was, he saw you, and I got you off in time to give him a rest and imagine he was safe."</p>
        <p>"But I shall never get such a chance</p>
        <p>again !" Jaspar said. The detective "Yes, you will. One of my men has been shadowing him for days, we have him and his master shadowed day and night. But not by people who give the whole show away and frighten the bird !" he said drily. Jaspar flushed. "I'm sorry!' he said humbly, "but I've had one or two shocks. It Me tell yon all I have learnt to-day !" When he had finished Mr. Seafe nodded.</p>
        <p>"Yes, I practically knew all that before, but one has to be very sure of one's facts be- fore one does anything decisive. I think, however, that in a few days now we shall have all the evidence we need to arrest one or two people!" "Then there's the young girl, Lottie Bond !" Janpar said, "I meant to call on Mr. _Hawke and find out where she was, but the uight of that wretched Chinaman put every- thing else out of my mind!" Mr. Scafe's blue eyes glinten oddly. "Ah! I think we shall find her!" he uaid quietiv, "all NV.2 want is a little patience. This Mr. Steinway is living in a flat, in the Albany. I vill call on him to-morrow, not now, as I intended, and you had better ac- company me," he added. "Be at the Yard about ten."</p>
        <p>But the next morning neither Jaspar nor Mr. Scrife paid their intended visit to Paul Steinway. A telegram came for Jaspar from tho detective asking him to call on him at Scotland Yard. and when, he was shown into tne roi"NThich mr. catC nAtiaily occu- pied the detective rose to greet him with a grave face. "What has happened?" Jaspar said quickly. "Why are we not calling on Stein- wav r'— ""Because the devil always works on the side of evil," was the reply. "I ought, and intended, to have gone last night when I should have found Paul Steinway and taken his evidence. hut now it is too late."</p>
        <p>"Too late!" Jaspar repeated, "Why?" "Because Paul Steinway was found dead this morning, stabbed to ihe hoart !" the de- tective replied arimlv. CHAPTER XiVI. TH E SINKING slur.</p>
        <p>Isobel Frant was in her bed.' oorn having a</p>
        <p>solitary breakfast, or, rather, oying with a piece of filleted sole and taking •several sips of coffee from the cup by her side. Her handsome face looked pale an_ thoughtful, and in her eyes was a moody. •discontented expression. The entrance of her maid roused her from her reverie, and her face grew lighter at the sight of the letter and paper in the woman's hand. "Why didn't you bring them in sooner, Marte?'` she said petulantly, "I hate waiting for my letters!" The woman murmured something her mis- tress did not bother to listen to, and left the room again, glad to do so, for none of the servants in the house eared for their master's niece, who, since the marriage of his daugh- ter, had been practically the mistress of the house, Mr. Trains having lost all interest in the management of the domestic affairs after Stella had left the house. In the meantime Isobel had glanced through her correspondence hurriedly. There were several invitations to dinners and lun- ches, and the usual chatty letters from so- called friends who delighted to write all the disagreeable things they did not dare to say to people's faces. One letter in particular made the colour flood Isobel's face. It was from a Mrs. Cator, who " was the most in- veterate gossip in Society, and the paragraph that drew Isobel's brows together in a frown of displeasure was a reference to her cousin. • • • So Stella has broken with licr husband. At least, that is what people are saying. Of course, I don't know, but it is a foolish marriage altogether, for anyone could see that though he adored her to the point of insanity she did not care a button about him. Please do no repeat this, dear; it may be only idle gossip after all Isobel replaced the letter in its scented en- velope with a sarcastic smile. She knew well enough about the domestic affairs of her cousin, for she had set herself to watch from the beginning when everything had out- wardly been couleur de rose, and each glimpse she had had of Stella's white fragile face had been a source of bitter gladness to her. She had waited and hoped and plotted, with the help of Paul Steinway, to wreck through Clifford Hawke the happiness of the woman he had married, and as she had seen each blow strike home, and had wit- nessed the gradual dropping of all domestic pretence between Stella and her husband, Isobel's heart had been filled with an unholy _</p>
        <p>glee. It was some months since she had spoken to Stella, for her cousin had gone about prin- cipally with Lady Mary, who returned 'so- oel's hate most cordially, and since the day of the wedding, when her passionate Oititburst had startled and upset Stella, they had both avoided each other. But Isobel still waited for the day when she should see the public downfall of the man who had repulsed her advances so callously. She opened the paper and began to read the social news in a listless manner. Sud- denly she started from her lazy, indolent at- titude and gave a hoarse cry of amazement and horror. , "It can't be," she muttered, "it can't be! I must find out, I can't rest here till I am sure!''</p>
        <p>A. few minutes later her maid was startled by receiving oeremciory orders to get the</p>
        <p>gan. courtcouis:y.</p>
        <p>morning bath ready 'tor ner capricious mis- tress, and in less than the usual time Isobel was dressed to go out. She had chosen a black gown and hat, and her appearance was quite different from that of the smart society woman she usually was. She hurried out of the house, ignoring the footman's suggestion of a hansom, or the motor, and soon had walked swiftly away, her tall figure in its black attracting no attention. A few moments later she rang the bell of Clifford Hawke's house in Park-lane.</p>
        <p>The man who opened the door stared al her in amazement..</p>
        <p>"Is Mr. Hawke in? she said quickly. "Yes, madam," the man replied, "but I don't know if--" She brushed past him into the hall, "I am Miss Frant. Ask Mr. Ilawke if I can see him. Say I won't keep him long." As she waited for the servant fo return, Isobel glanced round the room he had shown her into. It was a small morning-rcom, and had an air of being seldom used by either the master or mistress. She turned suddenly rol the door opened, and a slight flush stained her pale face as she gazed into Clifford Hawke's calm, inscrutable eyes. "This is an unexpected pleasure," he be-</p>
        <p>TURKISH ISLAND CAPTURED BY ITALY.</p>
        <p>She flung out her hand with a suddenly im- patient gesture. "Don't! I can't bear platitudes at such a time as this! You know why I have come here at this unearthly hour to see a man who has never shown me the slightest regard since I have known him!" she went on, her haggard, burning eyes raking his face hungrily. He shook his head smilingly. "I am afraid not. however, I am sure you have a good reason as you have always had when honouring me with a visit, only," he added, "I hope you won't think me rude for asking you to be quick, because I am in the midst of packing!' Her eyes flashed suddenly. "Ah! Then I am in time," she said slowly. "I see you are running away!" He laughed. "Hardly 'running' away! Since I am going in my usual Way with lug- gage and servants. Have you a message for me?" he added. "I. suppose something out of the way brought you here?" Isobel broke into a hard little laugh. "Yes, ' something out of the way '!" she repeated ; then, leaning forward, she said, in a loud, tense voice:</p>
        <p>"Why do you try to fence with me?" she cried more quietly. "You must see that you are defeated now—and the death of Paul Steinway makes it worse for you "Why so?" he queried carelessly. "He was 'very much alive when I saw him yesterday, and—but this you apparently know, sines you were in his confidence; he seemed lese likely to die than anyone I ever saw !" "Then you admit having seen him, though at first, you denied it?" Isobel said quickly. "Why should I deny it, now that we an both so frank?" he retorted. "What have . to fear from you, though you were BO keen on hunting me down? My wife has left me," he went on, "and now other people arc. It's a sure sign of coming ruin when the rats leave the sinking ship !" Isobel leant forward eagerly; a sligh4 colour had come into her face.</p>
        <p>'There is yet time," she said slowly, "that is why I came to tell you to go while you can _</p>
        <p>"lon are very kind," he replied, "but why do you wish me to escape,' as you put it, supposing there is any necessity for such a thing?" "Because I know that you killed Paul Steinway," she answered in a low voice.</p>
        <p>"Paul Steinway is dead. He was murdered last night—and—oh, can't you see?" sho broke off passionately. "Are you mad? Don't you see what it means?" For a brief second the muscles round Clif- ford Hawke's mouth twitched, and the strong fingers that lightly balanced a fountain pen were seized with a sudden trembling. Then he glanced at Isobel's white, agitated face and laughed quietly. "It is very sad, certainly, but really I don't see where the particular meaning comes in. I also thought Paul Steinway was burnt in a fire," he added. "You know you are lying !". Isobel said coolly. "You and Paul Steinway met here yesterday. Ah ! you can't disguise your sur- prise," she cried, as he started violently. "You don't know that all these , months I have been in almost daily communication with Paul Steinway, and that I knew all ' his plans—when, as Mr. Smith,' he began his battle in the dark against you I hated you," she went on, the words coming from her lips in quick, hurried gasps. "I hated you 'be- cause you had flung my love in my face, and I helped Paul Steinway to find out every- thing, and I longed for your exposure, as much as he did !" She paused and glanced at the man's 'im- passive face. It had changed colour slightly during hcr speech, and in his deep-set eyes there gleamed an expression of curiosity, "Since you have been the friend of Paul Steinway so long, why do you come here to me?'' he said slowly. "I also knew that you and he had met—but I did not think you were so actively interested as to take up his cause so eagerly. I suppose your visit here, then, is to denounce me in some way !"</p>
        <p>VJIy CIO yOU precc.:a TO meT butelv that attitude need not be kept up! I thought if I came to tell you that you are in danger that you would understand! Because you are a ' sinking ship ' I am not like the other rats— would gladly share your disgrace—your limes even do not turn me from you "Yet you could betray and talk me over with Steinway," he said slowly. "You are inconsistent, Miss Frant!" "Because I was mad—mad with mortifica• tion because you ignored me for Stella!" she went on, "but surely you believe that my ob• ject in coming here this morning was to warn you to go, and to tell you that in spite of everything I still eared, and would rather share your exile and degradation than an- other man's hononr and fame!"</p>
        <p>"You are very kind," he said quietly, "but I would not let anyone share what is coming to me—not even the woman. I love, and who, though our lives have drifted apart, is still my wife whom I worship." "And who hates you and will be glad to be free from you," Isobel flashed contemptu• ously, "do you think that Stella—that cold, icy model of convention—would put her arms round your neck and say Clifford, I love you, and not one of your evil deeds can alienate my love?' No, you fool! she will recoil in horror when she knows how red your hands are with blood!" "And, strange as it may sound in your ears," he replied gently, and with a certain dignity that acted on her passion like a grip of iron might on a runaway horse, "it is her very coldness and iciness that make me love my wife as I have never loved any other woman in my life, for it puts her, in her purity and innocence, as far above my reach as a star is from the gutter !" "I suppose that type of woman would ap- peal to a man with your past!" she retorted sullenly, "anyway, I am the only person who gave you a thought—l—" she paused suddenly. A curious insistent peal rang through the house, and it was repeated al- most at once while a loud knocking began at the same time.</p>
        <p>Isobel glanced nervously at Clifford Hawke. Ms face had grown a little pale, and his figure stiffened with expectation. "Who is that?" she said quickly, "why don't the servants answer the bell?"</p>
        <p>—lfe smiled. "Because my instructions were that they waited five minutes. There are only two servants in the house, the others were paid off and told to go. I think," he added, "that you did rather an awkward thing in coming here, Miss Frant, I am sorry." Her cheeks flushed. "I am not. At least, though you don't care about me, you will not be alone—now !" A gleam of admiration shone in his face for her almost superb courage at an hout when cowardice would be excused in any woman. "Thank you," he said quietly, "I really - appreciate your action! His voice was drowned by the renewed ringing and knocking, and instinctively Iso- bel covered her cars. - -</p>
