      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>BID FOR A BRIDE</head>
        <p>A BID FOR A BRIDE</p>
        <p>BLANCHE EARDLEY, Author a " Kitty Bell —Actress," "The Lady Killer," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>CHAPTift. XXV. MR. SCAFE STOPS A CEASE. After the astounding discovery he had made as he had watched Paul Steinway go into Clifford Hawke's house, Jaspar had been dazed with this new knowledge that had been thrust upon him. He had learnt in a second what he had given up years of valuable work to accomplish—namely, that the man who had married and then cruelly deserted his sister was none other than Clifford Hawke, the financier. As he thought of it while he strolled up and down Park-lane he began to piece together all the little details that mada ap-his acquaintance with Clifford Hawke, and which, in the new knowledge he had, were all witnesses for the truth. There was the Show Sunday he bad given at his studio, and at which Clifford Hawke had shown such sudden confusion. Then there was that other day-- the day that he had lost his self-control, and had first told Stella of his love—and when Clifford Hawke had suddenly appeared and taken her away. It was then that he had missed all his papers concerning his sister's marriage and life with her husband, and which he had mentioned in the presence of Clifford Hawke. It was all so clear to him now that he held the clue in his band, and at first he could think of nothing else save that this man had come into the lives of two women who had different claims upon his - love, the first his sister, and the seconl -- Stella! As he stood moodily watching the white gate of Clifford Ilawke's house,. hoping to see Paul Steinway come out so that he could go in and tackle Hawke, not only on his own ac- count, but Lottic's too, he gave a sudden start, and shrank back behind the pillars of a doorway. Slinking up the steps of Clifford Hawke's servants' entrance was the striking figure of a Chinaman, who stood for a moment watching the traffic as it poured down Park- lane, then crossed the road and entered the Park. For a second Jaspar stood petrified with amazement, then he almost jeopardised his life by springing forward in front of a motor- car, and brought down upon his head the candid opinion of a cabman who had pulled up just in time. , He reached the other side of the road and entered the Park, keeping the Oriental's figure in view. His heart was beating with excitement and wonder, for he. had never ex- pected such luck as to again come face to face with the Chinaman who had followed him that day on the 'bus, and whom he could swear he had seen the clay he had gone to the house in the East End from which the bogus telegram from Lottie had been sent. What could the man want with Clifford Hawke unless he was an accomplice, he wondered as he followed his quarry through the winding paths of the Park, until they reached Albert Gate, and then out on to the roadway again. No matter how tired-he was, he meant to trick him to his lair and wring the truth out of him, for the Oriental was either ti, paid servant of Clifford Hawke, or an ally, and the time had some for some decisive action, for who knew to what desperate measures Clifford Hawke might not stoop wizen he realised that he was found out? Suddenly, owing to a stop in the traffic, the Chinaman paused and glanced behind him, and Jaspar knew that he was right, for as their eyes met his face grew a sickly green and into his long, narrow eyes there leapt a look ,3f abject terror. The next moment he had dashed into the crowd, and Jaspar, all his nerves tingling with the strain, broke into a run that drew curious glances from the passers-by, and one or two suspicious ones from a policeman. In another few seconds he had almost caught up with the Chinaman, who now .was disre- garding appearances and winding in and out of the crowds with the sinuous stealth of an animal, when suddenly Jaspar felt his arm clutched in a vice-like grip, and turning ronnd impatiently to see who it was that had impeded his chase, he saw a slim, insignifi- cant-looking man of the artisan class with a stubby beard and a muffler round his neck. On his back he carried a bag of tools. "What the dickens—" Jaspar began angrily, then paused in perplexity. In spite of the man's ragged attire there was some- thing oddly familiar about the fiat blue eyes that shone with such peculiar intelligence from beneath the shadow of the cap he had . pulled over his brow. "What do you want?" he said more quietly, "you are stopping me from an im- portant chase!" The ghost of a smile flitted across the man's. features. "That's all right, giiv'nor!" he said in a wheezy voice. "If you'll drop. into this pub I'll tell von something just as important over a glass of four ale!" Woticieringly, and with a shrug of his shoulders since he had lost , sight of the Chinaman, Jaspar followed his strange com- panion into the bar of a public-house, and when they had been served with their drinks by an indolent-looking barmaid, he said abruptly: "Well, what is it? I hope this is no fool's errand you dragged me in here for The man raised his glass. "Ere's 'ealth, gliv'nor," he said hoarsely. Then, after a swift glance round the almost empty bar he leant forward and in a diffe- rent t 0n.2 said quietly •</p>
        <p>WOUND. The secret of happiness. It is health. So bear in mind that Steedman* Powders correct the slight stomach troubles which maks children so fretful, and their parents depressed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>DOAN'S INVESTIGATIONS CONTINUED.</head>
        <p>DOAN'S INVESTIGATIONS CONTINUED.</p>
        <p>Warrington Woman Comes Forward.</p>
        <p>Our recent announcement, that we were arranging to conduct an engtiry into Doan' numerous heal cases, has met with the widespread appreciation of our readers. To-day a well-known Wei rington resident comes f irward and gives her personal experi- ence, which she authorises us to publish for the benefit of °theta. Mrs. M Reny, of 2, Oxford-street, Latchford, War- rington, says :—" I began to suffer from kidney complaint, some years ago, and I had dreadful pains in the small of my hack. When I bent down I had great difficulty in getting upright again. Often I had to take hold of something to help me. There were other signs that my kidneys were not acting properly. "Being told of Doan's backache kidney pills and ti e good they had done others, I decided to try the medicine. "I took four boxes in all, and Jean honestly say they have done me more good than anything else I ever used. I have been much better in every way since taking Doan's pills. (Signed) (Mrs.) M. HEAY," Over 5 years aftcr her cure, Mrs. Beay said :—"I am still keeping very well. Dean's pills did me great good and I have often recommended them. lam glad to say my cure has been a permanent one." 2s. 9d. a box, fl boxes 13s. 9d. ; of all dealers. Ask for DOAN'S backache kidney pills, like Mrs. Reny had.</p>
