        <p>Padgate.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF THE POSTMISTRESS.</p>
        <p>Photo Burr, HE LATE MRS. HORRIDGE.</p>
        <p>We regret o have to report the decease of another Padgatonian in the person of Mrs Horridge, ho for the past 30 years hal held the responsible position of post- mistress. A daughter of the late Thomas Daintith,Mrs Horridge vas born in Padgate 613 ears ago and except for a few years spent at Winwick during the lifetime of her first husband she bad always resided in the village. For the past two and half yerrs her bealth had been very unsatisfactory and the end came peacefully in the early hours of Tuesday. Her second husband and eight daughters (three of whom are in Canada) survive her. The funeral rakes place at Winwick Church to-day.</p>
        <p>MAYFLOWER BAAAAR.—As advertised in our last issue, the above bazaar si as continued on Saturday evening in the Wesleyan schoolroom. The opening ceremony was performed by children. Master A Gordon Bennett, who presided, rnaJe a brief speech and called upon Miss Gwen Bennett to declare the bazaar open. This she did in a speech corny osed in rhyme, the elf et of which was to call forth much applause. Master Stanley Proffitt in the name of those present thanked the openers for their presence and the effective way in which they had carried out their duties. At the close the Secretary announced the total receipts for the three days as £4lO is. 63. It was also mentioned that several pounds had not yet come in, and will be added to the above sum.</p>
        <p>FUNERAL OF Miss. WRIC lIT.—An imbressive demonst•a.ien of respect was witnessed at the funeral of the late Mrs Arthur Wright, matron of the Cottage Homes, which took place at Christ Church on Saturday afternoon. The cortege was headed by the scholars of the institution and numerous representatives of the Board of Guardfsni and staff also attended to pay air t tribute to an esteemed official. The Vicar titev G W Dickinson) officiated in the chnrch and at the graveside. The chit'? mourners were:—Mr Arthur Wright (husband aud superintendent of the Homes), Mrs Drover (sister), Mr George Cole (brother), Mr Stctt (brother-in-law, Birkenhead, repreeenting tle Liverpool and District Poor Law Officers' Association) Mrs Stott (sister), Mr A Thorne (brother-in-law), Messrs Joseph Wright, James Wright and Ilsnry Wright (brothers,n• law), Mr and Mrs John Platt (brother-in-law and sister-in-law), Mrs Wilkinson (Blackpool, sister-in-law), Mr Herbert Wright, Mr J Wright turt Mr Percy Drover (nephews), and Nurse Kane (Colne). Among others present in the church were Dr Hodgkinscn (deceased's medical attendant) and Mrs Hodgkinson, Messrs William Houghton and John Backhouse (Gu=rdiarns), Mr A Bottomley (clerk to the Board of Guardians), Mr It W Peaaock (assistant clerk), Mr Moss (acting master at the Workhouse), Mr Basil Shearer (relieving and inquiry o nicer), Mr F IT Ankland (relieving officer), Mrs Furness (a member of the Ladies' Visiting Committee at th 3 Cottige Hr mesl, Mr T J Lee, Mr Alfred Jolley, Mr Scotson, Mr Rol-eft Lunt, Mr F Blackwell, Mr and Mrs Joseph W Houghton, Mr T Welsby, Mr 13 Randall, Mrs W Monks, Mr and Mrs Bowl, Mr George Righey, Mr Joseph Pomfrey, Mr F Young, Mr French, Mr T liccleston, Mrs Constable (portress at the Workhouse), Mr Clare (labour master), and the teachers and staff of the Cottage Homes. At the close of the last rites each girl from the Homes threw a small bunch of wild hyacinths (kindly presented by Mr William Houghton) on the coffin, as a last token of respect to the deceased matron. Many beautiful wreaths were Ben. The funeral arrangements were carried out by Mr H Greenough, of Warrington.</p>
