      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>HOLY CROSS CHURCH. GOLDEN JUBILEE CELEBRATED.</p>
        <p>The golden jubilee of the opening of the Holy CfCS3 Roman Catholic, Church was celebrated on Sunday at St. Helens by a series of notable services and gather- ings almost unprecedented in the annals of the town, which were attended by the Archbishop of Liverpool (Dr. Whiteside) and the Right Rev. Dr. Brindle, D. 5.0., Bishop of Nottingham. In the morning Pontifical High Mass was celebrated by the Archbishop assisted by Dean O'Sulliven, presbyter assistance ; Father Hearne and Father Hayden, S.J.;deacon and sub-deacon; Father Ward and Dr Bennett eeacons at the throne ; Archbishop's M.C.,yather Morris ; MO of the Mass, Father Sergeant ; and Father Crook, chaplain to the Bishop of Nottingham. The choir sang Sewell's setting of the Mass most beautifally, Mr J Barr, the choir- master, taking the orgrn, and having the assistance of Mr A Coops, choirmaster of Sacred Heart. Bishop Brindle pres.ched an earnest and devotional sermon from the words, "1 was glad when they said unto me we will go into the House of the Lord.' In the afternoon, when it was announced that the Archbishop would hold a reception at the Town Hall, a crowd of close on 3,00 e filled the hall and all its approaches. 'The Archbishop and the Bishop were ' escorted to the hall by the Scouts and Bugle Band from the Gerard Hall,. and they both were most , enthusiastically received. Dr. Brindle wore his D. 5.0., the Order of St. Isabella the Catholic, conferred on , him by King Alfonso of Spain, the order conferred on him by the Sultan, and other remembrances of his t service in the Army. said they could pride themselves that Fathernt y- fi vße•a3l-dewa sn' St. Helens held the first place with regard to its Catholizity of any town in England. They had more than one-thi.d of the plantation belonging t ) their church, and Preston and other Catholic towns could not say quite so much. Archbishop Whiteside, in response to a most enthusiastic greeting, said they were still prepared to fight tooth and nail to kill any attempts that were made on their schools. He thought he heard distant rumblings of battle, and they did not know what might come on them in the course of ano'her twelve months. But what he did know—aad he had his finger on the pulse of the Catholics of Lanxishire—was that they were ready for the battle and their powder was dry. (Cheers). Dr. Brindle referring to the Boy Scouts, said he knew some parents did not like that movement, because they thought it would result in their b 035 going soldiering but not more than sor 10 per cent, of the lads ever became soldiers. General Baden-Powell was not think- ing of making them soldiers when he started the move- ment. He wanted to do something tbat would ensure that the boys had an honourable character and career. The principle that was'instilled into the lads from first to last was that they should be in all things honour- able, and that a day was lost unless they could say at the end that they had done Borne good aotion for some- one else. _ . .</p>
        <p>The assembly were then dismissed with the Arch- bishop's blessing. In the everting tho Archbishop officiated Benediction, and en eloquent sermon was preached by the Provincial, the Rev. J. Browne, S.J ,of Manchester</p>
        <p>The Workhouse Master reported that there were two adults and four children in the House who would le sailable candidtel for such Homes. Mr. Cooke, relieving officer for Runcara district, reported one juvenile case. Mr. De Prez, the Budworth relieving officer, stated, he had no cases, and Mr J Pearson, relieving officer,Frodsham district, reported three cases. Mr Woodward said he was one of the committee who attended the Conference. Instructors would be at th 6 places to teach the children and cultivate them as far as was possible. The charga of 103 per week covered expensts. The Tarvin Workhouse was something similar to the one at Lunesda'o. fa the opinion of the committee it was absolutely necessary that Boards of Guardians themselves should take some steps in the matter of dealing with feeble-minded parsons who came under their care. If they did not, it would probably cost tem far more if anyone -le took the matter up He moved that the Board approve of the scheme. Mr Antwiss seconded, . _</p>
        <p>Mr. Morr s disagreed with M. Woodward. Why should they transfer cas(s from ore Workhouse to another? It was in the same category. It was certain to his mind that the officers of that House would be relieved of certain duties. If the Tarvin Union was capable of dealing with feeble minded oases then he would say their's was. (" NP, No.") Mr. Linaker : You missed a point about that. Mr. Wcodward : I will answer it. _ .</p>
        <p>Continuing, Mr. Morris said they had Children's Homes to tackle and be thought they were capable of dealing with the oases themselves. Tile), bad a number of items of expenditure ahead of them. Mr. Woodward held that in their instituti n ti ay bad no provision for the treatment of such cases. (Dear, bear.) It was not proposed that they should be trans- ferred to Tarvin and Luneedttle as inmates of a work- house. The two places were to be set apart for the accommodation and instruction of the feeble minded. Every case would be taken there for the instruction of the children. The probability was that those two institu- tions would become established for dealing only with the feeble minded. That, he was convinced, was one of the greatest necessities of their administration. They had not been able to do anything for them in the past The institutions would be set apart solely for the patients. (Applause). Mr. Norman said that ever since he became a memi*er of the Board that question had been a burniug one. De was very much surprised that now they had that oppor- tunity of having the patients dealt with, views should be raised trying to deter from dealing with the matter. There were some very diffic 1t caves—cases which were not bad enough for an asylum and were left on their hands. They ought to be glad thPy bad the opportunity of having the cas a dealt with. (Haar, hear.) Mr. Morris said he was end, r the impression that the crates were to be transferred to two workhouses and in that case thought that they cou'd just es well have dealt with them thernseive; Mr. Pilling said he heartry agreed with Vie sugges- tion, and sea mnuber of the committee along with Mr. Woodward be thought that gear lemon had explained the position very lucidly. (Hear, hear). Islr Hayes said the two main points emphasized at the conference were the matter of detention and classifl a- Hon. Classifioati n was essential in cas-s of that kind. Continuing, to said they Lad a patent now and by send- ing to the institutioa would save 4s. leause the charge at the Royal Albert Asylum was lie, whereas the charge at the Tarvin institution was only 10s. A report on the question of the feeble-roitded recently issued stated that there were pearly 1,000 such cases in the North We, tern area. The Board uranimensly approved of the rcherne. RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL. The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural Dis- trict Council was held at Dutton on Tuesday, Mr Linaker presiding.</p>
        <p>CLOSING OF STAG BRIDGE. A letter was read from the Manchester Ship Canal re the closing to vehicular traffic of the Stag Bridge, on the Chester-road from May G,h to June 15th for altera- tions to be made. It was clearly urderstood that the bridge was only temporarily closed against at h'cular traffic. THOSE BoNFS ! The Shrews'lury superintendent of the London and North-Western and Great-Western Joint Railways wrote stating that inqu'ries had been made re the alleged bone nuisance. Ile contended that no avoidable nuisance arose on the data mentioned by Dr Salby. The bones were sent from Frodstuun ta tielsby by the earliest train possible, and that tha small from the bones was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <p>CHEAPER GAS.</p>
        <p>The St. Helens Gs Committee decided on Wednesday to reduce the price of gas in the borough by twopence per 1,000 feet. The net profits for last year were £11,500. The price of gas for some years has been 2s 2d per 1,000 cubic feet, and Is Si for power purposes. Meters and gas stoves are supplied free to customers 1 and the price, with the exception of Widnes, is much' lower than any other borough in the country. The Committee decided to give £5,000 of their proflts to the relief of the rates.</p>
        <p>SUNDAY CINEMATOGRAPHS BANNED. ST. HELENS WATCII COMMITTEE'S DECISION. The St. Helens Watch Committee dealt on Monday with the question of Sunday cinematograph shows, which had been deferred a month ago to get informa- tion from other towns, Mr Maxwell, barrister, asked first for a licence for a ' building to be erected in Heath-street. The committee had before them a strenuous objection from the Rev Father Carr, of St. Austin's Church, which is close to the site of the proposed picturedrume, and petitions from most of the Catholic congregations of the town in support of the objection. Mr Maxwell, however, said that all that they asked for was a s'x days' licence without Sunday performances, and this the committee granted. Similar six days' licences were granted for new picturedromes in Thatto Heath-r cad, Boundary-road, and Willmer-street, Sutton. The Town Clerk (Mr Andrew) submitted replies from ninety-three towns on the matter. In sixty-four tovms Sunday performances were absolutely prohibitei ; in six towns they were prohibited with occasional per- missions ; in fourteen they were not prohibited, but Sunday performanees were very infrequent ; while in only eight towns were Sunday shows regularly given. Th. committee decided by a large majority to pro- hibit Sunday performances altogther in St. Helens. This decision will apply to the five picturedromes which have been giving Sunday shows for some time past, and against ahicli the St Helens Church Council had sent petitions opposing their continuance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <p>not anything like what was suggested. The best wa being done to avoid complaints but it would appear that complaints were being made quite unnecessarily. Mr Linaker (junior) said he did not know however they could say unnecessary complaints had been made. He met the bones as often as anyone and knew what the nuisance was. Mr. Woodward : Cm they talk ? (Laughter) Mr. Linakar : No they cry. (Renewed laughter). Mr. Woodward moved that the Council were crn- vinced that complaints in regard to the nuissnce had been quite justified, also that the meant to see that the nuisarce was abated and if it was not forthwith abated further acticn would be taken. The resolution was carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>CORONATION EXPENSES.</head>
        <p>CORONATION EXPENSES.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a letter from the Local Government Bard stating that they bad considered the appeal of the Council against the decision of Mr Adams, district audi•ou, in allowing the sums of £29 and .£2 175 as charges in the general expenses accounts after transfer thereto from the teparate parochial accounts of the contributory places of Aston and Aston Grange respectively. The sums in question represent expendi- ture in connection with the public celebration of the Coronation of his Majesty. The Board were of the opinion that the case was one which might probably be decided upon the merits in the exercise of the power conferred on them by Section 4 of the Poor Law Audit Act 1848. They accordingly decided that the sums in question should be transferred to the separate parochial accounts of the two places. This matter was left in the hands of the two parochial committees.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>CHESHIRE BRINE BILL.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE BRINE BILL.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported that at a meeting on the 4th inst. of the Brine Pumping Bill Committee, it was reported that agents had written him that the Bill was likely to be in Committee of the Luse of L)rds early next meek and that a consultation of all opp-nents of the Bill was eugges,ed by the Salt Union Limited, to tike place on Monday, May 6th. It was resolved that the clerk attend I hi consultation with the agents End Counsel; that Messrs Linaker, sen., and Woodward attend before the Committee of the House of Lords to give evidence on behalf of the Council ; that the other members of the committee hold them- selves in readiness to go up to London in the event of their services b&amp;ing required. The Clerk also reported having briefed Mr. Blennerbasset, K.C. The action of the committee was confirmed and it was stated by Mr. Linaker that the meeting had been post- poned and he could not say definitely when it would be held.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL. The monthly meetirg of the Newton District Council was held at the Earlestown Town Hall on Monday, Mr' W Thompson presidrd and there were also present : Messrs C B F Borron, E T Evans, Dr W Latham, W Roughley, J Sankey, W Davies, .1 Higham, E L Bent W Goal n, with C Cole (clerk), A Bowes (surveyor) and R T Surtees (engineer).—A letter was read from Mr W Longton 'toting that as he had no horses suitable for the work, he desired to give up the contract for horsing the Fire Brigade.—lt WRS resolved that Mr Longton's contraA be determined, and that Mr H Walton's tender for supplying horses for the flre engine be accepted.— A letter was read from the Provincial Road Car Co. Ltd„ applying for licences to run motor omnibuses within the district, and it was resolved that the mater be referred to the chairman, clerk and surveyor</p>
        <p>" You mnst not endanger several lives for the sake of one," said the Brentford magistrate to a chauffeur wio said that he was travelling at high speed to fetch a medical man to attend his employer, who was da gerously ill.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>AT THE RACES.</head>
        <p>AT THE RACES.</p>
        <p>- --- The police haul on Cup day at Chester Races was represented by ten cases betore the magistrates on Thursday. Li the case of Arthur Slade, a London clerk, who received a month for frequenting the racecourse, Inspector Hodgkiss, of Warrington, said a gentleman reported Lavin',b his purse and £8 stolen, but com- pl.inunt took it from the thief whom he pointed out to witness. Prisoner ran away, but witness captured him. -- Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>MOND A Y.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Alderman Bolton and Messrs A J Jolley, W Furness, J W Haywood, Pilling and T Locker. STRUCK WITH A BOTTLE.—Patrick Byrne, 9, Hill Street, was charged on remand with unlawfully wound- ing Patrick Lavell on Saturday evening, April 27th. The Chief Constable stated that at 10.35 on the Saturday night the prisoner went to the lodging-house No. '7, Hill-street. under the influence of drink. Ho bad some bother with Lavell afid went hack to No. 9 where he said it was his intention "to do "for someone before 12 o'cicck. He piokcd a quart bottle off the table and struck complainant across the nose whilst he ly asleep on the sobt.The injury h:cl to be treated at the Infirmary Mr Browne defended and called witnesses to prove that the two men were on the best of tern's and Fad had no bather of any description. The prisoner had been struck on the head just previous to the assault by his wife and that had prabably upset him. Tne prisoner bad a good character at work.—A floe of 40s. and cost was imposed. TUESDAY.—Before Councillor Henry Roberts (pre. siding), Councillor C Jackson Holmes and Mr T Locker.</p>
        <p>.1 BROTHER'S Ass kI.TLT ON SISTER —Thomas Quinn, of Refuge-square, liewsey, was charged on a warrant with having assaulted Lis sister, Mrs Annie Kimbljp, of the same address, on Sunday night.—The prosecutrix, whose eyes were discolourel, said that her child and her sister's child had been falling out, and when she interfered her brother seized her by the throat aud savagely hit her with a brush.—A neighbour said she saw the pi honer knock his sister down with the brush. —P.i3oner denied the assault and called his mother to support his statements, which ware to the effect that the husband of the prosecutrix had assaulted Mrs Kmblin.—Quinn, who was described by the Chief Con- stable as a man of the most brutal type, was lined El 163 including costs, or in 'de`ault one month imprisanment. WEDNESDAY.—Befcre Messrs C White and other tragist rates.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED THEFT OF GYMNASTIC APrLIANCES.—John Sma,t, 23, 11,53 le-street, was charged with Freaking into I. pav lion at the rear of 188 Winwick-road, and stealing a par of Sandow developers, a set of boxing gloves and four rairs of pump; valued at 303. the property of John Holiday.—lnspector Williamson, in asking for a remand, said the prosecutor used the structure as a gymnasium and on going there on Tuesday found it had been broken open and the articles were missing. The police went to prisoner's house and be sold he was the man they were after. He s sid four of them were con- cerned in the theft and that they were drunk at tho trrne.—The case was remanded for a week, tail of £5 being allowed.</p>
        <p>THURSDAV.—Before Mr A ;.1. Julley (in the chair), De Naden, fessrs Wft Marts, J Upson, E Woods and R chardson, HEAVY SENTENCE FOR WINDOW SMASIIER.--AlphßS Cohen, of no axed abode, was charged on remand with being drunk and wilfully breaking windows at the Lion Hotel, Bridge-street to the value of .£lO on May Ert and els) with assaulting Samuel Worthington.— Samuel Worthington ,manager of the Lion Hotel vaults, said that about 9.15 on the night in question the prisoner entered the vaults and, noticing that he had had enough drirk, v itness refused to serve him. He</p>
        <p>n the -1 : 011</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON OFFICER'S CAPTURE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <p>INSTANT RELIEF RAPID CURE GOUT AND RHEUMATISM MADE'S PILLS.</p>
        <p>SUFFERED 19 YEARS ! !</p>
        <p>4, Pool Road, Smethwick, March 26th, 19l I. Dear Sir,—l must give you praise and congratula- tion upon your wonderful remedy ; Eade's Gout and Rheumatic Pills.</p>
        <p>I have SUFFERED FOR THE LAST NINE- TEEN YEARS UNTOLD PAIN and agony and have taken dozens of bottles of doctors' medicine, but NOTHING HAS GIVEN ME SO MUCH. RELIEF AS YOUR PILLS! _ . _ .</p>
        <p>I shall advise all sufferers that I know to try them, I know it will be for their own good. You may make what use you like of this letter for the truth requires no study. I remain, yours faithfully, CHAS. FLE rCHER.</p>
        <p>EADE'S GOUT PILLS Are perfectly safe in their action, eliminate all injurious matter, and are highly restorative. Sold everywhere in Bottles, is. lid. and 2s. 9d. or sent post free for stamps or P.O. by the propriet GEORGE EADE, 232, Goswell road, London _ --</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <p>put the prisoner out in Bridge-street and about five minutes afterwards he herd glass being smashed. On going out he saw one of the windows in Bridge. street broken. The man threw more stones through the windows and they passed over people's heade. In closing with the prisoner be sustained a black eye, but he held him until the arrival of P.C. Porter.—The evidence of Arthur Ditchfield, plumber, 90, Chester-road, was to the effect that the windows were expensive embossed ones and the damage would be estimated at about £IO.—P.C. Porter said that when he visited the spot, the manager was struggling with the prisoner and he was bleeding on the face.—Prisoner: Did you see me bleeding ?—No, neither did you make any complaint that Mr. Worthington had hit you.— Prisoner said he went in the house to see a friend and Mr. Worthington got over the counter and struck him in the face. The prosecutor had called the assistance of his barman and they pitched him out in the street as if ho were a dog. What he did was through aggravation.—The prisoner was sent to prison for four months with bard labour.</p>
        <p>ASSAULT CHARGE DISMISSED.—James McMahon was charged on remand with having been found on the enclosed premises of 6, Dutton-street, and also with having assaulted a little girl named Burke, of 6, Dutton- street—lnspector Williamson said it appeared Mrs Burke went out about 9 10 on Saturday and left four children in the house. The prisoner, it was alleged, came in the house and gave a little girl a penny to keep his visit quiet. The mother returned about 10.30 and found the prisoner endeavouring to get out of the back door, but there was no thoroughfare. She got hold of the prisoner and put him out through the front door. Later on she found the prisoner in the Borough Arms and brought him out. He gave no explanation as to how he came to be in the house and denied both charges. In answer to the first charge be said "It is altogether wrong," and in reply to the charge of assault said " I know nothing whatever about it." 'Evidence was given by Agnes Burke (8) to the effect that the prisoner had given her a penny and had gone upstairs where the other girl aged six was in bed. She heard her sister shout out. Miss Burke gave evidence in support of the Inspector's opening statement and otter evidence baying been given for the prosecution, Mr Browne, who defended, called Mahon into the witr ess box. Mahon said be had been to a picture , palace up to 9 o'clock on the night in question and after that went in a public house at Bridge Foot. Ile did not go to Dutton-street, in fact be did not know where it was. When Mrs Burke struck him at the Borough Arms he wanted a policeman to come along because he thought it was a case for him instead of for the prosecutors.Mr Browne submitted that there was no case of identification for the street was very narrow and the witnesses might easily have been mistaken. He suggested it was a case "Not proven."—The magis- trates retired and on their return announced that the case would be dismissed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>LINER ASHORE NEAR VALPARAISO.</head>
        <p>LINER ASHORE NEAR VALPARAISO.</p>
        <p>A telegram from Valparaiso says that the steamer OraYia has gone ashore in a fog eight miles west of that port. Assistance has been sent. The Oravia's passengers include Mr Lowther, the British Minister to Chile.</p>
      </div>
