      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>HEALTH WEEK. A National Summary.</head>
        <p>HEALTH WEEK. A National Summary.</p>
        <p>It is clear that the National Health Week experi- trent was fully justified. some of the leading towns had scarcely time to organize with sufficient credit to themselves and therefore deferred celebrations until another year. Others again like Manctester and Liver- pool are reserving themselves for special campaigns on tuberculosis. The celebrations were often most elaborate in the most healthy and well-governed towns. Letchworth, the first garden city for example, made full use of its opportunities and pressed into its service its ablest citizens. Halifax entered into the scheme with enthusiasm. Some of the schools there are ventilated by a special mechanical process nd the public were invited to impect thest and to see also swimming dis- plays at four of the public schools possessing baths. Other local features were exhibitions of morris dancing organised games and breathing exercises. About 25 meetings for women were held during the week. Burton-on-Trent bad an ambitious programme carried through by an existing Health Society. The Amalga- mated Society of Railway Servants discussed the health question as d passed a special resolution on the relation of the living wage to the quality of life. At Oxford some of the civic sanitary institutions were on show and visits were also paid by the public to dairies, breweries, bakeries, laundries, flour mills and marmalade factories. Berkhampsted went in for Health Talks to Women a. lid. started a_ creche where mothers could leave their _</p>
        <p>babies, while listening to the lecturettes. The Poole Mothers' Association could not induce the municipality to move,so they took a shop and exhibited hygienic baby linen, feeding bottles, banana crate cradles, &amp;c. Crowds surged round the window. St. Joseph's Industrial School, Manchester, bad a special Health Week all to itself. Four hundred boys between the ages ol 7 and 16 were thus touched.</p>
        <p>A FATAL WEDDING. A fortnight ago, Mr J W Willis, auctioneer and surveyor, of Kingston-on-Thames, was married at Teddingtbn. Three days later he died suddenly whilst on his honeymoon at Southend. Last week the young widow sailed for Canada. On Tuesday the death took place of a well-known and highly-respected Kingston gentleman, who was ODO of the guests at Mr. Willis's wedding, and who was ore of those who saw the young married couple off on their honeymoon. Death followed a chill contracted op the wedding day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <p>"</p>
        <p>~ .</p>
        <p>"</p>
        <p>.0 I</p>
        <p>NOM AtESPON LABOR 03INIA VINCIT —We shall be pleased to nublish your reflections on the Health Week in our nest 13E130.—En. "Ex."</p>
        <p>A Sanitary Suggestion. you kindly allow me to heartily support the suggestion made by "A Ratepayer" last week, to the effect tha', during the 'summer months, the pail closets in the town should be emptied more frequently than once a week. If the cost of emptying the pails twice a week is too serious a matter for the authorities to face, may I suggest that a disinfectant should be freely sprinkled in the closets at least twice during each week. When we remember that the back passages are very often the only available places for the young children to play in, surely it is worth while to take every reasonable precaution to see that the surroundings are as guarded against disease germs as , possible. While writing may t call attention to the question of the paving of our streets. I wonder what system the Paving and Sewerage Committee adopt in deciding what streets shall be paved, and when. I admit that a great amount of street making has been done during this last year or so, but I find that many comparatively new streets are being paved, and old ones leftunpaved. I have lived in Pickmere-street for nine years, in a house which was built 13 or 14 years ago, and the street is not paved yet. In the winter months pools of !rater collect, and make the place a rerfect quagmire: an the summer months we are troubled with clouds of dust. Surely it is time the matter was attended to.— Yours truly. 21, Pickmere at., Warrington.</p>
        <p>A. TILLING, Junr.</p>
        <p>National Service League and Health. Sir,—On reading the report of the meeting held by the N.S.L. I was surprised to find that whilst the speakers decried the conditions of our workingclass, both in factory and home life, with their consequent physical degeneracy, they failed to see that economic causes cannot be effectively combated by physical improvements only. I do not deny that the N.S.L. are interested in the physical well-being of the workers, still it requires a stretch of imagination to see how military training is going to affect the poverty problem which has its roots in economic exploitation. The object of the N.S.L is to provide an Army for home defence by compulsory military training, as a means of liberating the standing Army for foreign aggressicn—"Expansion of Empire." That, I believe, , is the basis of the N.S.L. and it is with the objtct of carrying out that work that the youth of the nation is being appealed to. Now, when speaking of" National defence" the question necessarily arises " Have all an equal stake in the nation ? " I think not, and further if the conditions of millions of our countrymen are such that their life consists of drudging ttil, in return for which they receive a mere existence, what have they to defend ? And yet no one can truthfully deny that along side of this we have an absolute waste of wealth, more than sufficient to provide a healthy life for all squandered on the maintenance of a minority whose production is nil. We cannot breed a love of country upon the barren soil cf class exploitation. Whether we study human society living under a conditicn of savagery or barbarism we find that prosperity spells the well being for all within the tribe, not a few As a conse- quence of this economic equality we get a patriotism worthy of the name. To the proletariat patriotism is an empty word. If Russian Poles are used to displace the British factory worker, and Chinese the British seaman, because they are cheaper, on what Founds can we appeal to patriotism ? If commercial- ism, which is only the reflex of industrialism, has become international why draw national boundaries? It is only under mcdern civilisation, based upon industrial capitalism, that an appeal to a bastard patriotism is necessary. If military training is a way out of physical degeneracy, bow is it that our time expired soldiers and sailors, when they return to civilian life, become as degener- ate as their brothers, the factory workers ? Why have we degenerate people living in slums and working in unhealthy surroundings? Is it because we lack knowledge of h' .w to live a healthy life? Nothing of tl e kind, my friends. The limitations of the purchasing power of the worke's is tire cause of physical degeneracy, not lack of training or want of effort. Half the domestic life of our townswomen is spent in a vain attempt to combat filth thrown out of our factory chimneys. We build a factory, and scarcely has its chimney commenced to throw out poisonous fumes than the jerry builder surrounds it with workmen's dwellings as though its very existence depended upon the assimilation of its noxious gases by the people themselves. And yet we have people who believe that a month's training per year, or the removal of a few back to back houses and an cccasi nal lecture on how to keep a window box, are going to meet the difficulty. How are we going to account for the mushroom-like growth of the semi-detached villa during the last decade? Is it because the people who inhabit those desirable</p>
        <p>residences have received a military training, or is</p>
        <p>because they draw a salary which meets the financial demands of Rich conditions? Yet these people are no more important in the social production of wealth than they who inhabit the four-bob-a week slum dwelling. The only problem for the worker is the wages problem. A pound per week will always spell slum dwellings and degeneracy ; given economic opportunity the demand for better dwellings is sure to follow. Shorter hours of toil mean more time for recreation and a fuller and healthier life What ale Ave asked to defend? Our home. Whose home? The worker's home. Why? What could happen that would make our condition worse than at present ? Gt rman invasion, and then what? —still Capitalism, my friends. No, y u cannot go back to barbarism or even fuedalism ; it is not so profitable. Who make the wars? The people of any particular country ? Not a Lit of it. They might have to do the fighting. Our international gang of financiers make war over markets, and then appeal to the patriotic spirit of the workingclasses to carry out their schemes. National Service League ! Whose nation? Come again ! CHARLES DUKES. Dundonald Avenue, Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>lo , " Lc tetl4.sth T 11, . .r •'i !r illl ' llici ' ' ill ' e--'4l-15:"''' , kil EDITOR • --A1147- ___.---------4_.;-- . TICE TO COW- 'DENTS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>SYNDICALISM AND HUMAN NATURE.</head>
        <p>SYNDICALISM AND HUMAN NATURE.</p>
        <p>Many writers and speakers are warning us against the dangers of Syndicalism. We are told that a section of the workers owning and .controlling the machinery of production would be a menace to society. Owing to the selfishness of human nature there would be attempts to hold up the rest of the com- munity for higher prices and the warfare among the different groups would be worse than the present industrial strife. Let us follow the evolution of thought which has led to Syndicalism. Craft or sectional unionism was established to fight individual capitalists. As capitalist corpora- tions became bigger, craft unionism Lecame inefficient and so we have the modern move- ment towards industrial unionism. Industrial unionism means the organisation of the majority of workers into a vast federation in order that the condition of the whole working class may be raised. While this movement has been developing, the idea of working- class ownership of the means of production has been going round. To those workers who have lost faith in Parliamentary action or who think that it should be supplemented to be an efficient means to this end, the proposals of Syndicalism appeal. Syndicalism is industrial unionism carried to its logical conclusion. Unionism is to be perfected till there is such solidarity among the workers that wages will be raised to the highest limit. Profits having been thus eliminated, the machinery aproduction will be worthless to its present owners and the workers will then take possession. . .</p>
        <p>Now the^ objection to Syndicalism is not made on the moral grounds of its confiscating the machinery of production. If capitalism becomes unworkable it is said that the machinery might just as well as not be taken over by the workers and run for their own benefit. Of course, it is implied that there would be employment for the dispossessed capitalists, this being made possible by a general reduction of hours. And as all would become workers and as no real hardship had been suffered by anyone, the confiscation would have been justified. It is objected that the great danger of Syndicalism would come after the acquisition of industry by the workers. Syndicalism by giving control to particular groups of worlc,rs would place before them the temptation of holding up the rest of the community for higher prices for their work. In other words the opponents of Syndicalism say that a state of industry which had been brought about by the solidarity of the workers would become unworkable for lack of this quality. Because the railway workers had thrown in their lot with a vast federation of workers to gain the machinery it is argued that they would soon afterwards fight the rest of the federation for an undue share of the:products of industry. Let us give Syndicalists their due. Do they really intend to give power to a part to govern the whole? Although we may not agree with their methods, their ideals are much the same as those of all progressives. When they say "control of each industry by the workers of that industry" is it not possible that they mean it in the same sense that each town owns and controls its streets? To me it appears that Syndicalists intend that the workers in each industry shall be a committee or local authority work- ing and controlling that industry in accordance with a plan democratically originated by the whole federation. To think otherwise is to charge them with attempting to make the part greater than the whole. That combination of groups gives advantages and at the same time imposes obligations on all is a fundamental law that cannot be be ignored. "Central unity of plan with freedom of parts as to detail" must be the ideal of Syndicalists as of all democrats. But while the aim of Syndicalism may be attainable and worth striving for, the methods of Syndicalists are open to grave criticism. Without considering the question- able manner of obtaining possession of the machinery, let us see if possession is in itself of such importance as they imply. Democratically controlled industry such as they seek to establish will require democrati- cally trained men for its operation. That is to say there must be a considerable change of character in the individuals who form the organisation before there can be any advance on industry as at present conducted. This can be obtained only by efficient training which means a changed and powerful environment acting on the individual. In present day industry men either obey orders or issue them to others; they are not required to co-operate with their fellow-workers on equal terms. Order is maintained by external discipline. Damocratic industry or the self-government of groups implies self- restraint and initiative in the majority of the individuals composing them, and these qualities can only be generally cultivated in the atmosphere of industrial democracy itself. Syndicalists are justified in believing that human nature is capable of industrial democracy, but they err in thinking that the mere acquisition of machinery will bring it about. At the same time no other body of reformers suggest the means by which the necessary qualities, now latent in human nature, may be developed. The mechanism of human society can only express the average of the Characters of its units, and any change of form or function which does not take account of, and provide for, a higher development of individual character, can have but superficial results. Change of character, the forces making for it and the consequent fundamental changes in the structure of society, will be dealt with in a future article. _ _</p>
        <p>W. BAGULEY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKINGMEN'S ASSOCIATION (N.A. CENTRE). All fishing centres last week-end failed to supply the average number of anglers ow jog to the uncertainty of the weather. There was a poor attendance at Chester, Mickle Trafford and Acton Bridge, and the anglers who ventured out had very poor sport. S•-veral visited Chester but they returned without the flounder to weigh in, and that was what they made the journey for. This special was not won last seascn, and up to the present there is a clean sheet this year. Those who visited the Gowy had very poor sport. One or two eels were taken, but none in great quanti- ties, and not a single trout was taken. Pike were not to be had from the Shaker Bridge to Plinston's Pool. We heard of one member having a run but that was all. There is no doubt t' at more anglers are going in for this class of fishing than ever during the close season. Acton Bridge was again visited, and results from here were most disappointing, several members spend- ing all week-end without taking an eel. Considering the amount taken other seasons and the weight, there must be a great falling off of eels in this whtar. But then there has not teen a good bag of eels taken this season in comparison with the previous season. Let us hope that the recent rain will put them on the move more. It will certainly freshen the different streams and put colour into them. Many members were wait- ing for the rain before again turning cut. Up to the time of writing we have not heard of any- one fishing Long Barn last week end, but there are several shop men, who close on Thursday afternoon, going to take advantage of the short journey. The club meeting is on Monday next at eight o'clock. We are still enrolling a new member now and then. Will the old members please call for their cards as early as possible as it will then give the officials a better chance to work matters into shipshape before the opening season arrives. A.S.</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 11, 1912.</p>
        <p>AFTER THE FIRE AT CARTONS.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON ra:d he was astonished to hear Coincillor Bellian's remarks. Of course,his experi- e ice entitled him to speak f anything connected with motors, but why mot r engines should be good for out- lying districts end not fc r Warrington he (Councillor 'Pemberton) could not understand. He admitted that sometimes the horses were not on the scar a as quickly as they ought to be; it was not as if they had their own horses waiting at the fire station, like Manchester and Liverpool. He had known fr )ni twelve t 3 fi'teen mina s elapse I efor3 the horses got there. (" No.") 'You may sty 'No'" continued Councillor Pemberton "hut I am telling you what I have seen myself, and I believe my oan eyes before what anybody else tells me." Ten minutes might mean a matter of life or de ,th, or of £l,OOO or £'4,0,000 damage in case of fire. Promptitude of arrival was everything. They could not afford to look entirely at the question of economy in a m Ater like fire prevention. Councillor BELLIAN : You cannot sive one moment's time by the use of any motor fire engine in Wargiog- ton, provided you keep your hors .s on the premises. You have always to wait until the men get to the fire station, and nine times out of ten the horsas are there as soon as the men. The station is within five minutes reach of every part of the borough. Cmocillor ASHCROFT said thit, comparing Warring- ton with districts of similar population, they had the finest amateur brigade in the country. He had seen it turn out in two or three minute.' but had never known it to take as long as Councillor Pemberton had stated. They could not afford to keep horses on the premises. Councillor HAWTHORN said he was told it took the ' brigade over ten minutes to get to Garton's fire. A gentleman stsnding on Warrington Bridge saw the smoke issue Irom the roof, a policeman go to the tele- phone to give the alarm, and the brigade arrive on the scene, and his complaint about the brigade was that they were s'ow in turning out, for an important town like Warriugton. What was desired was not so much the provision of a motor engine as to keep the horses on the remises. Much veluable time might be lost in going to Connor's stable for the horses. Councillor SKELLAND said he had seen the horses turn out within three minutes. Councillor Wasoti said he was at the fire station when the bell rang and the first engine turned out in about six minutss and was on the ground in eight minutes. The second engine was ten minutes in turn- ing out. Councilh.r HAWTHORN : Eight minutes is very valuable time. . . .</p>
        <p>Alderman BURTON said he hoped to make some state- ment on the question at the next Council meeting. It would be a great help to the brigade, if manufacturers would see that an ample supply of water was available when they got on the scene. The minutes were approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>AMID 11:1E Mr. F. Tin-nu., Deputy Fire Supei iutendent, in the foregrcund.</head>
        <p>AMID 11:1E Mr. F. Tin-nu., Deputy Fire Supei iutendent, in the foregrcund.</p>
        <p>Council and the Fire Brigades Equipment. MOTOR ENGINE SUGGESTED.</p>
        <p>The recent disastrous tire at Garton's warehouse g tve rise to a discuss'oa on the equipment of the Corpora- tion Fire Brigade at the meeting of the Town Council on Tuesday evening. Aldermaa BURTON, who moved the adoption of the minutes of the Fire Engine Committee, stid the huge dimensions of the fire put it beyond the power of any fire engines to sul dua or extinguish the flames. He felt certain that every member of the brigade did the best he could. :Many of the men were exposed to danger and s3me of them had miraculous escapes. Counclior ASHCROFT seconded.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT said the brigade acted bravely and did their duty as well as possible under extremely trying and hazardous circumstances. The doubt was as to whether their equipment was absolutely and entirely up-to-date. In giving expression to that opinion, ha was speaking not only for himself tut for an important body which represented practically all the leading manufacturers of the town—the Chamber of Commerce. They had the matter under consideration at their last Council meeting, which was largely attended, and asked him respectfully to suggest to the committee the desirability of having a motor engine ; in view of the size of the town and the great risks incurred by some of the larger works. They would also like to know whether all the minor details of equipment were up-to-date. Warrington was a town of such inportance and the industries represented were so large and influential, that the Council ought to do ever. thing pDssible to prevent the recurrence of such a catastrophe or to minimise the effects t) the utmost extent.</p>
        <p>Councillor BELLIAN s.id that if the Chamber of Com- merce wished to press forward the purchase of a motor engine they had not the slightest idea of what it would cost the town. To start with, it was impossible to have anything of that description without_ a duplicate. But even if they had had fifty motor engites they could not have stopped Carton's fire. The Corporation brigade proved itself quite equal ti-+ Crosfields, which was particularly a professional brigade. How on earth they could have saved any of the building by motor engines was beyond his power to conceive. The Committee had seriously thought of the suegestion but practically dropped the idea. Motor engines might be necessary for large cities or places with large outlying districts, but for a comparatively small place like Warrington,u here access to all parts was so easy,the Council ought to be satisfied with the ordinary fire engines.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>RATEPAYERS' BURDEN.</head>
        <p>THE RATEPAYERS' BURDEN.</p>
        <p>Town Council's Alarm at Constant Increase.</p>
        <p>APPEAL FOR IMPERIAL AID.</p>
        <p>For a long time the feeling has been gaining ground' that the ratepayers are entitled to substantial help ft om Imperial sources to meet the ever-growing burden imposed upon them by the administration of national concerns like education. As far as Warringthn is concerned, this opinion tcok prelatical shape on Tuesday evening when, at the meeting of the Town Council, Ccuccillor Pemberton introduced the follow- bag resolution (a) That this Council views with alarm the grow- ing increase in the contriburion to be borne by the ratepayers towards the cost of education without adequate provish n being made by the Government for an equitable contribution from the Imperial Exchequer. (b) That a copy of this rePolu ion be sent to the Prime Minister and the P.esidents of the Local Government Board and Board of Education with a request that the contributions from Imperial and local funds be immediately considered with a view to a just apportionment being made. (c) That a copy of this tesolion be fora arded to other Local Authorities with a request to them to pass a similar resolution. He pointed out that in 1905 the Imp, rial exchequer contributed 127,142 to Warringtou's expenditure on education and the ratepayers were called upon to find £10,408 This year the Government contribution was estimated at £29,671, whereas the ratepayers' share of the burden had risen to nearly £25,000 —one and a half times as much as in 1905 The steady increase of the expenditure and the inequitable propor- tions borne from imperial and local sources respec- tively were mo-t alarming. If tFe tendency were to continue, be did not know what the ratepayers were going to do. Education was a purely national concern and the Board of Education could dictate to the local authorities as to how the money should be sprit. Moreover, he did not believe the ratepayers were getting half the value for the money expendtd. When they considered that they had only 300 more childten in the r•chocls to-day than in 1904 due to the exclusion of scholars under five years of. age—the increase of expenditure was terrific A bold stand ought to be made by local authorities and the resAutioa, which had been drafted with a view to commending itself to the Ccuhcils throughout the country, was weak and mad a quate to express what he felt. Ccunciltr r ROBERTS seconded. His principal reason for doing so was that, like a great many others, he believed that the question of education, being a national question ought to be paid for out of the national purse. Under present conditions, a large proportion of the tetter.placed classes of the community escaped their obligations a rider that head Dy the proper administration of educational matters, the nation as a whole ought to pay. It wa-, however, only fair to point out that education authorities bad an opportunity a year or two ago of protAating against an alteration in the conditions—which protest, if successfully carried out, would have prevented a considerable increase in the cost of education. That was when the Board of Education increased the cubic space per child from 8 to Wt., thus reducing the school accommodation available and necessitating the erection of new schools. Alderman BENNETT, in supporting, said he was glad that Warrington would give the lead to ot'aer enlightened municipalities cn that question. If they could get the cordial support of other authorities, there was reason to hope that Parliament would take action. Parliament never seemed to move becauseit was right and wise to do so, but only when it was kicked. (Laughter.) The proper policy was for all public-spirited com- munities to apply the necessary stimulus. What was really wanted was ttat the whole question of the relations bt tween local and imperial expenditure should be carefully considered and an equitable readjustment arrived at.</p>
        <p>-•• Councillor JENKINS siid there was a strong and growing opinion throughout the country that the local communities were very unjusly trusted by the Imperial Exchequer. He bad every reason to think that the resolution would receive very sympathetic consideration from other authorities. Councillor HAWTHORN pointed out that the question of tke inequality of rating had been brought before the Chanexilor of the Exchequer egain and again and he had promised to look into it. They hoped that by constant hammering they would be able to accelerate the pace. Councillor HENSHALL said the ratio of expenditure as against the local ratepayer was constantly nsing. Local authorities were in revolt and what was wanted was not so much the sending of resolutions which, after all, were only pious opinions. The kingdom of earth, like the kingdom of heaven, suffereth violence, and the violent, the determined and the strenuous took it by force. If all the Councils which felt as strongly endas \l'nrri persistentng t o n attacko th on the Government authorities,e question could present a united they might hope for early success. The resolution was carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>WORKHOUSE MASTER AND MATRON.</head>
        <p>WORKHOUSE MASTER AND MATRON.</p>
        <p>The New Appointments. Mr. and Mrs. W. T. Brown, master and matron of the Blything Union Workhouse, Suffolk, were on Wednesday appointed master and matron of the Warrington Union Workhouse in succession to Mr. and Mrs. J. F. Awbery who have retired. There were fifty-six applications for the joint appointment and a sub-committee visited eight Workhouses in different parts of the coultry in order to select candidates to appear before the Board. The three couples finally selected were :—Mr and Mrs W T Brovvn, master and matron of 813 thing Union Workhouse, Suffolk; Mr and M.B NV Loxbarn,master and matron of HaLlingden Union Workhouse ; Mr. and Mrs. Child, master and matron of Exeter Union Workhouse. Mr. and Mrs. Child found at the last moment that they were unable to make the journey north on Wednesday. After interviewing the other selected candidates, the choice of the Board fell upon Mr. and Mrs. Brown, who were unanimously appointed The commencing salary is £ll5 for the master and £6O for the matron, with the usual residential allowances The emoluments are valued at £65 per annum in each case. In thanking the Board, Mr. BROWN said be sincerely hoped his wife at.d be would have good health and strength to enable them to carry out the duties to the entire satisfaction of the Board. (Applause). Mr. Brown began his connection with the Poor Law as assistant master at Middlesborough. His subse- quent appointments have been : Assistant master at Greenwich ; Infitmary steward at tereenwich ; master at Petersfield. He has held the mastership of Blything Union for four-and-a-half years. Mrs. Brown's previous appointments have been : Staff nurse at St Luke's Infirmary, London; charge nurse at Greenwich ; matron at Petersfield.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>WONDERFUL ZAM-BUK RECORD. Bad Legs, Scalded Foot, Poisoned Sore, Cuts and Bruises, all quite Healed.</head>
        <p>A WONDERFUL ZAM-BUK RECORD. Bad Legs, Scalded Foot, Poisoned Sore, Cuts and Bruises, all quite Healed.</p>
        <p>Zam-Buk has scored a series of wonderful triumphs in the home of Mr. and Mrs. S. C. Madden, C, Green- street, Huddersfield, Father, mother, and practically all their seven children—Lilian (15), Doris (14), Stephanie (12), John Edward (10), Reginald (7), Lemard (5), and Mona (I)—have derived marked bereft from this wonderful healer. Mr. Madden, an iron-turner, chatting with a pressman, said : "I knocked the fleshy part of my leg against the edge of a valve-casting three years ago. The skin was torn, and in a few days the raw flesh started festering. An ulcer formed, while the flesh all round got red-raw with ialaranuation. The pain was simply terrible. "The ulcer spread and ate deeply into the flesh. I got so lame that 1 had to stay at home. I thought I should never walk again, and my mates thought so, too. For two months the doctor visited me regularly, but his treatment didn't bring any improvement. "Then I started with Zana Buk and it is entirely to this rare balm that I owe my wonderful recovery. Zam-Bul eared my pain splendidly, taking away all the b:ting and smarting sensation. It reduced the Lflammation and swelling, to), and all the corruption was gradually ell awn from my leg. The big, deg) ulcer then filled up with healthy flesh, over which Zam-Buk grew new skin. I returned to work with my leg sound and healthy again. Mrs. Madden then told the reporter that a sore suddenly appeared on her left leg. " A chemist said it would be a life-long trouble. I was quite frightened, particularly as the ulcer got rapidly bigger and more inflamed and rainful. The skin turned quite black, too. My husband persuaded me to try Zam-Buk, and instead of having my leg bad all my life, the ulcer was soothed, cleansed, and healed in a very short time. " Leonard (5) was sitting near the fire waiting for his bath when some stew boiled over on to his foct. rhe scalded parts were smeared with Zam-Buk, which soon soothed the child's pain and covered the raw flesh with nice cool skin.</p>
        <p>"The day Doris (14) btatted work, a brass eyelet from her shoe worked through her stocking and scratched a nasty sore on her foot. Doris was in great pain and we were afraid of blood-poison. Zam- Buk, however, cleanse:: the place and healed it beautifully. "The children are alway- knocking, bruising, or cutting themselves, 'tad I don't know what I should do without Zam-Buk, which I always keep ready on a handy shelf."</p>
        <p>, Mr. McKenna, the Home Secretary, speaking on Wednesday at the annual dinner of the Newspaper Society, said it had become a growing fashion for heavy damages to be given against newspapers in libel actions. He suggested that the press should undertake a campaign to instruct the public in the unfairness of juries awarding vindictive damages. The true vindica- tion was an apology and compensation for actual loss suffered. Vindictive damages were absolutely wrong in policy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>CYCLIST'S NOTE-BOOK.</head>
        <p>THE CYCLIST'S NOTE-BOOK.</p>
        <p>[By " Silver Street."] Now that we have got Easter well over (and I say " well " advisedly, for it is many a long year since cyclists bad so blank an Easter holiday), with its gales, we may look forward to calmer times at Whit- week. Whatever may be the disadvantages of living at Warrington, the cyclist is favoured by many natural features, the chief being his nearness to our beloved Wales. When one reflects that a 20 miles ride will place him on the borders of the gallant principality, and that he lives at the gate, as Warrington Bridge may well be called for almost all Lancastrians, one's smpathies may be spared for his unfortunate brethren who have counties to cross before they reach the b autiful mountains and woods and waterfalls that figure in so many photographs. As two of the three routes to North Wales are so well known, I propose to take you by a road as much superior in beauty and grandeur of scenery as it is in height above the sea (1,700 feet). And first get Sheets 42 and 43 of the Ordnance Survey, half inch scale. You can get them at Pearse's dissected ar.d mcunted for the pocket at 2s. each, or on paper at Is. to mount for yourself. I will describe my method of mounting them next n eek, and I can recommend the economical system as well for its saving as for its instructive value, and no one can fail altogether even at the first attempt. I, for my pat t, have alit as used Bartholomew's half-inch series and their sheets No. 11, 12 and RI will act almost as well. Pearse keeps them also, if they will take the trouble, order them rclled, not creased.</p>
        <p>The object first is to get to Osvvestry, and it is immaterial whether you take the south road through Tarporley, Whitchurch and Ellesmere, or the western road via Chester, Wrexham and Chirk. I prefer the former, as the ride through Ellesmere and onwards is so fine. Then the castle at Whittington is well worth a snapshot. From Oswestry we take the south road t Llynclys (pron. lunclus, with the accent on iun), and- to the right up to the charming Tanat valley. You cannot do better than arrange your second night's stay at Llanrhaiadr-yn-Mcchnant, to give it its full proud title, for the country ahead is very poor in rest houses, and at Llanrhaiadr is a very good and reason- able one. This will also give a chance of visiting the highest waterfall ,in the whole of these islands. The Pistyll Rhaiadr is four miles up the valley from the inn, and if you dabble (at all in botany you will be delighted with the number of new plants that these high valleys will show you. Violas as big as a halfpenny, new spurges, and rock plants and marsh plants too, that the Cheshire plain cannot grow. The Gossiping Guide to Wales gives the height of the fall as 309 ft., which is just short of twice the height of Niagara. I may say I have seen this statement of the , height of Pistyll Rhaiadr disputed, but it must be far above 200 ft. high and in its three leaps gives one an impression of the grandeur of falling water that can not be equalled in Britain, especially if the stream is pretty full. On leaving Llanrbaiadr next morning make for Penyboatfawr station, and up the Cwm Hirnant. All the way up the valley you will see the telephone wires in connection with the Liverpool aqueduct from the great lake just on the other side of the hill. Presently 1 you descend the Marchnant valley to the great dam and on the top of the hill to the right is the Vyrnwy hotel. Two of us once valiantly pushed forward to that hotel for the night, and were charged 14s. for supper and breakfast and a bed! But the carpets are all Axmiosters, there are twelve tables in the dining room, and everything is fitted up to match. We were fortunate, for if we had arrived a day later, we should have had to ride on ten or more miles, as the Lot d Mayor and Water Committee had booked every room for the next two days. They were coming to inspect the taps, I guess. You ought, however, to go up to the hotel just to see the view of the lake from the terrace. And now you have before you the very FINEST CYCLE RIDE IN ALL WILES.</p>
        <p>The first five miles are along the west side of lake Vyrnwy,having on your left fields containing thousands of the lovely butterfly orchid, alternating with groves of young pines that the Liverpool Corporation are wowing as part of their afforestation scheme. Then a walk up the Cwm Eunant, and two miles good riding across the moor, followed by a magnificent coast down the Bwlch a Groes (the Pass of the Cross.) After seven miles the free-wheeling ceases at ,Dinas Mawddwy (Din-as Mowthooy) the ancient capital of the district. Excellent accommodation can be had here, but if you have had good luck, you can reach Dolgelley for the night and put up at the Criterion Hotel (unlicensed) Mrs. Davis. Before this fine old town is reached there is another climb over the BwlchOerdrsvys (The Wintry Gate Pass) another pass not so fine at the one we have just come down, but equal to any in the Lake District and a good road to boot. At the Cross Foxes Inn, three miles from Dolgelley a descent commences that takes the cream off the day's pleasure. Some one has found very rich iron ore up there and by means of a traction engine and trailer waggons has converted a formerly model road into a quagmire, with which the authorities struggle in vain. ,So, until the side road to Dclgelley Station is reached you must be contemt to walk, unless something wonderful has happened since I was there. Thus we have reached Dolgelley by a route not one cyclist in a thousand knows of, and one that will never be crowded, you can bet your bottom dollar on that.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>*"Silver Street" will be pleased to answer queries from correspondents each week in this column. • WARRINGTON CYCLING CLUB.</head>
        <p>*"Silver Street" will be pleased to answer queries from correspondents each week in this column. • WARRINGTON CYCLING CLUB.</p>
        <p>Despite the bad weather conditions rrevailing throughout the afternoon a mall number of the enthusiastic members carried out the run down to Acton Bridge on Saturday last. The captain's enthusiasm with his new duties must hive bean some- what damped by clouds that broke out while he awaited I his followers at headquirters about the tints of departure. A brace of the stalwarts set out, however, but report does not say whether they chalked the board before leaving. Perhaps in the hope of some ardent spirit by chance afterwards passing that way they may have left the direction taken, writtea on the wall. Muddy roads and rain do not enhance the pleasure of cycling and it must have beeni with mixed feelings that "Black" and "Tan" ploughed their way over the ten miles journey out, and one can imagine the captain remarking on his bad lu-3k while climbing the Stratton pimple. Upon arrival the pedallers wore j3ined by members who had covered the distance by other means, when the gathering then took on home pretensions to a club run. Bo ,cling was not available and during the after- noon the party walked to Dutton Locks where the interesting operations of the campers who were setting ,up their summer props were v.ewed, a return after- wards being made to the Railway Hotel where an excellent tea was waiting. . _</p>
        <p>- With the Wednesday evening bowling meetings,as the days lengthen the attendance grows larger and the number of members out at Sankey on Wednesday wis well in excess of that of the prev:oas meeting. The recent rain has had a wonderful effect on the Sankey green, and while the woods took some little pushing the pecularities of certain familiar marks were quite obvious. Interest in the games,wtiih wereall foursomes, was keen and some really good ends were noticed. Play was kept going until late and a number of white jacks would have proved useful. The club's arrangements for the c ming week are : To-day's run to Bollington, where the afternoon will be spent on the green. On Wednesisy the b •wiing meet- ing with the Warrington Bowling Club at Sankey Chipel House, and Thursday evening's ran to Winwiok</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-05-11_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>N.H. VENTURE IN DEVON.</head>
        <p>THE N.H. VENTURE IN DEVON.</p>
        <p>--- The exhibition match to be played by Oldham and Huddersfield, at Plymouth, to-day, is exciting consider- able interest, not only in the locality but throughout tt?e three ;westernmost shires generally, for serious issues hang on the question of the impression the game makes on the public mind. For this reason adherents of the old Rugby Cuien game rezard the event with some amount of misgiving. The promoters of the new departure have been busily engaged in tapping other towns, with the result that delegates will be present from many of the more important districts in Devon, Cornwall, Somerset, and even further afield, and it is understood that if the majority are pleased with the display, and find that it hits the popular fancy they will immediately join in conference with members of the Northern Union Com- mittee with a view to the formation of a Western Branch of the Union.</p>
        <p>LIVERPOOL AND NORTH WALES STEAMBOAT SERVICE. From the advertising columns it will be seen that the Liverpool and North Wales Steamship Company are announcing the sailings of their palatial steamer La Marguerite for the Whitsuntide holidays. Commencing Saturday, May 18th, the favourite saloon steamer St. Elvies will take up the daily railings until Whitsuntide at 10.45 a.m. for Llandudno, Beaumaris, Bangor and Menai Bridge, due back at 7.30 p.m. La Marguerite will take up the Whitsuntide sailing; commencing Saturday, May 25th, and continue daily during Whitsuntide holidays. There will also he extra sail- ings at 2.15 p.m. on Saturdays, commencing May 25th, by St. Elvies or other steamer. We understand that last season was a record for season tickets issued, and there is no doubt the demand will increase especially when it is taken into account the variety of contracts issued to meet the wishes of the travelling public.</p>
      </div>
